/* Chapter 10 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":10,"title":"Forward Sales: Salam and Istisna‘a","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[241,278],"minutes":90,"estimates":{"f1":90,"f2":80},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch10-1","text":"Explain why Salam and Istisna‘a are exceptions to the rule against selling what does not exist or is not owned.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-2","text":"Describe the economic role and benefits of Salam.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-3","text":"State the conditions of a valid Salam: subject matter, price (Salam capital), delivery, options, amendment and penalty.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-4","text":"Explain why Salam in currencies is invalid.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-5","text":"Explain methods of disposing of Salam goods (Parallel Salam, agency, promise) and post-execution scenarios.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-6","text":"Describe Salam Sukuk and the risks and mitigation in bank Salam (Box 10.3).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-7","text":"Define Istisna‘a and state rules on subject matter, price, penalty (Shart-e-Jazai), binding nature, Parallel Istisna‘a and agency.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-8","text":"Compare Istisna‘a with Salam and with Ijarah/Ujrah (Box 10.10) and apply case studies.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-9","text":"Explain why Salam and Istisna'a are treated as exceptions to the general Shari'ah conditions of a valid sale, and how each avoids Gharar despite deferred delivery.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-10","text":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract: eligible subject matter, capital/price rules, delivery terms, and the treatment of Khiyar.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-11","text":"Walk through how a bank uses Salam as a financing technique, including Parallel Salam and other disposal alternatives, and identify the risks involved.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-12","text":"Explain why Salam in currencies is generally disallowed, and the reasoning behind this restriction.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-13","text":"Define Istisna'a, distinguish it from Salam and from Ijarah/Ujrah, and explain its price, penalty (Shart-e-Jazai), and binding-nature rules.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-14","text":"Describe how Parallel Istisna'a and agency arrangements let banks act as both manufacturer/seller and purchaser.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch10-15","text":"Apply Salam- and Istisna'a-based financing structures to realistic scenarios (agriculture, export preshipment finance, construction, government infrastructure).","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Salam and Istisna‘a are the Shari’ah-approved forward contracts — the only lawful way to sell goods that do not yet exist. They underpin agricultural, working-capital, export and infrastructure finance, and help you see why conventional futures and options fail Shari’ah tests.","whyExpanded":"Salam and Istisna'a are the two Shari'ah-sanctioned exceptions that let Islamic finance support forward-delivery transactions - Salam financing agriculture and commodities, Istisna'a financing manufacturing, construction and infrastructure. Together they fill financing gaps Murabaha and Ijarah cannot reach (pre-harvest farmer finance, long-gestation construction projects) and underpin Salam- and Istisna'a-based Sukuk. Understanding their conditions and risk points is essential for structuring real project and trade finance deals.","overview":"The chapter treats Salam — its basis, benefits, conditions, security, disposal of goods, post-execution scenarios, securitisation, rule summary and use by banks with risk mitigation and case studies — and then Istisna‘a: definition, subject matter, price, penalty clause, binding nature, guarantees, parallel contracts, agency, post-execution, potential, risk management, comparisons and case studies in housing, export and project finance.","summary":"Salam and Istisna‘a are the permitted forward sales — exceptions to the rule that goods sold must exist and be owned and possessed, made free of Gharar by conditions. In Salam the full price is paid at contract for fungible, precisely specified goods normally available at a fixed delivery time and place; it cannot be in currencies, specific items or debts; it is binding; late-delivery penalties go to charity; goods may be disposed of through independent Parallel Salam, a third-party promise or agency (never back to the seller). Banks under-use Salam but it suits agriculture, SMEs, exports and working capital, with defined risk mitigation. Istisna‘a is an order to manufacture or construct, with material supplied by the manufacturer, price payable in instalments, binding once work starts, allowing a price reduction for late delivery (Shart-e-Jazai), and usable through Parallel Istisna‘a for housing, exports, buildings and infrastructure. (Chapter synthesis — the book has no separate summary section; Section 10.9 summarises Salam rules.)","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 10 covers the two Shari'ah-sanctioned exceptions to the rule against selling nonexistent goods: Salam (full advance payment for deferred delivery of fungible commodities, directly sanctioned by the Prophet pbuh, historically serving farmers) and Istisna'a (manufacture-to-order sale, legalized via Istihsan, serving construction and industrial financing). Salam requires strict conditions - fungible subject matter, full advance payment, fixed delivery terms, no Khiyar al-Shart - and disposal of Salam goods before delivery relies on the more permissive scholarly view enabling Parallel Salam and Salam Sukuk, always excluding the original seller to avoid buy-back. Istisna'a is more flexible - no mandatory advance payment, instalment pricing, a purchaser-benefiting Shart-e-Jazai late-delivery penalty - but is only binding once the manufacturer starts work. Both modes carry real operational risks that the chapter catalogs alongside their mitigants, and both remain underused relative to Murabaha despite significant financing potential in agriculture, trade, construction, and infrastructure.","summarySection":null,"takeaways":["Salam: full prepayment; fungible, specified, available goods; fixed time/place.","No Salam in currency, specific items, land or subjectively valued items.","Salam binding; rescission only by consent at the exact price.","Salam goods disposed via Parallel Salam, promise or agency — never to the seller.","Late delivery: Salam penalty to charity; Istisna‘a price reduction to buyer.","Istisna‘a: manufactured items; instalments allowed; binding once work starts.","Parallel Istisna‘a: independent, third party, delivery date not earlier.","Know Box 10.2 (Salam vs Murabaha) and Box 10.10 (Istisna‘a vs Salam vs Ujrah).","Salam and Istisna'a are the two exceptions to the general rule that a sold commodity must exist and be possessed by the seller - each justified by specific conditions that neutralize Gharar.","Salam requires full advance payment for deferred delivery of a precisely-specified, fungible (Mithli) commodity; Salam in currencies/gold/silver is generally disallowed because it would constitute Bai' al Sarf requiring simultaneous exchange.","Khiyar al-Shart and Khiyar al Ro'yat are disallowed in Salam; only Khiyar al 'Aib (post-delivery defect option) is available, and the contract is binding except for genuine market-wide unavailability.","Parallel Salam lets a bank resell purchased Salam goods before taking delivery (under the more permissive scholarly view), but can never be structured with the original seller - that would be prohibited Bai' al 'Inah.","Istisna'a does not require advance payment (price can be paid in instalments tied to completion) and is nonbinding until the manufacturer starts work - a fundamentally different risk profile from Salam.","The Shart-e-Jazai penalty clause in Istisna'a reduces price for late delivery and directly benefits the purchaser's income; Salam's late-delivery penalty, by contrast, must go to charity.","Banks can act as both seller and buyer through Parallel Istisna'a (subcontracting to a genuine third party) and through agency arrangements for construction supervision or asset resale, with each contract legally independent.","Both Salam and Istisna'a remain underused by Islamic banks relative to Murabaha, largely for operational reasons (upfront cash requirements, physical goods handling) rather than any Shari'ah defect - both retain significant untapped financing potential."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you state the Hadith basis and conditions of Salam?","Can you explain why Salam in currencies is invalid?","Can you explain the rules on Salam capital?","Can you list disposal options and the Parallel Salam rules?","Can you handle failure and inferior supply scenarios?","Can you list Salam risks and Box 10.3 mitigations?","Can you explain Istisna‘a’s price, penalty and binding rules?","Can you reproduce Box 10.10 and walk through Boxes 10.13–10.16?"],"topics":[{"id":"t10.1","section":"10.1","title":"Introduction: Forward Sales as Exceptions","pages":[241,241],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam","istisna"],"intuition":"Normally you cannot sell what doesn’t exist or what you don’t own. Why do Salam and Istisna‘a get an exception?","simpleExplanation":"A valid sale needs an existing commodity, owned and possessed by the seller. Salam and Istisna‘a are exceptions because conditions are imposed that remove Gharar. As delivery is deferred, they are forward sales. Islamic commodity forward markets can exist under their rules; modern futures, options, derivatives and swaps do not qualify.","academicExplanation":["The three basic conditions of sale — existence, ownership (and its risks), and physical or constructive possession — are imposed to avoid Gharar and dispute. Salam and Istisna‘a are exceptions because conditions are prescribed that free them from Gharar. Because delivery is deferred, they are forward sales; commodity markets for future delivery can exist under their rules, whereas modern futures markets in options, derivatives and swaps do not qualify."],"examExplanation":"Salam and Istisna‘a: exceptions to existence/ownership/possession, with conditions removing Gharar; forward sales; conventional futures/derivatives don’t qualify.","keyPoints":["Three basic sale conditions; two exceptions.","Three basic sale conditions — existence, ownership, possession — are relaxed only for Salam and Istisna."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Which forward deals qualify?","kind":"practice","text":"(1) A mill prepays in full for 500 tons of specified cotton due in March: Salam, valid. (2) A builder is ordered to construct a warehouse to specification, paid in stages: Istisna‘a, valid. (3) Two traders agree today on a wheat price for March, with nothing paid and the deal settled by price difference: a conventional future, not valid (p. 241)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Salam and Istisna are forms of conventional futures contracts.","right":"They are exceptions to the rule against selling what does not exist, allowed because their conditions remove Gharar; options, swaps and modern futures do not qualify (p. 241)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.5","t8.8.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why are Salam and Istisna‘a permitted despite involving goods not yet existing?","options":["Because they involve interest","Because conditions are prescribed that free them from Gharar","Because they are gifts","Because regulators allow it"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 241."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.1","sectionTitle":"Introduction","title":"Forward Sales as an Exception to the General Conditions of Sale","covers":["t10.1"],"overview":"Explains why Salam and Istisna'a are permitted as exceptions to the normal rule that a sold commodity must exist and be in the seller's possession.","simpleExplanation":"Normally, Islamic law requires that whatever you sell must already exist and belong to you. Salam and Istisna'a are two special exceptions to that rule - they let you sell something that doesn't exist yet, as long as you follow extra conditions designed to remove the uncertainty (Gharar) that would otherwise make such a sale risky and unfair.","academicExplanation":"The general conditions for a valid sale (detailed in Chapters 5-6) require: the commodity must exist; the seller must have acquired ownership (and its associated risk); and the commodity must be in the seller's physical or constructive possession. These rules exist to prevent Gharar and disputes over subject matter. Salam and Istisna'a are the two recognized exceptions, permitted because each carries its own additional conditions that neutralize the Gharar that would otherwise result from selling a nonexistent commodity. Because delivery in both is deferred to the future, they are termed 'forward sales.' Islamic commodity markets can therefore exist for future delivery of goods under Salam/Istisna'a rules, but modern conventional futures markets (options, derivatives, swaps) do not qualify under these Shari'ah-compliant forward-sale rules.","examExplanation":"Normal sale requires: (1) commodity exists, (2) seller owns it (with risk), (3) seller has physical/constructive possession. Salam and Istisna'a are the two exceptions to these rules, each justified by specific conditions that remove Gharar. Both are 'forward sales' (deferred delivery); conventional futures/options/swaps do NOT qualify as Shari'ah-compliant forward sales.","keyPoints":["The three general conditions a sale normally requires: existence of the commodity, seller's ownership/risk, and seller's possession.","Salam and Istisna'a are the two Shari'ah-recognized exceptions permitting sale of a not-yet-existing commodity, each governed by specific conditions that neutralize Gharar.","Both are termed 'forward sales' because delivery is deferred to a future date.","Conventional futures markets (options, derivatives, swaps) do not satisfy Salam/Istisna'a rules and are not equivalent Shari'ah-compliant instruments."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A commodity sold must normally exist, be owned (with risk) by the seller, and be in the seller's physical or constructive possession - except under the specific conditions of Salam and Istisna'a."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume any forward-delivery arrangement is automatically a valid Salam or Istisna'a. In fact, each carries its own strict set of conditions (covered later in the chapter); failing them reintroduces impermissible Gharar."],"distinctions":["Islamic forward sales (Salam, Istisna'a - real delivery required, specific conditions) vs conventional futures/derivatives (no delivery requirement, not Shari'ah-compliant)."],"relatedConcepts":["Gharar","Bai' Mu'ajjal","Murabaha"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.1","pages":[241]}}]},{"id":"t10.2","section":"10.2","title":"Bai‘ Salam/Salaf: Definition and Legitimacy","pages":[241,242],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam"],"intuition":"What is the one Islamic forward contract expressly allowed by the Prophet (pbuh)?","simpleExplanation":"Salam: the full price is paid in advance at contract for specified goods delivered later — the reverse of Bai‘ Mu’ajjal. Salaf (Hijaz term) and Salam (Iraq term) are interchangeable. In Madinah people prepaid for dates for one to three years without specifications; the Prophet required “known quality, specified measure and weight … and time of delivery”. Jurists later extended it to all precisely determinable commodities.","academicExplanation":["Bai‘ Salam is an ancient forward contract: price paid in advance for prescribed goods to be delivered later at a stipulated time with specified quantity and quality — contrary to Bai‘ Mu’ajjal (goods now, price later). Salaf/Taslif (advance payment) was used by Hijazi jurists, Salam by Baghdad jurists; since the commodity becomes a debt on the seller, it implies a loan without benefit. Salam was permitted by the Prophet (pbuh) without difference of opinion, notwithstanding the rule against selling what one does not possess: in Madinah people paid in advance for fruit for one to three years without specifying quality, measure or time; he ordained: “Whoever pays money in advance should pay it for a known quality, specified measure and weight … along with the price and time of delivery.” Hasanuz Zaman: rationale is necessity; the Madinan list covered wheat, barley, dates, grapes; later olives and raisins; jurists expanded it to all commodities precisely determinable in quality and quantity."],"examExplanation":"Salam: full advance price, deferred delivery of specified goods; opposite of Mu’ajjal. Hadith: known quality, measure, weight, time. Permitted by consensus; rationale: necessity.","keyPoints":["Salaf = Hijazi term; Salam = Iraqi term.","The goods are a debt on the seller.","Hijaz jurists said Salaf, Iraqi jurists Salam; the deferred goods are a debt on the seller.","Madinan list: wheat, barley, dates, grapes; the conquest of Syria added olives and raisins."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai‘ Salam","definition":"A forward sale in which the full price is paid in advance at the time of contract for specified goods to be delivered at a stipulated future time."}],"examples":[{"title":"The Madinan practice corrected","kind":"textbook","text":"People in Madinah paid in advance for dates due in one to three years without fixing quality or measure. The Prophet (pbuh) required a known quality, specified measure and weight, and a fixed delivery time — the conditions that turn such a deal into valid Salam (pp. 241–242)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Salam and Bai‘ Mu’ajjal are the same credit sale.","right":"In Salam the price is paid now and goods come later; in Mu’ajjal the goods are delivered now and the price is paid later (p. 241)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.4.1","t9.6.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What did the Prophet (pbuh) require when people paid in advance for fruit?","options":["Nothing","Known quality, specified measure and weight, price and time of delivery","Delivery within a week","Payment in gold only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 242."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.2","sectionTitle":"Bai' Salam/Salaf","title":"Bai' Salam/Salaf: Origins and Prophetic Permission","covers":["t10.2"],"overview":"Traces Salam's origins as an ancient forward-sale contract sanctioned directly by the Prophet (pbuh), and the terminology distinction between Salam and Salaf.","simpleExplanation":"Salam is an old form of forward sale where the buyer pays the full price up front for goods to be delivered later, at an agreed quality, quantity and date. It's the opposite of typical credit sale, where you get the goods now and pay later. The Prophet (pbuh) permitted this practice directly, but insisted that the goods' quality, measure/weight, and delivery time all be clearly specified to avoid disputes.","academicExplanation":"Bai' Salam is an ancient forward contract where the price is paid in advance at contract formation, for prescribed goods to be delivered later. 'Salam' and 'Salaf' were used interchangeably in Hadith literature; Hijaz jurists favored 'Salaf' (literally, payment in advance), while Baghdad/Iraq-based jurists favored 'Salam.' Because the future-delivered commodity becomes a debt on the seller, the transaction is also termed Salaf, implying a loan without benefit. This is the direct opposite of Bai' Mu'ajjal (where goods are delivered immediately and price is deferred). Salam's permissibility, despite the general Shari'ah rule against selling a commodity not in the seller's possession, is unanimous among early and contemporary jurists, grounded in a specific Hadith: upon migrating to Madinah, the Prophet (pbuh) found people paying in advance for fruit/dates to be delivered in one to three years, without specifying quality, measure, weight, or delivery time - and he ordained that whoever pays in advance must specify known quality, specified measure and weight, along with the price and time of delivery. S.M. Hasanuz Zaman frames the rationale for this exception as one of 'necessity' - the practice continued after the Prophet's time and was unanimously treated as permissible by later jurists, primarily benefiting farm/orchard owners (the Madinan list covered wheat, barley, dates, grapes; the Syrian conquest added olives and dried grapes), with jurists progressively expanding the eligible commodity list to anything precisely determinable in quality and quantity.","examExplanation":"Salam = forward sale, full price paid in advance, goods delivered later at specified quality/quantity/date (opposite of Bai' Mu'ajjal, where goods are delivered now, price paid later). 'Salaf' (Hijaz usage) and 'Salam' (Baghdad/Iraq usage) are interchangeable terms; since the future commodity is a debt on the seller, it's also called Salaf (a benefit-free loan). Permissibility is unanimous among jurists, based on the Prophet's (pbuh) Hadith in Madinah requiring known quality, specified measure/weight, price, and delivery time for advance-paid fruit sales - the rationale (per Hasanuz Zaman) is 'necessity.'","keyPoints":["Salam = forward sale with FULL price paid in advance at contract signing, goods delivered at a specified future date, quality, and quantity.","Salam is the structural opposite of Bai' Mu'ajjal (where goods are delivered immediately and price is deferred).","'Salaf' (Hijaz usage, meaning payment in advance) and 'Salam' (Baghdad/Iraq usage) were used interchangeably in Hadith literature; because the deferred commodity is a debt on the seller, it is also framed as Salaf (a benefit-free loan).","Salam's permissibility is unanimous despite the general prohibition on selling what the seller does not yet possess, based on a specific Prophetic Hadith requiring known quality, specified measure/weight, price, and delivery time for advance-paid fruit sales in Madinah.","S.M. Hasanuz Zaman frames Salam's permission as grounded in economic 'necessity' - it primarily served farm/orchard owners needing advance funds."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' Salam (Salaf)","definition":"An ancient forward-sale contract in which the price is paid in advance at contract formation, for prescribed goods (specified quality, quantity, delivery date) to be delivered later."}],"conditions":["The goods must be specified by known quality, measure and weight, along with the price and time of delivery, per the Prophet's (pbuh) direct instruction."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's Instruction on Salam in Madinah","kind":"textbook","text":"Upon migrating to Madinah, the Prophet (pbuh) found people paying in advance for fruit/dates to be delivered within one to three years, without specifying quality, measure, weight or delivery time. He ordained: 'Whoever pays money in advance (for fruit to be delivered later) should pay it for a known quality, specified measure and weight, along with the price and time of delivery.'"}],"commonConfusions":["Students often confuse Salam with Bai' Mu'ajjal. Salam = pay now, receive goods later. Bai' Mu'ajjal = receive goods now, pay later. They are structural opposites."],"distinctions":["Salam (price paid now, goods delivered later) vs Bai' Mu'ajjal (goods delivered now, price paid later) - opposite timing structures."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' Mu'ajjal","Gharar","Murabaha"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241,242]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Bai‘ Salam","b":"Bai‘ Mu’ajjal","text":"Salam: price now, goods later. Mu’ajjal: goods now, price later."}]},{"id":"t10.3","section":"10.3","title":"Benefits of Salam and its Economic Role","pages":[242,243],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam"],"intuition":"Why might a farmer prefer Salam to an interest-bearing loan — and why might a trader like it too?","simpleExplanation":"The seller gets money upfront for personal, productive or trading needs; the buyer gets goods at a usually lower price and is protected against price rises. Historically Salam financed farmers for one to three years (even land improvement, wells, irrigation). It avoids interest cost and marketing hardship, links growers directly with city traders, can stabilise prices (no resale before delivery curbs speculation), and offers the benefits of a swap at lower cost without interest.","academicExplanation":["Salam is structured free from Riba and Gharar, based on genuine need. The seller covers liquidity needs; the buyer obtains the commodity when planned, usually at a lower price than cash, secured against price fluctuation. Hasanuz Zaman: the one-to-three-year period implies substantial advances; buyers were traders; advances met productive and consumption needs; the three-year term suggests fixed investment (land improvement, gardens; wells and irrigation in Syria). For farmers it is preferable to interest loans — no increased cost, and relief from marketing risk; it created direct grower–trader links. It can stabilise prices at moderate levels during seasonal falls in demand; since resale of Salam goods before transfer is not allowed, it protects prices from speculative rises, unlike interest-financed inventory which raises costs. It gives the buyer a price hedge, protects both from revenue and price-indexed debt risks, reduces incentives to manipulate reported revenue, involves no predetermined cash debt, and can bring the benefits of a swap without interest at lower transaction cost."],"examExplanation":"Seller: advance liquidity. Buyer: lower price, hedge. Farmers: no interest cost, no marketing hardship. Direct grower–trader link; price stabilisation; swap-like benefits without interest.","keyPoints":["Salam price is usually lower than cash price.","No resale before delivery curbs speculation (majority view).","Salam can give the benefits of a swap without interest and at lower transaction cost."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why delivery periods of one to three years matter","kind":"textbook","text":"Hasanuz Zaman infers that advances were large, buyers were traders, and funds met both consumption and fixed investment such as land improvement, wells and irrigation (pp. 242–243)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.10","t8.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which is a benefit of Salam to the buyer identified in Section 10.3?","options":["Guaranteed interest","Usually a cheaper price and protection against price fluctuations","Immediate delivery","No need to pay in advance"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 242."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.3","sectionTitle":"Benefits of Salam and the Economic Role of Bai' Salam","title":"Benefits and Economic Role of Salam","covers":["t10.3"],"overview":"Explains why Salam benefits both farmer-sellers and trader-buyers, its price-stabilizing effect, and its function as a Riba-free, Gharar-free alternative to interest-based financing and conventional swaps.","simpleExplanation":"Salam is a win-win: the farmer gets cash now for expenses like buying seed or improving land, while the trader-buyer locks in goods at a price that's usually cheaper than the future cash market price, protecting against price swings. Unlike an interest loan, Salam doesn't increase the farmer's costs, and because Salam goods generally can't be resold before delivery, it helps keep prices stable rather than fueling speculation.","academicExplanation":"Forward sale via Salam is structured to be free from Riba, Gharar and exploitation, meeting genuine business need on both sides: the seller (typically a farmer) gets advance cash to cover personal, productive, or trading needs; the buyer secures the commodity at a price typically cheaper than the eventual cash market price, hedging against future price fluctuations. Per S.M. Hasanuz Zaman's analysis, the Hadith's one-to-three-year delivery periods suggest sizeable advances aimed at traders/prospective traders (not consumers), meeting both productive and consumption needs, and possibly funding fixed investment like land improvement or irrigation. Compared to an interest loan, Salam does not increase the farmer's cost, and it spares him marketing risk/hardship, fostering a direct grower-to-city-trader relationship. Because Shari'ah disallows reselling Salam goods before actual transfer, Bai' Salam helps stabilize prices at a moderate level during seasonal demand troughs and protects against speculative price rises - contrary to interest-financed production/inventory building, which raises costs further inflated by speculative trading. Salam provides a price hedge for the buyer and protects both parties from revenue/price-indexed-debt risk, with less incentive for the seller to manipulate reported revenues (unlike conventional forward markets), since price variation risk is already transferred to the buyer via the predetermined price, while quantity risk is contractually fixed. As a forward contract without a predetermined cash debt on either party, Salam can deliver benefits similar to a conventional swap - without interest and at substantially lower transaction cost - and financing can be obtained directly from buyers (including banks, competitively) without an intermediary.","examExplanation":"Salam benefits: seller gets advance cash (no interest cost, avoids marketing hardship); buyer gets below-market price and protection from future price rises. Delivery periods (1-3 years per the original Hadith) suggest funding for traders/production, not mere consumption. Because Salam goods generally cannot be resold before delivery, it helps stabilize prices (vs speculative interest-financed inventory building). Salam can replicate swap-like benefits (hedging) without interest, at much lower transaction cost than a conventional swap.","keyPoints":["Salam benefits the seller (advance cash for productive/personal needs, no interest cost) and the buyer (typically below-market price, protection from future price rises).","The 1-3 year delivery periods in the founding Hadith suggest the buyers were traders/prospective traders (not consumers) and that funds may also have financed fixed investment (land improvement, irrigation).","Because Shari'ah generally disallows reselling Salam goods before actual transfer, Salam helps stabilize prices during seasonal demand troughs and protects against speculative price rises.","Salam allocates price risk to the buyer (via the predetermined price) and fixes quantity risk contractually, reducing the seller's incentive to manipulate reported revenues (unlike conventional forward markets).","Salam can deliver swap-like hedging benefits without interest, at a lower transaction cost than a conventional swap, and can be obtained directly from buyers (including competing banks) without an intermediary."],"definitions":[{"term":"Swap","definition":"A conventional agreement whereby one party replaces one cash flow/commitment with another indexed to some price or interest rate; the chapter notes Salam can replicate swap-like hedging benefits without interest and at lower transaction cost."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Salam's price-stabilizing effect is incidental. The book frames it as a direct structural consequence of the Shari'ah rule against reselling Salam goods before delivery, which curbs speculative price manipulation."],"distinctions":["Salam-financed production/inventory (cost not inflated, price-stabilizing) vs interest-financed production/inventory (cost inflated by interest, further inflated by speculative trading)."],"relatedConcepts":["Gharar","Riba","Parallel Salam"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.3","pages":[242,243]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Salam","b":"Interest-based production loan","text":"Salam adds no interest cost, removes the farmer’s marketing risk and, because resale before delivery is restricted, dampens speculative price rises; interest loans raise production and stock costs (p. 243)."}]},{"id":"t10.4.1","section":"10.4.1","title":"Subject Matter of Salam (and Salam in Currencies)","pages":[244,246],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam","mithli-qimi","bai-sarf"],"intuition":"What can — and cannot — be bought through Salam?","simpleExplanation":"Anything precisely determinable in quality and quantity: fungible (Mithli) goods, not specific units. It must be normally available in the market at delivery; it cannot be money (gold, silver, currencies), identical items exchanged (wheat for wheat), specific items (“this car”, land, buildings, trees, “this field”) or subjectively valued items (gems, antiques). The seller need not produce the goods. Salam in currencies is invalid because currency exchange must be simultaneous.","academicExplanation":["Consensus: everything precisely determinable in quality and quantity; well-defined but not particularised to a specific farm, tree or garden; only Mithli goods whose units do not differ significantly. Not where both items are identical (wheat for wheat) nor money-like items (gold, silver, currency). Classical jurists disagreed on animals, flesh, fodder, bread, milk, vegetables, precious stones, cloth, carpets, bricks, drugs etc. because standardisation was difficult. Contemporary scholars: all goods standardisable into identical units — grains, oil, iron, copper, regularly available company products like carpets and tinned goods (AAOIFI 3/2/2). The commodity must be normally available at least at delivery (all schools; AAOIFI 3/2/8), and specifications must cover all price-affecting characteristics.","Salam is not allowed for identified things (“this car”), things the seller cannot be responsible for (land, buildings, trees, produce of “this field”), or subjectively valued items (landscapes, precious gems, antiques — AAOIFI 3/2/3). Companions paid Syrian peasants in advance without asking whether they owned standing crops — the seller need not produce the goods.","Salam in currencies: the majority disallow it, though a few allow it and some banks used it as an alternative to bill discounting. Paper money is only a price (Thaman) with no value absent government commitment; US Dollars as price for Pak Rupees is a currency exchange needing simultaneous payment, while Salam defers delivery. Justice Khalil-ur-Rehman: Fulus were sub-money for fractions; paper money is like gold and silver. IRTI (Umar, 1995) and Shirbini: currency exchange cannot be via Salam. Hanafi and Maliki jurists: Salam capital is money and the two items must not be of a kind whose exchange leads to Riba (Ibn Rushd). Allowing it would open a floodgate of Riba."],"examExplanation":"Salam subject: Mithli, precisely specified, available at delivery; not money, not identical exchange, not specific items/land/buildings, not subjectively valued items. Seller need not be producer. Salam in currencies invalid (currency exchange must be spot).","keyPoints":["Not particularised to a specific farm or field.","Must be available in the market at delivery time.","Salam in currency = floodgate of Riba."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Syrian peasants","kind":"textbook","text":"Companions paid Syrian peasants in advance for wheat, barley and oil; asked whether the peasants owned standing crops, they said “we never asked” — the seller need not produce the goods himself (p. 245)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Salam may specify “the produce of this field”.","right":"It may not: a particular field may yield nothing. Salam is for fungible goods defined by specification and generally available in the market at delivery (p. 244)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.5","t5.2","t10.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which can be the subject of Salam?","options":["The produce of ‘this specific field’","1000 tons of a defined variety of rice","A particular antique vase","Pakistani Rupees against US Dollars"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 244–246."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.4-10.4.1","sectionTitle":"Features of a Valid Salam Contract / Subject Matter of Salam","title":"Features of a Valid Salam Contract: Eligible Subject Matter and the Currency Restriction","covers":["t10.4.1"],"overview":"Details the conditions governing what may be sold under Salam - fungibility, standardizability, market availability - and explains why Salam in currencies/gold/silver is generally disallowed, plus the full transaction flow banks use.","simpleExplanation":"Only things that can be precisely defined by quality and quantity - like wheat, rice, or standardized manufactured goods - can be sold via Salam; you can't use it for something unique like 'this specific car' or a particular field's future harvest, because those can't be guaranteed. The seller doesn't even need to grow or make the goods themselves - they just need to be able to supply them from the market when the time comes. And you can't do Salam in money itself (currencies, gold, silver) because exchanging money for money has its own strict same-time-same-value rules.","academicExplanation":"There is consensus that anything precisely determinable in quality and quantity can be the subject of Salam; the commodity must be well-defined but not particularized to a specific unit (farm, tree, garden) - only fungible (Mithli) items whose units don't significantly differ from each other qualify, and Salam cannot occur between identical items (e.g. wheat for wheat). The commodity itself must not be of the nature of money (gold, silver, currency). Classical jurists debated many specific items (animals, honey, milk, precious stones, etc.) given the difficulty of standardization in their era; contemporary scholars conclude that any goods standardizable into identical units (wheat, rice, barley, oil, iron, copper, regularly available branded/packaged products) qualify, provided the commodity is generally available in the market at least at the agreed delivery time (not out of season or rare/inaccessible). The buyer must unambiguously define quality/quantity, covering all characteristics that could cause price variation. Salam is NOT allowed for a specifically identified item ('this car') or for things the seller cannot be held responsible for (a specific field's future produce, since that field might yield nothing), nor for items whose value depends on subjective assessment (landscapes, precious gems, antiques). Notably, per a Hadith reported by Imam Bukhari (Abdur Rahman bin Abza and Abdullah bin Abi Aufa), the Salam seller need not own standing crops or be the producer - specification is deliberately generic so the seller can source the item from the market. On Salam in currencies: the majority of jurists disallow Salam in gold, silver, currencies or monetary units (though a minority permit it, and some banks use it as an alternative to bill discounting). The reasoning: paper money is Thaman (a medium of exchange/measure of value) with no inherent utility of its own, wanted only for payment - unlike gold/silver/Fulus (which had trade uses beyond payment and so could historically be traded as commodities). If a Salam's price is one currency and its 'commodity' is another currency, this is in substance a currency exchange, which Bai' al Sarf requires to be simultaneous - directly conflicting with Salam's deferred-delivery structure. The IRTI/IDB study (Umar, 1995) and Allama Shirbini both confirm: if both the Salam principal and the sold item are money, the transaction is invalid, since Salam permits deferring only ONE of the two exchanged values, while currency exchange requires simultaneous payment of both. The conclusion: forward sale/purchase of currencies via Salam is not valid; the objects of Salam sale must be trade commodities, not currencies (which fall under Bai' al Sarf).","examExplanation":"Salam subject matter must be: precisely determinable in quality/quantity (fungible/Mithli, not unique/Qimi), well-defined but not tied to a specific unit (not 'this field's crop'), generally available in the market at delivery time, and NOT of the nature of money (gold/silver/currency). Seller need not own or produce the goods themselves - specification allows sourcing from the market. Salam in currencies is generally disallowed by majority view: a currency-for-currency Salam is in substance Bai' al Sarf (requiring simultaneous exchange), which conflicts with Salam's deferred-delivery structure - so it is invalid (minority of jurists and a few banks permit it as bill-discounting alternative, but this is not mainstream).","keyPoints":["Salam subject matter must be fungible (Mithli - units don't significantly differ) and precisely determinable in quality and quantity - not unique/one-of-a-kind items.","The commodity must be generally available in the market at least by the agreed delivery time - not out-of-season, rare, or otherwise inaccessible.","Salam is invalid for a specifically identified item ('this car'), for a particular field/orchard's future produce (the seller can't guarantee a specific field yields anything), and for items valued by subjective assessment (landscapes, gems, antiques).","The Salam seller need NOT own, grow, or produce the goods himself - per Hadith, Companions confirmed they never asked Syrian peasant sellers whether they owned standing crops; the seller can source the item from the market.","The majority of jurists disallow Salam in gold, silver, currencies or monetary units, because currency-for-currency exchange is governed by Bai' al Sarf (requiring simultaneous payment of both values), which directly conflicts with Salam's deferred-delivery structure."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mithli","definition":"Fungible - goods whose various units do not differ from each other in any significant manner, making them eligible for Salam (as opposed to Qimi/non-fungible goods, where every unit differs in quality and price)."},{"term":"Fulus","definition":"Copper or other inferior-metal coins historically used as sub-money for fractional payments; possessing intrinsic commodity value, distinguishing them from today's fiat paper currency."}],"conditions":["The Salam commodity must be fungible (Mithli), precisely determinable in quality and quantity, well-defined but not tied to a specific individual unit (farm, tree, garden).","The commodity must be generally available in the market at least at the agreed time of delivery.","Salam is not valid for a specifically identified/designated item, for a specific field/orchard's produce, or for items whose value depends on subjective assessment.","Salam cannot be conducted in currencies, gold, or silver, per the majority view, since this would constitute a currency exchange requiring simultaneous payment under Bai' al Sarf."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Hadith on Syrian Peasant Sellers","kind":"textbook","text":"As reported by Imam Bukhari, Companions Abdur Rahman bin Abza and Abdullah bin Abi Aufa said: 'When the peasants of Syria came to us, we used to pay them in advance for wheat, barley and oil to be delivered within a fixed period.' Asked whether the peasants owned standing crops, they replied, 'We never asked them about it' - confirming that a Salam seller need not himself produce or own the goods in advance."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think the Salam seller must be the actual producer (e.g. the farmer growing the crop). The book is explicit, via the cited Hadith, that this is not required - the seller only needs to be able to supply the specified goods from the market by the delivery date."],"distinctions":["Fungible/Mithli goods (Salam-eligible: standardizable units) vs non-fungible/Qimi goods (not Salam-eligible: every unit differs in price/quality) - though Istisna'a can cover Qimi goods requiring manufacture."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al Sarf","Gharar","Parallel Salam"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4-10.4.1","pages":[243,244,245,246]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Salam Agreement","description":"The bank (as buyer) and the seller (e.g. a farmer or trader) agree on the precise quality, quantity, price, and delivery date/place of a fungible commodity that will generally be available in the market at delivery time."},{"step":"Advance Payment (Salam Capital)","description":"The bank pays the entire agreed price to the seller on the spot, at the time the contract is signed - this upfront payment is what distinguishes Salam and is the seller's source of working capital."},{"step":"Production/Sourcing Period","description":"Before the delivery date, the goods remain at the seller's risk; the seller uses the advance funds to grow, produce, or otherwise source the specified commodity, without needing to personally own or manufacture it."},{"step":"Delivery of the Commodity","description":"At the agreed time and place, the seller delivers the commodity of the stipulated specification to the bank; upon actual or constructive possession, ownership and risk transfer from the seller to the bank."},{"step":"Bank Disposes of the Goods","description":"Since the bank generally cannot resell the goods before taking delivery, once it has taken delivery it disposes of them via Parallel Salam, a prior promise to purchase from a third party, appointing the original seller as selling agent, or direct sale in the open market."}]}],"conditions":["Fungible (Mithli), standardisable goods","Precisely specified quality and quantity (all price-relevant attributes)","Normally available in the market at delivery","Not money (gold, silver, currency)","Not identical to the price commodity (no wheat for wheat)","Not a specific identified item, land, building, tree or ‘this field’","Not a subjectively valued item (gems, antiques)"]},{"id":"t10.4.2","section":"10.4.2","title":"Payment of Price: Salam Capital","pages":[246,247],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam","bai-dayn"],"intuition":"Why must the Salam price be paid upfront — and can it be paid by cancelling a debt?","simpleExplanation":"The price is normally money but may be goods (without Riba in barter) or usufruct. It cannot be an existing debt owed by the seller or a third party (debt for debt). It must be paid in full at contract; contemporary jurists allow a stipulated delay of two to three days. Crediting the seller’s account or a pay order is acceptable.","academicExplanation":["Price is normally legal tender; it can be goods, if not violating barter Riba rules, or usufruct of assets (Malikis: possession of part is possession of the whole, so not debt for debt). Outstanding debts of the seller or a third party cannot be set as price — debt for debt and Gharar. The term Salaf means advance payment; if delayed it is not Salam. The majority require payment in the contract session; Shafi‘i: on the spot before separation; some allow delay not amounting to debt; Malik up to three days; contemporary jurists (AAOIFI) allow two to three days if stipulated and before delivery. In barter, the same species cannot be advanced for deferred delivery (10 tons improved seed for 25 tons wheat — no), but a tractor for cotton is possible. Cash is not necessary: crediting the seller’s account or a cashable pay order places money at his disposal and is not the prohibited debt for debt."],"examExplanation":"Salam capital: money (or goods/usufruct); full payment at contract (≤2–3 days delay allowed by contemporary scholars); no debt set-off; account credit/pay order OK.","keyPoints":["Bank cannot offset an existing receivable as Salam price.","Improved seed for wheat at harvest: same genus — not allowed.","Majority: pay the full price in the contract session; Imam Malik and contemporary jurists allow a delay of up to two or three days if stipulated.","Usufruct of an asset can be Salam capital (Maliki view)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Crediting the seller’s account","kind":"textbook","text":"The bank need not pay hard cash; crediting the seller’s account or issuing a pay order cashable on demand places the money at his disposal and is not a prohibited debt-for-debt exchange (p. 247)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bank may use a customer’s existing debt as the Salam price.","right":"An outstanding debt of the seller or of a third party cannot be the price: that is debt for debt and Gharar; the price must actually be paid (p. 247)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.5.5","t10.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A bank wants to use an existing loan owed by the farmer as the Salam price. This is:","options":["Permissible","Not permissible — debt for debt","Permissible if interest-free","Required"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 247."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.4.2","sectionTitle":"Payment of Price: Salam Capital","title":"Salam Capital: Rules for Payment of the Advance Price","covers":["t10.4.2"],"overview":"Details what can constitute Salam capital, timing rules for payment, restrictions on using debt as capital, and permissible payment methods.","simpleExplanation":"The buyer in Salam has to pay the full price up front - that's the whole point of the contract. It's usually money, but can also be goods or the right to use an asset (usufruct), as long as it doesn't create its own Riba problem. You can't use an existing debt someone owes you as the 'payment' - that would be trading one unpaid obligation for another, which is not allowed. And while payment should ideally happen at the very moment the contract is signed, most scholars allow a short grace period of a few days.","academicExplanation":"Price is normally stipulated and paid in legal tender, but can also be in the form of goods (provided it doesn't violate the Riba prohibition on barter) or usufruct of assets - the Maliki view treats usufruct as an immediate receipt of capital based on the maxim 'taking possession of a part of a thing is like taking possession of the whole thing,' so usufruct-as-capital does not constitute prohibited debt-for-debt. Outstanding loans/debts owed by the seller (or transferred from a third party) cannot be fixed as Salam price - this would be an exchange of obligation for obligation (debt for debt), which is forbidden, both because Salam's very validity depends on genuine capital being advanced, and to avoid Gharar; the term Salaf itself means advance payment, so delayed payment cannot be termed Salam. On timing: the majority require payment at the very time/meeting of contract signing; Imam Shafi'e insists on payment on the spot before the parties separate; some jurists (Imam Malik) allow up to a three-day delay; contemporary jurists similarly allow a stipulated two-to-three-day delay, provided it precedes the delivery period. On barter Salam: goods cannot be advanced against deferred delivery of the SAME species of goods (e.g. wheat seed for wheat at harvest is disallowed), but a different type of asset (e.g. a tractor) can serve as price for an agreed quantity/quality of a different commodity (e.g. cotton or rice) - though banks in practice generally avoid this and use money. On mode of payment: cash payment is not strictly necessary - crediting the seller's account or issuing a cashable pay order is acceptable; while crediting an account might appear, in letter, to be a 'debt for a debt', in spirit it is not, since the money remains at the seller's disposal.","examExplanation":"Salam capital: normally money, but can be goods (non-Riba-violating barter) or usufruct (Maliki view: 'possession of a part = possession of the whole'). CANNOT be an existing debt/loan (debt-for-debt is forbidden - the very meaning of Salaf is advance payment). Timing: majority require payment at contract signing/session; Shafi'e strictest (on the spot, before parties separate); Imam Malik and contemporary jurists allow up to ~2-3 days' delay if stipulated, before the delivery period begins. Same-species barter (e.g. wheat seed for wheat) is disallowed; different-species barter (tractor for cotton) is permitted but rarely used in practice. Payment mode: cash not required - crediting the account or a cashable pay order suffices (not deemed prohibited debt-for-debt 'in spirit').","keyPoints":["Salam capital is normally money but can also be goods (non-Riba-violating) or usufruct of assets (per the Maliki maxim, 'possession of a part is like possession of the whole').","An existing debt/loan (owed by the seller or a third party) cannot be used as Salam capital - this would be a prohibited debt-for-debt exchange, and it undermines the requirement of genuine advanced capital.","Timing of payment: the majority require payment at the contract session itself; Imam Shafi'e requires it strictly on the spot before the parties separate; Imam Malik and contemporary jurists permit a stipulated 2-3 day delay, provided it precedes the delivery period.","Same-species barter Salam (e.g. wheat seed for wheat at harvest) is not allowed; different-species barter (e.g. a tractor as price for cotton) is permitted in principle, though banks in practice generally use money instead.","Cash payment is not strictly required - crediting the seller's account or issuing a cashable pay order satisfies the advance-payment requirement, since the funds remain at the seller's disposal."],"definitions":[{"term":"Salam Capital","definition":"The price paid in advance by the Salam buyer at contract formation - normally money, but permissibly goods or usufruct, provided it does not consist of an existing debt."}],"conditions":["The buyer must advance the whole price at (or, per most contemporary views, within a short stipulated delay of) contract formation - the very meaning of Salaf is advance payment.","An outstanding debt owed by the seller, or a third-party debt transferred to the seller, cannot be used as Salam capital.","Same-species barter (advancing a quantity of goods against deferred delivery of the same species) is not allowed in Salam."],"principles":["The Maliki legal maxim 'taking possession of a part of a thing is like taking possession of the whole thing' underlies why usufruct can serve as Salam capital without constituting prohibited debt-for-debt."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think 'advance payment' strictly means physical cash handed over. The book clarifies that crediting the seller's account or issuing a cashable pay order also satisfies the requirement, since the funds are placed at the seller's disposal."],"distinctions":["Debt-for-debt (prohibited: using an existing loan/debt as Salam capital) vs genuine advance payment (required: fresh capital advanced at contract formation)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Gharar","Bai' al Sarf"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]}}]},{"id":"t10.4.3","section":"10.4.3","title":"Period and Place of Delivery","pages":[247,248],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam"],"intuition":"How precise must the delivery date and place be?","simpleExplanation":"Time and place of delivery must be fixed. Hadith practice was one to three years; later jurists allowed as little as fifteen days or even one day. Contemporary scholars: any known due date suitable to the commodity; delivery in instalments allowed. Before delivery risk is the seller’s; after, the buyer’s. If no place is stipulated, the contract location applies.","academicExplanation":["Delivery time must be precisely fixed and place agreed. Early practice: one to three years for farm products; later jurists reduced it to fifteen days, some to one day (minimum time to transport between markets); some required an exact date, others a definite period such as harvest. Contemporary scholars: known due date and place; from a few days to years; delivery in consignments/instalments if agreed (AAOIFI 3/2/9). Goods remain at the seller’s risk before delivery; delivery physical or constructive; transfer of risk and authority of use are ingredients of constructive possession. If place is not stipulated, the place of contract applies, or as agreed by custom (AAOIFI 3/2/10)."],"examExplanation":"Fix date and place; any known period; instalments allowed; risk passes on delivery (physical/constructive); default place = contract place.","keyPoints":["Delivery can be in instalments.","Later jurists reduced the minimum delivery period to 15 days or even a day; contemporary scholars require only a known due date and place.","Delivery in instalments is allowed; if no place is stipulated, the place of contract applies."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Delivery in instalments","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank buys 3,000 tons of rice on Salam, deliverable 1,000 tons each in June, July and August at the bank’s Karachi warehouse. Instalments are allowed if agreed. Had no place been stated, the place of contract would apply (pp. 247–248)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Risk passes to the Salam buyer at the contract date.","right":"The goods stay at the seller’s risk until delivery, physical or constructive (p. 248)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If the Salam contract does not specify the place of delivery, delivery is at:","options":["The buyer’s warehouse","The place where the contract was executed (or as customarily agreed)","The nearest port","Any place chosen by the seller"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 248."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.4.3-10.4.5","sectionTitle":"Period and Place of Delivery / Khiyar (Option) in Salam / Amending or Revoking the Salam Contract","title":"Delivery Terms, Option Restrictions, and Revocation Rules in Salam","covers":["t10.4.3","t10.4.4"],"overview":"Covers the requirement to fix delivery time/place, why most option rights (Khiyar) are disallowed in Salam (except for defects), and the strict conditions under which a Salam contract can be amended or revoked.","simpleExplanation":"A Salam contract must clearly state when and where the goods will be delivered - this can range from a few days to several years depending on the goods. Once signed, Salam is a firm, binding deal: neither party can back out just because market prices moved in their favor or against them. The buyer doesn't get a 'let me see it first' option like in normal sales (since the goods don't exist yet), but does get to reject genuinely defective or substandard goods after delivery.","academicExplanation":"The Salam contract must precisely fix the delivery period/time and place. Early Hadith compilations mention one-to-three-year delivery periods for farm products; later jurists reduced this to as little as fifteen days or even one day (the minimum time needed to transport goods between markets); some jurists required a precise date, others accepted a rough but definite period/occasion (e.g. harvest time). Contemporary scholars simply require the due date and place to be known, ranging from days to years depending on the commodity, with delivery in instalments/consignments permitted if mutually agreed. Before delivery, goods remain at the seller's risk; after delivery (physical or constructive), risk transfers to the buyer - the defining ingredients of constructive possession are risk transfer and delegated authority of use/consumption. If no delivery place is stipulated, the place of contract execution is used by default, subject to customary practice. On Khiyar: jurists disallow Khiyar al-Shart (option to rescind) in Salam because it would disturb/delay the seller's right of ownership over the price; the buyer also has no 'option of seeing' (Khiyar al Ro'yat), unlike normal sales - since the goods don't exist yet. However, AFTER delivery, the buyer retains Khiyar al 'Aib (option of defect) and the option of specified quality: if goods are defective or non-conforming, the buyer can rescind, recovering only the paid price (no increase). On amendment/revocation: the seller is bound to deliver as stipulated; the buyer cannot unilaterally alter quality, quantity, or delivery period after payment. Both parties may rescind by mutual consent (in full or part), with the buyer recovering exactly the amount advanced (no more, no less). Because either party might be tempted to rescind for pure price-movement reasons (seller if market price rises above the Salam price, buyer/bank if it falls), it is advisable to make bank-supplier Salam irrevocable, with the sole exception being genuine unavailability/inaccessibility of the goods at delivery time (and only if the bank refuses to extend delivery to the next supply season) - in which case, revocation returns exactly the amount paid, no more, no less. If goods are supplied early, the buyer is generally not bound to accept unless it suits him, and can refuse only for nonconformity with specifications - price changes alone never justify refusal or rescission by either party. Substitution of the agreed goods (after the due date, by mutual consent) is allowed only if the substitute is of a different genus and its market value does not exceed the original commodity's value at the delivery date, and this substitution possibility must not itself be pre-stipulated in the contract.","examExplanation":"Delivery: time/place must be precisely fixed (days to years depending on commodity); instalment delivery is allowed if agreed; risk stays with seller until delivery (physical/constructive), then transfers to buyer. Khiyar: Khiyar al-Shart (rescission option) and Khiyar al Ro'yat (option of seeing) are BOTH disallowed in Salam (no pre-existing goods to inspect); but Khiyar al 'Aib (defect option) is available AFTER delivery for defective/nonconforming goods (recovery limited to price paid, no increase). Revocation: Salam is binding/nonrevocable except by mutual consent; the sole unilateral exception is genuine goods unavailability at delivery time (bank must first refuse to extend to next season); price movement alone NEVER justifies rescission by either party. Post-due-date substitution of goods is allowed only with mutual consent, different genus, and value not exceeding the original commodity's delivery-date value.","keyPoints":["Delivery time and place must be precisely fixed in the Salam contract; instalment/consignment delivery is permitted if mutually agreed.","Risk remains with the seller until delivery (physical or constructive); after delivery, risk transfers to the buyer.","Khiyar al-Shart (rescission option) and Khiyar al Ro'yat (option of seeing) are both disallowed in Salam - the former because it would disturb the seller's right over the advance price, the latter because the goods do not yet exist.","Khiyar al 'Aib (option of defect) remains available AFTER delivery: if goods are defective or non-conforming, the buyer can rescind but recovers only the price paid, with no increase.","Salam is a binding, nonrevocable contract except by mutual consent; the sole recognized unilateral exception is the seller's genuine inability to access the goods at delivery time, and only if the buyer refuses to extend delivery to the next supply season.","Price movement alone (in either direction) never justifies unilateral rescission by either party - this is precisely why making bank-supplier Salam irrevocable is advisable."],"definitions":[{"term":"Khiyar al-Shart","definition":"The stipulated option/right to rescind a contract within a fixed period - disallowed in Salam because it would disturb or delay the seller's right of ownership over the advance price."},{"term":"Khiyar al Ro'yat","definition":"The 'option of seeing' available in normal sales, allowing a buyer to rescind after physically inspecting goods - not available in Salam since the goods do not yet exist at contract formation."}],"conditions":["The delivery time and place must be precisely fixed in the Salam contract.","Substitution of the agreed Salam goods after the due date is permitted only with mutual consent, using a different genus of goods whose market value does not exceed the original commodity's delivery-date value, and only if not pre-stipulated in the contract."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume Salam allows an inspection-based option like ordinary sales. Since the goods do not exist at contract formation, Khiyar al Ro'yat (option of seeing) simply does not apply; only Khiyar al 'Aib (defect option) becomes available, and only after actual delivery."],"distinctions":["Khiyar al-Shart/Khiyar al Ro'yat (disallowed in Salam, pre-delivery) vs Khiyar al 'Aib (available in Salam, but only post-delivery for genuine defects)."],"relatedConcepts":["Khiyar al 'Aib","Constructive Possession","Force Majeure"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.3-10.4.5","pages":[247,248,249]}}]},{"id":"t10.4.4","section":"10.4.4","title":"Options, Amendment/Revocation and Penalty in Salam","pages":[248,249],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam","khiyar","late-payment-penalty"],"intuition":"Once a Salam is signed, can either side walk away if prices move?","simpleExplanation":"No Khiyar al-Shart or Khiyar al-Ro’yat in Salam; after delivery the buyer has options of defect and quality (recovering only the price paid). Salam is binding: neither party may unilaterally change terms; both may rescind by mutual consent, refunding exactly the price. Banks should make it irrevocable except when goods vanish from the market. Early delivery need not be accepted unless in the buyer’s interest. Substitutes after due date by consent (different genus, value not above the original, not pre-stipulated). A late-delivery penalty may be pledged to charity — not bank income (AAOIFI 5/7); insolvent sellers get more time.","academicExplanation":["Options (10.4.4): jurists disallow Khiyar al-Shart (it delays the seller’s ownership of the price) and Khiyar al-Ro’yat. After delivery the buyer has Khiyar al-‘Aib and the option of specified quality; on rescission only the price paid is recovered, without increase.","Amending or revoking (10.4.5): the seller must deliver as stipulated; the buyer cannot unilaterally change quality, quantity or period after payment. Both may rescind by mutual consent wholly or partly; the buyer gets back exactly the amount advanced. Sellers may wish to rescind when market prices rise, banks when they fall — so bank Salam should be irrevocable; the only exception is complete absence of goods from the market at delivery, when the seller may rescind if the bank refuses to extend to the next season. If goods are supplied early, jurists generally do not bind the buyer to accept (some relax subject to the buyer’s interest); price changes allow neither party to refuse. Salam is non-revocable except by mutual consent (majority). After the due date, the buyer may take other goods if agreed, of a different genus, with market value not exceeding the original’s at delivery, and not stipulated in the contract (AAOIFI 4/2, 5/4).","Penalty (10.4.6): the seller may undertake to pay into the bank’s Charity Account for late delivery — a self-imposed penalty. AAOIFI 5/7: a penalty clause for delay in delivering the Salam commodity is not permitted — i.e. it cannot be the bank’s income. If the seller fails due to insolvency, he should be granted an extension (AAOIFI 5/6)."],"examExplanation":"No Khiyar al-Shart/Ro’yat; defect/quality options after delivery (price refund only). Binding; rescission only by consent at exact price. Substitution rules. Late-delivery penalty only to charity; insolvency → extension.","keyPoints":["Refund on rescission = exactly the advance, no more or less.","Substitute: different genus, value ≤ original, not pre-stipulated.","Contrast with Istisna‘a’s Shart-e-Jazai."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Substituting cotton for wheat","kind":"textbook","text":"After the due date, if wheat is completely unavailable, the parties may agree to take cotton instead — a different genus, not worth more than the wheat at delivery and not stipulated in advance — but not corn (pp. 249, 253–254)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.11.4","t10.7","t6.12"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If a Salam contract is rescinded by mutual consent, the bank recovers:","options":["The market value of the goods","Exactly the price it paid, without increase or decrease","Price plus profit","Half the price"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 248."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"subsections":[{"number":"10.4.4","title":"Khiyar (Option) in Salam","page":248,"points":["No Shart or Ro’yat options","Defect and quality options after delivery"]},{"number":"10.4.5","title":"Amending or Revoking the Salam Contract","page":248,"points":["Binding; mutual rescission at exact price","Early delivery; substitution rules"]},{"number":"10.4.6","title":"Penalty for Nonperformance","page":249,"points":["Charity undertaking","Not bank income (AAOIFI 5/7)","Extension for insolvency"]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Options available","b":"Options not available","text":"After delivery the buyer has the options of defect and specified quality; Khiyar al-Shart and Khiyar al-Ro’yat are not available in Salam (p. 248)."}]},{"id":"t10.5","section":"10.5","title":"Security, Pledge and Liability of the Sureties","pages":[249,250],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["kafalah","rihn"],"intuition":"The bank pays upfront — how does it protect itself if the seller fails to deliver?","simpleExplanation":"Security, personal surety or pledge may be taken (Bukhari’s chapters on Kafeel and Rihn in Salam). On default the bank may sell the pledge and buy the goods from the market (involving the customer) or recover only its advance and return the balance. A surety must deliver if the seller fails. Liability can be shifted via Hawalah; if Salam is rescinded, surety liability ends and the pledge is released.","academicExplanation":["Security or pledge in Salam is permissible from the Sunnah (the Prophet borrowed grain against his breastplate); Imam Bukhari captioned chapters on Kafeel and Rihn in Salam, and Ibn Hajar explains Kafeel is implied. The seller may furnish security, personal surety or pledge. On default, the bank may sell the pledge and buy the stipulated goods from the market (with the customer involved to avoid exploitation) or take back its advance, returning the balance — never more than the price paid, as it is like a debt. A personal surety must deliver the goods if the seller fails; only the seller (not the surety) may revoke, and only the price is then taken. The seller may shift liability by Hawalah with the purchaser’s permission and the transferee’s acceptance. If Salam is rescinded, surety or transferee liability ceases and the pledge is released."],"examExplanation":"Security/surety/pledge allowed. Default: sell pledge and buy goods, or recover advance only (no more). Surety delivers; Hawalah possible; rescission releases surety and pledge.","keyPoints":["Bank can never recover more than its advance in cash.","Bukhari has chapters on Kafeel and Rihn in Salam; the Prophet (pbuh) borrowed grain against his breastplate.","Only the seller, not the surety, may revoke the Salam; rescission releases the surety and the pledge."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Selling the pledge on default","kind":"textbook","text":"If the seller defaults, the bank may sell the pledge and buy the specified goods from the market (involving the customer), or take back only its advance and return the balance (pp. 249–250)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t7.15","t10.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If the bank recovers cash from a defaulting Salam seller’s pledge, the maximum it may keep is:","options":["The market value of the goods","The price paid in advance","Price plus expected profit","Unlimited"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 249."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.4.6-10.5","sectionTitle":"Penalty for Nonperformance / Security, Pledge and Liability of the Sureties","title":"Penalties, Security, Pledges and Sureties in Salam","covers":["t10.5"],"overview":"Explains how late-delivery penalties must be charity-directed (not bank income), and the Shari'ah basis for taking security, pledges, and personal sureties in Salam.","simpleExplanation":"If the seller delivers late, the contract can require them to pay a penalty - but that money has to go to charity, not into the bank's own pocket, because the bank has no right to more than what it originally paid. To protect itself, the bank can also demand collateral or a guarantor from the seller (the Prophet himself once pledged his armor for a Salam-style grain purchase), and if the seller defaults, the bank can sell the pledged security to buy the goods elsewhere.","academicExplanation":"The Salam seller can undertake to pay into the bank's Charity Account for late delivery - a self-imposed deterrent penalty. AAOIFI's Salam Standard clause 5/7 disallows stipulating a penalty clause specifically against the Muslam Fihi (Salam commodity) delay becoming part of the bank's (seller's) income - meaning such penalties cannot enrich the bank; they exist purely to discourage wilful default (as elaborated in Chapters 4 and 7). If nonperformance results from genuine insolvency (not wilful default), the seller should be granted a delivery extension. On security: Imam Bukhari's chapters 'Kafeel fis Salam' and 'Al-Rihn fis Salam' report the Hadith of the Prophet (pbuh) borrowing grain from a Jew against a pledged iron breastplate - Ibn Hajar explains this establishes permissibility for both surety (Kafeel) and pledge (Rihn) in Salam, even though the specific Hadith text doesn't mention Kafeel explicitly. The seller can be required to furnish security, personal surety, or a pledge; if the seller defaults, the bank may sell the pledge and purchase the stipulated goods from the market (in collaboration with the customer, to avoid exploitation) or take its advance payment from the sale proceeds and return any balance - but recovery is capped at the original advance price (since the advance is treated as a debt owed by the seller, not subject to increase). If a personal surety was furnished, the surety becomes liable to deliver the goods if the seller fails; only the seller (not the surety) can authorize revocation, in which case only the price paid is recovered. With the buyer's consent, the seller can shift liability to a transferee via Hawalah. The surety's/transferee's liability automatically ceases (and the pledge is released) if the Salam contract is rescinded.","examExplanation":"Late-delivery penalty: permitted as a self-imposed deterrent, but proceeds must go to CHARITY, not bank income (AAOIFI clause 5/7); insolvent (non-wilful) sellers get a delivery extension instead of a penalty. Security: permissible per the Prophet's own precedent (grain purchase pledged with an iron breastplate) - covers both Rihn (pledge) and Kafeel (personal surety). On default, the bank can sell the pledge to buy replacement goods, but recovery from the pledge proceeds is capped at the ORIGINAL advance price (no increase) since the advance is a debt claim. A personal surety becomes liable for delivery on the seller's default; only the seller can authorize revocation (not the surety); liability can shift to a transferee via Hawalah with buyer consent; surety/transferee liability ceases automatically if the contract is rescinded.","keyPoints":["A late-delivery penalty in Salam must go to charity, not bank income (AAOIFI Standard clause 5/7 explicitly disallows the bank keeping such penalties) - it exists purely as a deterrent against wilful default.","A seller who fails to deliver due to genuine insolvency (not wilful default) should be granted a delivery extension rather than penalized.","Security/pledge/personal surety in Salam is permissible, grounded in the Prophet's (pbuh) own precedent of pledging his iron breastplate for a grain purchase.","On seller default, the bank can sell the pledged security to purchase replacement goods, but recovery from the proceeds is capped at the original advance price paid (since the advance is treated as a debt, not subject to increase).","A personal surety becomes liable to deliver the goods if the original seller defaults; only the seller (not the surety) can authorize contract revocation; liability can be shifted via Hawalah with the buyer's consent, and automatically ceases if the contract is rescinded."],"definitions":[{"term":"Kafeel","definition":"A personal surety in a Salam contract, who becomes liable to deliver the goods if the original seller fails to perform."},{"term":"Rihn","definition":"A pledge given as security in a Salam contract, which the bank may sell in the event of the seller's default to recover its advance payment or fund replacement goods."}],"conditions":["Late-delivery penalty proceeds in Salam must be directed to charity, not retained as bank income.","Recovery from a pledge's sale proceeds, on seller default, is capped at the original advance price paid - not subject to any increase.","Only the original seller (not a surety) can authorize revocation of the Salam contract."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's Pledge for a Grain Purchase","kind":"textbook","text":"Imam Bukhari reports the Hadith of the Prophet (pbuh) borrowing grain from a Jew against the pledge of his iron breastplate - cited as the foundational precedent establishing the permissibility of taking security/pledges (and, per Ibn Hajar's interpretation, personal sureties) in Salam transactions."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume a late-delivery penalty in Salam functions like a conventional late fee that boosts bank revenue. It does not - AAOIFI explicitly requires such penalty proceeds go to charity, not the bank's income."],"distinctions":["Wilful default (penalty applicable, charity-bound) vs genuine insolvency-driven nonperformance (extension granted, no penalty)."],"relatedConcepts":["Hawalah","Charity Account","Force Majeure"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.6-10.5","pages":[249,250]}}]},{"id":"t10.6","section":"10.6","title":"Disposing of the Goods Purchased on Salam","pages":[250,252],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam","parallel-salam","wakalah","wad"],"intuition":"A bank doesn’t want 5000 tons of wheat. How can it sell Salam goods — and can it sell them before delivery?","simpleExplanation":"The majority say Salam goods cannot be resold before delivery (a weak Hadith); Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn al-Qayyim, Ibn Abbas, Ahmad and Malikis allow it (to the seller only at the same or lower price; not foodstuff). The author finds the permissive view logical, supporting Parallel Salam, but advises not delivering before possession to be safe; negotiable Salam certificates are not yet accepted. Options: Parallel Salam (independent, never with the original seller or his owned entity), agency (separate agreement), a promise from a third party (with HJ; no prepayment needed), or selling after delivery. Selling back to the seller is buy-back.","academicExplanation":["The majority hold the Salam buyer may not dispose of goods before receipt — cannot resell even at cost, transfer, or use as partnership capital — relying on “Whoever makes Salam should not transfer it to others” (Abu Daud, Ibn Majah), which Ibn Hajar rates weak. Salam being an exception, the same basis permits Parallel Salam. Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn al-Qayyim: no problem exchanging before possession — to a third party at any price; to the seller at the same or lower price; Ibn Abbas, Imam Ahmad and Malikis agree, but not for foodstuff. Contemporary: Nazih Hammad permits; many others prohibit. The author: the permissive view is logical (no genuine text prohibits; price risk passes at contract), enabling Parallel Salam and Salam Sukuk; negotiable Salam certificates are not yet accepted by the majority; to be safe, no delivery before possession, though selling unidentified units out of inventory could be acceptable.","10.6.1 Alternatives: (i) Parallel Salam; (ii) agency with a third party or the customer; (iii) sale in the open market via a promise from a third party or after delivery. Goods cannot be sold back to the Salam seller — Parallel Salam with the original seller or an entity he owns is buy-back. After settlement, a separate Murabaha/Musawamah with the same client was allowed by the State Bank of Pakistan (a bank sold carpets to the Salam seller for export) but most scholars disagreed, so the bank shifted to appointing the client as export agent.","Promise: a binding promise from a third party to buy at a stipulated price; breach → actual loss; or wait and sell for cash/deferred (inventory). Agency: separate and independent from Salam; a target price may be set; a higher price obtained may be given to the agent. Parallel Salam: same conditions and specifications without dependency; delivery date may be the same but not earlier; the injured party cannot relate the first seller’s breach to the parallel buyer; two separate contracts (AAOIFI 6). Promise vs Parallel Salam: in a promise, the promisor need not prepay — its advantage."],"examExplanation":"Resale before delivery: majority no (weak Hadith); Ibn Taymiyah et al. yes (not foodstuff; to seller at ≤ price). Disposal: Parallel Salam (independent; not with original seller/affiliates; date not earlier), agency (separate), third-party promise with HJ (no prepayment), or sell after delivery. No sale back to seller.","keyPoints":["Parallel Salam delivery date cannot precede the original.","SBP allowed a separate post-settlement Murabaha; most scholars disagreed.","Promise avoids requiring the ultimate buyer to prepay."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Carpets sold back to the Salam seller","kind":"textbook","text":"A Pakistani bank sold carpets bought on Salam to the same seller the day after delivery for export. Since most scholars objected, it switched to appointing the client as its agent to export on the bank’s behalf (p. 251)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Parallel Salam may be made with the original seller or his company.","right":"That is buy-back; Parallel Salam must be with an independent third party (p. 251)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.10","t4.2.7","t8.8.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"With whom can a bank NOT enter a Parallel Salam to dispose of Salam goods?","options":["A third-party trader","The original Salam seller or an entity owned by him","A mill","An exporter"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 251."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.6-10.6.1","sectionTitle":"Disposing of the Goods Purchased on Salam / Alternatives for Marketing Salam Goods","title":"Disposing of Salam Goods: Parallel Salam and Marketing Alternatives","covers":["t10.6"],"overview":"Covers the debate on reselling Salam goods before delivery, the resulting permissibility of Parallel Salam, and the practical alternatives banks use to market goods received under Salam.","simpleExplanation":"Can a bank sell Salam goods it bought before actually receiving them? Most classical jurists said no, but some (like Ibn Taymiyah) disagreed, and this more flexible view is what makes 'Parallel Salam' - a second, independent Salam contract where the bank turns around and sells the same type of goods to someone else - possible in modern practice. The one hard rule: you can never sell the goods back to the original Salam seller (that would just be a disguised buy-back), and the bank can't just pass its own losses onto whoever it deals with next - the two contracts must stay completely separate.","academicExplanation":"The majority of jurists historically held that the Salam purchaser has no ownership/disposal rights over the goods until actual receipt, relying on a Hadith ('Whoever makes Salam should not transfer it to others') - though this Hadith is flagged as weak by Hafiz Ibn Hajar, undermining its use as a ruling basis. Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn al-Qayyim, Companion Ibn Abbas, Imam Ahmad, and the Maliki school took a more permissive view: exchanging the Salam subject matter before taking possession is permissible - if resold to a third party, at any price (same, higher, or lower); if resold back to the original seller, only at the same or a lower price (never higher) - though these scholars still disapprove reselling before possession if the subject is foodstuff. Contemporary scholars are divided; Shaikh Nazih Hammad and others maintain permissibility (no genuine text prohibits it), which the author endorses as the more logical position, partly BECAUSE it enables Parallel Salam and Salam-based Sukuk/certificates - crucial for Islamic banks' functioning. Transfer of ownership (and at least price risk) to the buyer is treated as occurring as soon as the Salam agreement is executed; otherwise, Parallel Salam's legality would become doubtful. Negotiable Salam certificates remain unresolved - most contemporary scholars have not accepted them; the safer practice is to avoid actual/constructive delivery of Salam goods before taking possession, though selling identical inventory units without identifying particular units may be acceptable. Marketing alternatives for Islamic banks: (i) Parallel Salam - a second, independent Salam contract as seller, on the same specifications/date, NOT tied to or contingent on the first contract; (ii) an agency contract with a third party or the original seller (customer) to sell the goods, kept separate/independent from the Salam agreement, with an indicated price (any higher price the agent achieves can be passed to the agent as extra benefit); (iii) direct sale in the open market by the bank itself, either waiting to receive the commodity and selling for cash/deferred payment, or via a prior binding promise from a third party (secured with Hamish Jiddiyah recoverable for actual loss on breach). The one absolute rule: Parallel Salam CANNOT be entered into with the original seller (even via a separate legal entity owned by that seller) - this would constitute prohibited buy-back. A noted deviation: after Salam settlement (ownership/risk transferred to the bank), a wholly separate Murabaha or Musawamah deal with the same client is permissible (State Bank of Pakistan allowed this) - though the majority of Shari'ah scholars were uncomfortable with this in practice (illustrated by a Pakistani bank reselling carpets to the original Salam seller the next day for export, which most scholars did not accept, leading the bank to switch to appointing the client as export agent instead).","examExplanation":"Majority classical view: no resale of Salam goods before receipt (based on a Hadith later flagged as weak). Minority/permissive view (Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn al-Qayyim, Maliki school, endorsed by the author): resale before receipt IS allowed - to a third party at any price, but to the original seller only at the same/lower price (never higher); still disapproved for foodstuff before possession. This permissive view underlies Parallel Salam and Salam Sukuk. Marketing alternatives: (1) Parallel Salam (bank as seller, separate independent contract, same specs/date, NEVER with the original seller - that's prohibited buy-back); (2) agency contract (third party or original seller sells on bank's behalf, kept independent from the Salam agreement); (3) direct market sale (wait and sell, or secure a binding third-party promise with Hamish Jiddiyah).","keyPoints":["The Hadith cited by the majority view against pre-possession resale ('Whoever makes Salam should not transfer it to others') is flagged as a weak tradition by Hafiz Ibn Hajar, undermining its authority as a ruling basis.","The more permissive view (Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn al-Qayyim, Maliki school) allows reselling Salam goods before possession to a third party at any price, but to the original seller only at the same or a lower price (never higher).","Parallel Salam can NEVER be entered into with the original seller (even through a separate legal entity owned by that seller) - this constitutes prohibited buy-back.","The two contracts in Parallel Salam (the original purchase, and the resale) must remain completely independent - one cannot be made contingent on the other, and a default in one does not excuse performance in the other.","Three main marketing alternatives for Salam goods: Parallel Salam (independent resale contract), an agency arrangement (third party or original seller sells on the bank's behalf), or direct sale in the open market (waiting to receive goods, optionally backed by a binding third-party promise secured with Hamish Jiddiyah).","Negotiable Salam certificates (freely tradable Salam Sukuk) remain unresolved among contemporary scholars - most have not accepted full negotiability."],"definitions":[{"term":"Parallel Salam","definition":"A second, independent Salam contract in which the bank (having bought goods under an original Salam contract) now acts as seller, selling the same type of goods on similar specifications - never with the original seller, and never contractually tied to the first contract."}],"conditions":["Parallel Salam cannot be entered into with the original seller in the first Salam contract, even through a separate legal entity owned by that seller.","The original Salam contract and any Parallel Salam or agency contract must remain independent - performance of one cannot be made contingent on the other.","If Salam goods are resold to the original seller (where a more permissive view is followed), the resale price cannot exceed the original price."],"principles":["Transfer of ownership (and at least price risk) to the Salam buyer is treated as occurring as soon as the Salam agreement is executed - this is what legally underpins the permissibility of Parallel Salam."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Pakistani Carpet Bank Example","kind":"textbook","text":"One Islamic bank in Pakistan had been selling carpets purchased through Salam back to the original Salam seller the day after Salam culmination, so the seller could export them under an existing L/C. Because the majority of Shari'ah scholars were uncomfortable with this arrangement (it risked resembling buy-back), the bank shifted to appointing the client as its export agent instead."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think any resale to the original seller is automatically prohibited buy-back. Under the more permissive scholarly view the book endorses, resale to the original seller IS possible, but only at the same price or a lower price - never a higher price, which would recreate the profit-on-debt structure buy-back is designed to prevent."],"distinctions":["Parallel Salam with an independent third party (permitted) vs a 'Parallel Salam' arrangement with the original seller or their affiliated entity (prohibited buy-back)."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al 'Inah","Hamish Jiddiyah","Salam Sukuk"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6-10.6.1","pages":[250,251,252]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Original Salam (Bank as Buyer)","description":"The bank pays the full price in advance to a seller (e.g. a farmer or trader) under an original Salam contract, for a fungible commodity of specified quality and quantity to be delivered on a future date."},{"step":"Second, Independent Salam Contract (Bank as Seller)","description":"Relying on the more permissive scholarly view (Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn al-Qayyim, the Maliki school) that transfer of ownership and price risk passes to the buyer as soon as the Salam agreement is executed, the bank enters a separate Parallel Salam contract as seller, agreeing to deliver goods of similar specification to another buyer."},{"step":"The Two Contracts Kept Independent","description":"The original Salam purchase and the Parallel Salam sale remain two legally separate contracts - neither is made contingent on the other, and default or rescission in one does not excuse performance of the other."},{"step":"Original Seller Excluded from the Parallel Contract","description":"Parallel Salam can never be entered into with the original seller of the first contract, even through a separate legal entity that seller owns - doing so would amount to prohibited buy-back (Bai' al 'Inah)."},{"step":"Delivery from the Original Seller","description":"On the agreed date, the original seller delivers the commodity to the bank; ownership and risk transfer to the bank upon actual or constructive possession."},{"step":"Bank Delivers Under the Parallel Salam","description":"The bank then delivers the commodity to its own Parallel Salam buyer, fulfilling its separate obligation under the second contract."},{"step":"Alternative Marketing Routes","description":"Where Parallel Salam is not used, the bank may instead dispose of the goods via an agency contract (a third party or the original seller sells on the bank's behalf, under an agreement kept separate from the Salam contract) or by direct sale in the open market once delivery is taken."}]}],"subsections":[{"number":"10.6.1","title":"Alternatives for Marketing Salam Goods","page":251,"points":["Parallel Salam","Agency","Promise to purchase","Sell after delivery"]}],"debate":[{"issue":"Can Salam goods be resold before taking delivery?","criticism":"Majority: no — relying on the Hadith “Whoever makes Salam should not transfer it to others.”","response":"That Hadith is weak (Ibn Hajar). Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn al-Qayyim, Ibn Abbas, Ahmad and Malikis permit it (except foodstuff; to the seller only at same or lower price). The author finds this logical since price risk passes at contract and it underpins Parallel Salam.","alternative":"Negotiable Salam certificates are not accepted by most contemporary scholars.","takeaway":"Use Parallel Salam, promise or agency; avoid physical/constructive delivery before possession."}]},{"id":"t10.7","section":"10.7","title":"Salam — Post-Execution Scenarios","pages":[252,254],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam"],"intuition":"Delivery day: goods arrive as agreed, don’t arrive, or arrive inferior. What can the bank do in each case?","simpleExplanation":"(1) Supply as contracted — the bank disposes as planned. (2) Failure to supply — wait, cancel and recover the price, or accept a replacement; the bank may ask the seller to buy from the market, or sell the pledge and buy the goods; cash recovery is limited to the advance. (3) Inferior goods — refuse and insist on supply or refund; accept at a discount; substitute (only if absolutely out of stock, fungible, different genus, value ≤ original); or accept part and refund the rest. Genuinely insolvent sellers are treated as insolvent debtors.","academicExplanation":["10.7.1 Supply as per contract: transaction culminates; the bank disposes of goods.","10.7.2 Failure: options (AAOIFI 5/8) — wait; cancel and recover the price; agree a replacement. The bank may ask the client to acquire the goods from the market; failing that, sell the collateral, buy the commodity and return any remainder; if insufficient, ask the customer to make good the deficit. If cash is taken instead of goods, only the advance price is recoverable; the price is a debt refunded without increase or decrease regardless of value change.","10.7.3 Inferior goods: disputes may be referred to an expert institution (clause in the agreement). The bank need not accept inferior goods; may accept, even at a discount; may adjust for superior quality or extra quantity (AAOIFI 5/3). Solutions: refuse and insist or take back the price (not a different amount); substitute if the item is absolutely out of stock — the new commodity fungible, value not more than the Salam commodity, not the same genus (wheat → cotton, not corn), at mutually decided market price; if available, the seller must buy it at any market price, and any new bank facility must be separate. Partial supply: accept the quantity and revise or refund the balance. An insolvent seller is treated as an insolvent debtor."],"examExplanation":"Scenarios: as contracted; failure (wait, cancel & refund price, replace; buy from market via pledge); inferior (refuse/refund, discount, substitute under strict conditions, partial acceptance). Cash recovery = advance only.","keyPoints":["Substitution only if absolutely out of stock.","Any new financing to the seller must be a separate transaction.","An insolvent Salam seller is treated like an insolvent debtor."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Seller cannot deliver","kind":"textbook","text":"A Salam seller fails to deliver 100 tons of wheat. The bank may wait, cancel and take back its advance (no more, no less, whatever wheat prices have done), or sell the pledge, buy wheat in the market and return any surplus to the seller (pp. 252–254)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"On a failed Salam the bank may recover the market value of the goods in cash.","right":"It may buy the goods from the pledge proceeds, but if it takes cash it can recover only the price it paid — no increase for changes in value (p. 253)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.4.4","t10.10.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Wheat under Salam is absolutely unavailable. Which substitute is permissible under the rules cited?","options":["Corn","Cotton of value not exceeding the wheat’s value","The seller’s animals","Wheat of another variety at a higher price"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Different genus, fungible, value ≤ original (p. 254)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.7","sectionTitle":"Salam - Post Execution Scenarios","title":"Salam Post-Execution Scenarios: Successful Delivery, Default, and Inferior Goods","covers":["t10.7"],"overview":"Covers the three outcomes after a Salam contract's delivery date arrives: smooth delivery, seller default/failure to supply, and delivery of inferior-quality goods, with the bank's options in each case.","simpleExplanation":"Once the delivery date arrives, three things can happen: the seller delivers exactly as promised (best case, bank proceeds to sell as planned); the seller fails to deliver at all (bank can wait, cancel and get its money back, or agree to a substitute); or the seller delivers goods that don't meet the agreed quality (bank can refuse and demand a proper substitute, accept at a discount, or split the difference if only part of the order is deliverable).","academicExplanation":"Scenario 1 - Supply as per contract: the seller delivers the stipulated commodity at the agreed time/place, the bank takes delivery, and disposes of it per plan. Scenario 2 - Failure in supply: the buyer has three options per AAOIFI clause 5/8: wait until the commodity becomes available, cancel the contract and recover the paid price, or agree to a mutually consented replacement subject to relevant rules. Practically, the bank may ask the client to acquire the goods (or part) from the market for supply; if the customer cannot, the bank sells any pledge/collateral, uses the proceeds to purchase the commodity from the market, and returns any remainder to the customer (or, if insufficient, asks the customer to make good the deficit). If the bank instead opts for a cash refund rather than replacement goods, it can only recover the amount originally advanced (since this is a debt in the seller's hands for the full deferral period) - no increase or decrease, regardless of the commodity's relative value change. Scenario 3 - Supply of inferior goods: forces the bank to either accept the inferior goods or rescind; disputes on quality can be adjudicated by an expert institution (a clause can be inserted for this at contract formation); the bank is not obliged to accept inferior goods but may agree to acceptance (possibly at a discount) or adjust for superior quality/additional quantity received elsewhere. Three solutions: (1) refuse and insist on the agreed goods (per the failure-in-supply procedure) or reclaim the price paid - this is permissible only if it doesn't involve returning a different price than originally paid; (2) if the agreed item is genuinely out of stock in the accessible market, ask the seller to supply a substitute (fungible, not the same genus as the original, value not exceeding the original Salam commodity's value; if the seller needs additional finance to procure the substitute, this must be treated as a wholly separate transaction from the original Salam - the two contracts can never be tied together); (3) for partial delivery, the bank accepts the available quantity and either revises the purchase order for the remainder or claims a refund of the corresponding balance of price paid. If the seller becomes genuinely insolvent and incapable of honoring the commitment, he is treated like any other insolvent debtor.","examExplanation":"Post-execution outcomes: (1) Successful delivery - straightforward, bank disposes per plan. (2) Failure to supply - buyer's 3 options: wait, cancel + recover paid price, or mutually agreed replacement; if the bank takes cash instead of goods, recovery is capped at the ORIGINAL advance amount (no increase/decrease) since it's a debt claim. (3) Inferior goods - bank can refuse (demand agreed goods or refund), accept a substitute (only if agreed item is genuinely out-of-stock market-wide; substitute must be fungible, different genus, value <= original), or accept partial delivery (revise order or get partial refund for the shortfall). New financing extended to help a seller procure substitute goods must be a SEPARATE transaction from the original Salam - the two can never be contractually tied together. Insolvent sellers are treated as ordinary insolvent debtors.","keyPoints":["On failure to supply, the buyer has three options: wait for availability, cancel and recover the paid price, or agree to a mutually consented replacement.","If the bank opts for a cash refund rather than replacement goods after a failed delivery, recovery is strictly capped at the original advance amount - no increase or decrease, since the advance is treated as a debt owed by the seller.","For inferior-quality goods, the bank is never obliged to accept them, but may choose to (possibly at a discount) or seek a substitute if the agreed item is genuinely out of stock market-wide.","A substitute commodity must be fungible, of a different genus than the original, and valued no more than the original Salam commodity - and any new financing to help the seller procure a substitute must be structured as a wholly separate transaction, never contractually tied to the original Salam.","For partial delivery, the bank accepts the available quantity and either revises the order for the shortfall or claims a proportional refund; a genuinely insolvent seller is treated like any other insolvent debtor."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Cash recovery after a failed Salam delivery is limited to the exact original advance amount, with no increase or decrease.","A substitute commodity for inferior/unavailable goods must be fungible, of a different genus from the original, and its value must not exceed that of the original Salam commodity.","Any new financing extended to help a seller procure a substitute commodity must be structured as a transaction wholly separate from, and never contractually tied to, the original Salam contract."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think a bank can simply demand its money back PLUS compensation if a Salam seller defaults. In fact, cash recovery is strictly capped at the original advance amount - the debt cannot be increased, consistent with the general Salam rule against re-pricing."],"distinctions":["Genuine market-wide unavailability (substitute goods with mutual consent are permitted) vs mere seller default despite availability (seller remains obligated to procure and deliver the agreed goods regardless of market price)."],"relatedConcepts":["Force Majeure","Insolvency","Fungible (Mithli) Goods"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7","pages":[252,253,254]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"10.7.1","title":"Supply of Goods as Per Contract","page":252,"points":["Normal culmination"]},{"number":"10.7.2","title":"Failure in Supply of Goods","page":253,"points":["Wait, cancel, replace","Sell pledge and buy from market"]},{"number":"10.7.3","title":"Supply of Inferior Goods","page":253,"points":["Refuse, discount, substitute, partial"]}]},{"id":"t10.8","section":"10.8","title":"Salam-Based Securitisation — Salam Certificates/Sukuk","pages":[254,255],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["salam","sukuk"],"intuition":"Can a government raise funds by selling future oil or aluminium?","simpleExplanation":"Governments with natural resources can issue Salam certificates for future delivery of products (e.g. oil to finance refining capacity), fully paid upfront. Holders may take delivery or sell via Parallel Salam; actual delivery, not paper settlement, is binding. Returns are not guaranteed; investors bear counterparty and market risk. Example: Bahrain’s aluminium Salam Sukuk via Bahrain Islamic Bank.","academicExplanation":["Salam certificates (forward contracts) can be issued against future delivery of a commodity, product or service — e.g. governments with petroleum, copper or iron; an oil producer expanding refining may sell oil products via Salam instead of interest borrowing. The purchaser may hold to delivery, sell via Parallel Salam before delivery at market price, or issue Salam Sukuk (SC) against the price paid. An SC may change hands; actual delivery and receipt, not paper settlement, are binding on the issuer or final holder; the issuer’s obligation equals what the real sector pays on the due date. SCs tie finance, production and sale; income is not guaranteed though it can be quasi-fixed; price risk belongs to investors, who bear counterparty risk (non-delivery) and market risk (inability to market or lower price), mitigable by structure. Bahrain: aluminium as underlying; the government sells aluminium forward; Bahrain Islamic Bank buys and represents other banks; the government issues Salam certificates undertaking to supply aluminium at a specified date."],"examExplanation":"Salam Sukuk: prepaid certificates for future delivery of commodities (oil, aluminium); actual delivery binding; not guaranteed; counterparty and market risk; Bahrain aluminium example. (Box 8.3: Salam Sukuk non-tradable.)","keyPoints":["Paper settlement not permitted."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Oil refinery expansion","kind":"textbook","text":"An oil-producing country could sell oil products by Salam instead of borrowing on interest, using the advance price to expand refining, with Salam certificates held by investors (p. 254)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Salam certificates can be traded freely like shares.","right":"The majority of contemporary scholars have not accepted negotiable Salam certificates; they represent a debt of goods (pp. 251, 254)."}],"relatedTopics":["t15.3.5","t8.8.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which commodity underlies Bahrain’s Salam Sukuk cited in the chapter?","options":["Oil","Aluminium","Wheat","Gold"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 255."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.8","sectionTitle":"Salam-Based Securitization - Salam Certificates/Sukuk","title":"Salam-Based Securitization: Salam Certificates/Sukuk","covers":["t10.8"],"overview":"Explains how Salam certificates let investors fund future commodity delivery (especially by resource-rich governments) and the risks Salam Sukuk holders bear.","simpleExplanation":"A government or company that will produce a commodity in the future (like oil or aluminum) can sell that future output in advance through Salam certificates, raising money now instead of borrowing at interest. Investors who buy these certificates are essentially betting on getting real delivery of that commodity later - so unlike a fixed-return bond, their income depends on what happens with that specific commodity, and they bear real risk if the seller can't deliver or if the market price drops.","academicExplanation":"Salam certificates, representing a forward contract for future delivery of a commodity, product, or service, can be issued especially by countries with large public sectors or substantial natural resource deposits (e.g. petroleum, copper, iron). Investors pay fully on the spot and receive purchase certificates in return - e.g. an oil-producing country wanting to expand refining capacity could sell oil products via Salam instead of interest-based borrowing, using the advance price for the expansion. The Salam purchaser can hold the contract to receive the shipment, sell the goods via Parallel Salam to another investor before the delivery date at whatever market price prevails, or issue Salam Sukuk/certificates (SCs) against the price paid for future delivery - which can change hands between contract inception and maturity, though actual delivery/receipt (not mere paper settlement) remains binding on the issuer or final certificate holder. The issuer's obligation to the investor mirrors exactly what the real-sector market pays on the payment due date - Salam certificates are geared to a specific commodity/project, so investor income shares in that commodity/project's outcome (not guaranteed, though it can be quasi-fixed), and because Salam certificates tie finance, production, and sale into one contract, price-change risk on the Salam subject matter belongs to the investors (Salam purchasers) themselves. Investors bear both counterparty risk (seller's possible inability to deliver) and market risk (buyer's possible inability to market goods favorably at delivery), both mitigable through deal structuring. A concrete example: in Bahrain, aluminium serves as the underlying asset for Salam Sukuk - the Bahrain government sells aluminium forward, Bahrain Islamic Bank (BIB) purchases it (representing other participating banks), and as consideration for the advance payment, the Bahrain government issues Salam certificates undertaking to supply a specified aluminium quantity at a specified future date.","examExplanation":"Salam certificates = forward-delivery investment instruments, especially useful for resource-rich governments (oil, copper, aluminium) to raise funds without interest-based borrowing. Purchaser options: hold to maturity, sell via Parallel Salam before delivery, or trade Salam Sukuk (SCs) - but actual delivery is always ultimately binding, not mere paper settlement. Investor income is tied to the specific underlying commodity/project (not guaranteed, though quasi-fixed is possible); investors bear counterparty risk (seller can't deliver) and market risk (buyer can't market goods favorably). Real example: Bahrain aluminium Salam Sukuk, with Bahrain Islamic Bank purchasing on behalf of participating banks.","keyPoints":["Salam certificates let resource-rich governments/companies raise funds for a specific future commodity delivery, as an interest-free alternative to conventional borrowing.","Salam certificate (SC) holders can hold to maturity, resell via Parallel Salam before delivery, or trade the certificate itself - but actual physical delivery, not paper settlement, remains ultimately binding on the issuer/final holder.","Salam certificate income is tied to the specific underlying commodity/project's outcome (not guaranteed, though it can be quasi-fixed) - investors bear real counterparty risk (seller default) and market risk (unfavorable resale conditions).","The Bahrain government's aluminium Salam Sukuk, purchased by Bahrain Islamic Bank on behalf of participating banks, is cited as a real-world example of Salam-based government securitization."],"definitions":[{"term":"Salam Certificate (SC)","definition":"A certificate representing a Salam-based forward contract for future delivery of a commodity, product, or service, which can change hands between contract inception and maturity, but whose ultimate obligation is real physical delivery, not paper settlement."}],"conditions":["Actual delivery and receipt of the underlying commodity - not merely paper settlement - remains binding on the issuer or the final holder of a Salam certificate."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bahrain Aluminium Salam Sukuk","kind":"textbook","text":"The Bahrain government sells aluminium forward via Salam; Bahrain Islamic Bank (BIB) purchases the aluminium, representing other participating banks. As consideration for the advance payment, the government issues Salam certificates and undertakes to supply a specified amount of aluminium on a specified future date."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Salam Sukuk offer a guaranteed fixed return like a conventional bond. The book is explicit that returns are not guaranteed (though they can be quasi-fixed) because investor income is genuinely tied to the underlying commodity/project's outcome."],"distinctions":["A conventional bond (fixed interest, unrelated to underlying business) vs a Salam certificate (income tied to a specific commodity/project, real counterparty and market risk borne by investors)."],"relatedConcepts":["Parallel Salam","Sukuk","Counterparty Risk"],"examRelevance":"detailed","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[254,255]}}]},{"id":"t10.9","section":"10.9","title":"Summary of Salam Rules","pages":[255,256],"tier":"revision","concepts":["salam"],"intuition":"A one-page checklist for any Salam contract.","simpleExplanation":"Full price on the spot; object is a debt so price can’t be delayed; price may be money, service or usufruct but not an existing debt; fungible, describable goods usually in the market; not currencies or specific items; deliverable; time and place specified; instalments allowed; early delivery if no inconvenience; no cash settlement — actual delivery; seller need not be producer; binding; banks can’t offset receivables; itemised value for multiple items; bank must take delivery when offered; parallel contracts or promises independent.","academicExplanation":["The seller supplies specific goods at a future date for a price fully paid on the spot; since the object is a debt, payment cannot be delayed (debt for debt). Capital is money, service or usufruct; no debt of the buyer against the seller or third party as capital. Objects fungible and describable (weight, size, volume, colour, quality, grade); negligible variation tolerated; usually in markets but not in the seller’s possession; agricultural, industrial, natural goods or well-defined services. Not money/currencies, specific things (“this car”), land, buildings, trees or subjectively valued items. Deliverability ensured; place and time specified; instalments permissible; early delivery if no inconvenience. No cash settlement — actual physical delivery; on rescission the actual price is recovered. Seller need not be owner or producer. Conclusive and binding; alterable only by consent. Banks should not offset receivables for the Salam price — a Salam against a loan or part cash/part loan is effective only to the extent of cash. For multiple items, specify each item’s value, delivery period and place. If the seller offers delivery on the due date, the bank must take it or the seller is absolved; the bank may refuse only for non-conformity or early offer (optional). Parallel contracts or promises are separate and independent."],"examExplanation":"Checklist: spot full price; no debt capital; fungible, specified, available goods; no currency/specific items; fixed time/place; actual delivery; seller needn’t produce; binding; no offset; bank must accept conforming delivery; parallel contracts independent.","keyPoints":["Salam against part loan is effective only for the cash part.","Refusing conforming on-time delivery absolves the seller.","If the seller offers delivery on the due date, the bank must take it or he is absolved; it may refuse only non-conforming goods or early delivery.","For multiple items, state the value and delivery period of each so partial performance can be settled."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bank must take conforming delivery","kind":"textbook","text":"On the due date the seller offers wheat exactly as specified. If the bank refuses because prices have fallen, the seller is absolved: the bank may refuse only non-conforming goods or early delivery that inconveniences it (pp. 255–256)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.4.1","t10.4.2","t10.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A bank pays half the Salam price in cash and sets off the other half against a loan owed by the seller. The contract is effective:","options":["Fully","Only to the extent of the cash payment","Not at all","Only if interest-free"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 255."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.9-10.10.1","sectionTitle":"Summary of Salam Rules / Salam as a Financing Technique by Banks / Risks in Salam and their Management","title":"Salam as a Bank Financing Technique: Practice, Comparison with Murabaha, and Risk Management","covers":["t10.9","t10.10","t10.10.1"],"overview":"Summarizes the consolidated Salam rules, explains why banks have historically underused Salam relative to Murabaha, contrasts the two modes directly, and catalogs the risks banks face in Salam financing along with mitigants.","simpleExplanation":"Even though Salam has deep religious sanction, Islamic banks haven't used it much in practice, mainly because it's operationally harder than Murabaha - banks have to pay the full price upfront, then actually deal with receiving, storing, and marketing physical goods, which Murabaha-through-an-agent avoids. But Salam has real advantages for farmers and small businesses, and banks that build up commodity expertise can make good profits from it. Salam does carry extra risks beyond Murabaha - price risk, delivery risk, storage costs - but each has practical mitigation tools, like taking security, using Parallel Salam, or getting binding purchase promises from buyers.","academicExplanation":"The consolidated Salam rules (10.9) reiterate: the Salam price cannot be delayed (else it becomes a prohibited debt-for-debt); capital is normally money but can be a service/usufruct; a buyer's debt against the seller (or a third party) cannot serve as capital; the object of exchange must be fungible and precisely describable; Salam cannot occur in money/currencies or in specifically identified/subjectively-valued items; delivery time/place must be specified (instalments allowed); goods remain at seller's risk before delivery; no cash settlement is allowed (actual physical delivery is mandatory, though a rescinded contract requires refunding the exact price paid); the seller need not be the producer; the contract is binding, alterable only by mutual consent; banks should not offset Salam price against their own receivables (a Salam sale partly against cash and partly against a loan is only effective to the cash portion); and if goods are refused for genuine specification nonconformity or premature offering, refusal is at the bank's discretion. Despite about a quarter-century of Islamic banking, Salam has been underused relative to Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, mainly because Salam offers no practical mark-up advantage over Murabaha while requiring full upfront cash payment and actual physical dealing in future goods (receipt, quality assessment, storage, marketing) - problems banks avoid via MPO's client-as-agent structure. Yet Salam offers real benefits for farmers/SMEs and profit potential for banks with commodity expertise; some IFIs have begun using it, including in combination with Murabaha for export financing. Box 10.2 directly contrasts Salam (deferred goods delivery, full advance price payment, defined-by-specification not particular commodity, cannot be used between items requiring simultaneous exchange like wheat-for-barley) against Murabaha (goods delivered on the spot, price can be spot or deferred, executed on particular identified commodities, can be used for spot-exchange-restricted items). Salam-specific risks (10.10.1): counterparty risk, commodity price risk, delivery/settlement risk, quality risk/low return-or-loss, asset-holding risk (storage/Takaful costs), asset replacement risk (buying from market if the original seller defaults), and fiduciary risk in Parallel Salam (the original seller's nonperformance can leave the bank exposed to its Parallel Salam counterparty). Mitigation tools (Box 10.3): precise MoU specification (time/quality/quantity) to reduce disputes; collateral/performance bonds for defective goods; charity-bound penalty clauses for late delivery; Parallel Salam and binding third-party purchase promises for price/marketing risk; purchasing only goods with good marketing potential, taking binding promises with sufficient Hamish Jiddiyah, or appointing the seller as selling agent, for marketing risk; recovering holding costs through proper market survey/feasibility study for asset-holding risk; embedding charity-bound penalty clauses for early-termination risk (Salam is binding, seller cannot unilaterally terminate); and, for Parallel Salam fiduciary risk, purchasing a similar asset from the spot market to fulfill the parallel obligation and recovering the resulting loss from the original defaulting seller.","examExplanation":"Salam underused by banks in practice (vs Murabaha) because: no mark-up advantage over Murabaha, requires full upfront cash payment, and forces banks into real physical-goods handling (receipt, quality check, storage, marketing) that Murabaha-via-agent avoids. Salam vs Murabaha (Box 10.2): Salam = deferred goods/spot price/commodity-by-specification/cannot use simultaneous-exchange items; Murabaha = spot goods/spot-or-deferred price/particular identified commodity/can use those items. Salam-specific risks: counterparty, price, delivery/settlement, quality, asset-holding, asset-replacement, and Parallel Salam fiduciary risk. Key mitigants: precise MoU specs, collateral/performance bonds, charity-bound late penalties, Parallel Salam + binding purchase promises (with Hamish Jiddiyah) for price/marketing risk, market survey for holding-cost recovery, and spot-market replacement purchase (recovering loss from the original defaulter) for Parallel Salam fiduciary risk.","keyPoints":["Salam has been historically underused by Islamic banks relative to Murabaha, mainly because it offers no mark-up advantage over Murabaha-Mu'ajjal while requiring full upfront cash payment and genuine physical dealing in future goods (receipt, quality assessment, storage, marketing).","Key Box 10.2 distinctions: in Salam, goods are delivered later and price is paid fully in advance (defined by specification, not a particular commodity, and cannot involve items requiring simultaneous exchange); in Murabaha, goods are delivered on the spot and price may be spot or deferred (executed on a particular identified commodity).","Salam-specific risks include counterparty risk, commodity price risk, delivery/settlement risk, quality risk, asset-holding risk (storage/Takaful costs), asset-replacement risk, and fiduciary risk arising from Parallel Salam.","Key mitigation tools: precise MoU specification of time/quality/quantity, collateral/performance bonds, charity-bound penalty clauses for late delivery, Parallel Salam combined with binding third-party purchase promises (backed by Hamish Jiddiyah) for price/marketing risk, and spot-market replacement purchases (with loss recovery from the original defaulter) for Parallel Salam fiduciary risk.","Banks should never offset Salam price against their own receivables from the client - a Salam sale executed partly for cash and partly against a loan is effective only to the extent of the cash portion."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["The Salam price cannot be delayed beyond the permitted short window - otherwise the sale is effectively a debt-for-debt exchange, which is prohibited.","Banks should not offset their own receivables against the Salam price; a sale partly for cash and partly against a loan is effective only to the extent of the cash portion.","Actual physical delivery is mandatory in Salam - no cash settlement is allowed, though a rescinded contract requires refunding the exact price originally paid."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 10.1 - Flow of Salam Transactions by Banks","kind":"textbook","text":"(1) The bank purchases the item from a client/seller with full prepayment of price and delivery on an agreed specified date. (2) The customer (seller) delivers the commodity at the agreed time and place. (3) The bank sells the commodity to a third party via Parallel Salam, a prior promise to purchase, appointing the original seller as selling agent, or waiting until goods are received and then selling in the market. (4) After taking delivery, the bank delivers to the third-party purchaser."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Salam's underuse reflects some Shari'ah defect in the mode. The book is explicit this is a purely practical/operational preference issue (upfront cash requirement, physical goods handling) - Salam is, in fact, the one mode expressly sanctioned by the Prophet (pbuh) himself."],"distinctions":["Salam (deferred goods, advance price, commodity by specification) vs Murabaha (spot goods, spot-or-deferred price, particular identified commodity) - the two core structural differences per Box 10.2."],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Parallel Salam","Hamish Jiddiyah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.9-10.10.1","pages":[255,256,257,258,259]},"calculations":[{"title":"Box 10.4 Case Study: The Salam Price Discount as the Bank's Implicit Return","formula":"Effective Return over Holding Period = (Locked Resale Value - Salam Capital) / Salam Capital; Annualized Return = Effective Return x (12 / Holding Period in Months)","inputs":[{"label":"Salam capital paid by Bank B to Farmer A on 1 January","value":"Rs. 100 million (for 5,000 tons of wheat deliverable 1 May)"},{"label":"Implied Salam price per ton","value":"Rs. 100 million / 5,000 tons = Rs. 20,000 per ton"},{"label":"Value locked in via B's promise arrangement with buyer C for the same wheat on 1 May","value":"Rs. 115 million, against Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money) of Rs. 15 million from C"},{"label":"Holding period (Salam contract date to delivery/resale date)","value":"1 January to 1 May = 4 months"}],"steps":["Salam capital advanced by the bank on day 1 = Rs. 100 million, for wheat priced today at a discount to what the bank expects to realize on delivery.","Value the bank has locked in for the same wheat 4 months later (via the promise-to-purchase with C) = Rs. 115 million.","Implicit discount/spread = Rs. 115 million - Rs. 100 million = Rs. 15 million earned over the 4-month Salam period.","Return over the 4-month holding period = Rs. 15 million / Rs. 100 million = 15%.","Annualized (simple) effective return = 15% x (12 months / 4 months) = 45% per annum."],"result":"The bank's Rs. 100 million Salam price is effectively 'discounted' Rs. 15 million (15%) below the Rs. 115 million value it has locked in for the same wheat 4 months later - a simple annualized effective return of about 45%.","interpretation":"This spread is exactly the economic mechanism described in Section 10.3: the farmer accepts a price today that is cheaper than what he expects the goods to fetch later, in exchange for immediate liquidity, while the bank/buyer accepts price risk and the burden of marketing the goods and is compensated by the resulting spread rather than by an interest charge. Because the wheat still has to be delivered, resold, and its price risk actually borne (Box 10.3's commodity-price and marketing risks), this 45% figure is a return on genuine trade risk, not a guaranteed, interest-like yield - had C not been secured with a binding promise backed by Hamish Jiddiyah, or had wheat prices fallen before resale, the bank's realized return could have been far lower or even negative.","generated":false,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.9-10.10.1 (Box 10.4)","pages":[259,260]}},{"title":"Practice Example - generated for learning: Annualizing the Salam Price Discount","formula":"Salam Capital = Salam Price per Unit x Quantity; Discount = (Expected Future Spot Price - Salam Price) x Quantity; Annualized Return = [(Expected Future Spot Price / Salam Price) - 1] x (12 / Months to Delivery)","inputs":[{"label":"Quantity of wheat contracted","value":"100 tons"},{"label":"Salam price agreed today (discounted)","value":"$180 per ton"},{"label":"Expected spot price at the 6-month delivery date","value":"$200 per ton"},{"label":"Delivery period","value":"6 months from contract date"}],"steps":["Total Salam capital the bank pays the farmer today = $180 x 100 tons = $18,000.","Expected value of the same 100 tons if sold at the future spot price = $200 x 100 tons = $20,000.","Implicit discount (the farmer's cost of getting cash now) = $20,000 - $18,000 = $2,000 over 6 months.","Return over the 6-month holding period, from the bank's side = $2,000 / $18,000 = 11.1%.","Annualized effective return = 11.1% x (12 / 6) = 22.2% per annum."],"result":"By paying $18,000 now instead of the $20,000 the wheat is expected to fetch in 6 months, the bank earns an implicit 11.1% return over the period - roughly 22.2% annualized - if it can resell the wheat at the expected spot price.","interpretation":"The discount below the expected future spot price is the Salam buyer's substitute for interest: it compensates the bank for paying cash today, tying up funds for 6 months, and bearing the commodity's price and marketing risk until resale (Box 10.3). It is not a contractually fixed rate - if wheat prices fall below $200/ton by delivery, or the bank cannot market the wheat promptly, its realized return will be lower than 22.2% or could turn into a loss, which is precisely what distinguishes this spread from an interest-bearing loan.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.3, 10.10","pages":[242,243,257]}}]}]},{"id":"t10.10","section":"10.10","title":"Salam as a Financing Technique by Banks (Boxes 10.1–10.8)","pages":[256,263],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam","parallel-salam","murabaha"],"intuition":"Why have banks under-used Salam — and how can it finance farmers, exporters and factories?","simpleExplanation":"Banks rarely use Salam because it has no practical advantage over Murabaha: full upfront payment, taking delivery of goods, marketing and default risks. Yet it is the only mode expressly allowed by the Prophet and suits farmers and SMEs. Box 10.1: bank buys with prepayment; seller delivers; bank sells via Parallel Salam, promise, agency or market. Box 10.2: Salam vs Murabaha. Cases: wheat with promise (Box 10.4), preshipment export (10.5), central bank refinance pool (10.6), sugar mill working capital (10.7), accounting (10.8).","academicExplanation":["Islamic banks have not generally used Salam because it offers no practical advantage over mark-up Murabaha: full cash price immediately, delivery of goods rather than money, marketing, and defaults (inferior or late goods). Practical problems — taking delivery, assessing quality, storing, disposing — are perceived relative to conventional practice and easy MPO with client as agent. Recognising the requirement of real business, risk mitigation avenues and that Salam is the only mode expressly allowed by the Prophet, banks should use it more; it benefits farmers and SMEs and can be more profitable with commodity expertise; some IFIs now use it alone or combined with Murabaha for exports.","Box 10.1: (1) bank buys from client A with full prepayment for delivery on a specified date; (2) A delivers; (3) the bank sells to C by Parallel Salam, a promise, agency of A, or market sale after receipt; (4) the bank delivers to C. Box 10.2 (Salam vs Murabaha): Salam defers delivery with spot full price; Murabaha delivers spot with spot or deferred price; Salam is by specification, Murabaha on particular commodities; Salam cannot be executed in things requiring spot exchange (wheat for barley), Murabaha can.","Box 10.4 (case study): farmer A sells 5000 tons of wheat on 1 January for Rs.100m paid spot; B takes C’s promise to buy for Rs.115m on 1 May with HJ of Rs.15m; A delivers on 1 May; C executes the sale and signs a promissory note. Box 10.5 (preshipment export): exporter with Rs.1.1m rice order; importer’s L/C in favour of B; Salam for 1000 tons at Rs.1m paid in advance; agency to export; after delivery B owns risk and reward (including cancellation or damage; Takaful borne by B); B receives L/C proceeds. Box 10.6 (central bank refinance): CB and bank create a Musharakah pool (bank’s assets: compliant stocks, Ijarah, Murabaha receivables <33%); CB invests an amount equal to Salam export finance; income shared by agreed ratio; reserves from profit above a level. Box 10.7 (working capital): sugar mill A needs working capital; bank B offers Salam with an agency agreement independent of the Salam; B indicates a target resale price of Rs.20/kg; A sells sugar of defined quality to B at Rs.19/kg for delivery on 31 December, receiving the price in advance; on physical or constructive delivery the sugar is at B’s risk; if A, as agent, sells at Rs.21, Re.1 per kg may be his service fee if the bank agrees; if prices fall to Rs.18, B bears the loss.","Box 10.8 (accounting, AAOIFI): Salam financing recognised when paid; Parallel Salam when price received; measured at cash paid or fair value in kind; provision if delivery improbable or value declines; presented as ‘Salam Financing’ and Parallel Salam as a liability; commodity received at historical cost (lower quality → market value, loss recognised); extended delivery keeps book value; cancellation without repayment → receivable; pledge shortfalls → receivable (or credit if excess); period-end at lower of cost and cash equivalent; profit/loss recognised on delivery in Parallel Salam."],"examExplanation":"Salam under-used (upfront cash, delivery/marketing risk) but Prophet-approved; suits farmers, SMEs, exporters. Box 10.1 flow; Box 10.2 vs Murabaha; cases: wheat + promise (Rs.100m→115m), preshipment export with agency, CB refinance Musharakah pool, sugar working capital (bank bears price risk), accounting.","keyPoints":["Wheat case: promise at Rs.115m with Rs.15m HJ.","Export case: bank bears risk after delivery, pays Takaful.","Sugar case: gains/losses on resale are the bank’s."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 10.4 — Wheat Salam with promise","kind":"textbook","text":"Farmer sells 5000 tons of wheat to bank B for Rs.100m paid on 1 January, delivery 1 May. C promises to buy for Rs.115m on 1 May with Rs.15m Hamish Jiddiyah. On delivery, C executes the purchase."},{"title":"Box 10.7 — Sugar mill working capital","kind":"textbook","text":"Bank buys sugar at Rs.19/kg for delivery on 31 December, paying in advance, and appoints the mill as selling agent with a target of Rs.20. If sold at Rs.21, Re.1 may be the agent’s fee; if prices fall to Rs.18, the bank bears the loss."},{"title":"Box 10.5 — rice export by Salam","kind":"textbook","text":"The bank pays Rs.1m for 1,000 tons of rice deliverable on 1 January and appoints the exporter as agent; after delivery the bank owns the rice and bears Takaful and cancellation risk, and collects Rs.1.1m under the L/C (p. 260)."},{"title":"Box 10.7 — sugar mill working capital","kind":"textbook","text":"The bank buys sugar by Salam at Rs.19/kg for 31 December delivery and appoints the mill its agent with a target price of Rs.20. Above Rs.20 the extra may be the agent’s fee; if the price falls to Rs.18, the bank bears the loss (p. 261)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Salam has no advantage for banks over Murabaha.","right":"Banks under-use it because of delivery and marketing burdens, but it is the only mode expressly allowed by the Prophet (pbuh) and suits farmers and SMEs (p. 257)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.10.1","t14.4.1","t14.4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Box 10.5 (Salam preshipment export), who bears the risk if the rice consignment is damaged after delivery to the bank?","options":["The exporter-seller","The bank as owner","The importer","The negotiating bank"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 260."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Bank buys goods from client with full prepayment for delivery on a specified date","Client (seller) delivers at the agreed time and place","Bank sells: Parallel Salam, third-party promise, client as agent, or market sale","Bank delivers to the final buyer"],"table":{"caption":"Box 10.2 — Salam vs Murabaha","head":["Aspect","Salam","Murabaha"],"rows":[["Delivery","Deferred","Spot"],["Price","Paid in full in advance","Spot or deferred"],["Subject","By specification, not a particular commodity","Particular commodities"],["Items requiring spot exchange (e.g. wheat for barley)","Not allowed","Allowed"]]},"calc":{"type":"salam-profit","note":"Reproduces Box 10.4: Salam price, promised resale price and HJ → bank’s gross margin and HJ coverage if the promisor defaults."}},{"id":"t10.10.1","section":"10.10.1","title":"Risks in Salam and their Management (Box 10.3)","pages":[258,259],"tier":"core","concepts":["salam","risk-management"],"intuition":"What can go wrong in a bank Salam and how is each risk mitigated?","simpleExplanation":"Risks: counterparty, commodity price, delivery/settlement, quality, asset holding (storage, Takaful), replacement, and fiduciary risk in Parallel Salam. Mitigation: security and liquidation to buy goods; detailed MoU; collateral and performance bonds; charity penalty for late delivery; Parallel Salam and promises for price risk; buy marketable goods with binding promises and HJ; seller as agent; factor holding costs; binding contract with charity penalty against early termination; buy spot to supply the parallel buyer and recover from the original seller.","academicExplanation":["Risks: counterparty; commodity price; delivery/settlement; quality/low return or loss; asset holding (storage, Takaful); asset replacement (buying from market); fiduciary risk in Parallel Salam. Box 10.3: (1) counterparty and delivery — the customer may default after payment → liquidate security and buy goods; disputes with multiple consignments → specify time, quality, quantity in the MoU; defective goods → collateral and performance bonds; late delivery → penalty clause to charity. (2) Price risk → Parallel Salam and third-party promise. (3) Marketing risk → buy goods with good marketing potential, binding promises with sufficient HJ; seller as agent. (4) Asset holding → recover costs in parallel transactions with market surveys and feasibility. (5) Early termination — client refunds and refuses supply → Salam is binding; penalty to charity. (6) Parallel Salam — original seller fails and parallel buyer sues → buy similar asset spot and recover loss from the original seller."],"examExplanation":"Salam risks (7) and Box 10.3 mitigations: security, detailed MoU, performance bonds, charity penalty, Parallel Salam/promise, marketable goods + HJ, agency, cost recovery, binding contract, spot purchase to fulfil parallel contract.","keyPoints":["Fiduciary risk arises in Parallel Salam."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Parallel Salam default","kind":"textbook","text":"If the original seller fails, the Parallel Salam buyer can still sue the bank; the bank buys similar goods in the spot market to deliver and recovers any loss from the original seller (Box 10.3, p. 259)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.9","t10.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which tool mitigates commodity price risk in Salam according to Box 10.3?","options":["Interest rate swap","Parallel Salam or a third-party promise to purchase","Rollover","Indexation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 258."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"Box 10.3 — Risk mitigation in Salam","head":["Risk","Mitigation"],"rows":[["Counterparty/delivery: default after prepayment","Liquidate security; buy goods from market"],["Disputes over multiple consignments","Specify time, quality, quantity per item in the MoU"],["Defective goods","Collateral and performance bonds"],["Late delivery","Penalty clause — amount to charity"],["Commodity price risk","Parallel Salam; third-party promise to purchase"],["Marketing risk","Buy marketable goods; binding promises with Hamish Jiddiyah; seller as agent"],["Asset holding cost","Recover in parallel transactions after market study"],["Early termination by seller","Binding contract; charity penalty"],["Parallel Salam: original seller fails","Buy spot to supply parallel buyer; recover loss from original seller"]]}},{"id":"t10.11","section":"10.11.1","title":"Istisna‘a (Order to Manufacture): Definition and Concept","pages":[263,264],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna"],"intuition":"How do you finance a ship, a building or a road that doesn’t exist yet?","simpleExplanation":"Istisna‘a is a sale of something to be manufactured or constructed in the future at an agreed price, legalised on Istihsan. The manufacturer supplies both material and labour (if the buyer supplies material it is Ujrah, not Istisna‘a). Because it is a sale at contract, no fresh offer and acceptance is needed on completion — unlike MPO. It is binding, and a defect-liability or maintenance period may be agreed.","academicExplanation":["Istisna‘a, like Salam, is a sale before the commodity exists; accepted because it contains no prohibition, is common practice and eases life. Zuhayli: it evolved for manual work (leather, shoes, carpentry) and now finances infrastructure — ships, aeroplanes, large machinery. As a financing mode it is legalised on Istihsan (OIC Fiqh Council Res. 65 (3/7); AAOIFI). It is an agreement culminating in a sale at an agreed price whereby the purchaser orders something to be manufactured, assembled or constructed (or caused to be) for future delivery; the manufacturer must deliver per specifications at the agreed time. Since the sale is executed at contract, no renewed offer and acceptance is needed after preparation — unlike the promise in MPO. Uses: houses, plant, projects, bridges, roads. The manufacturer arranges raw material and labour; if material is supplied by the purchaser, it is Ujrah. It is binding: the manufacturer must supply and the buyer accept and pay; a defects/maintenance period may be agreed (AAOIFI 3/1/7)."],"examExplanation":"Istisna‘a: sale of a thing to be manufactured/constructed, agreed price, future delivery; manufacturer provides material + labour; legalised by Istihsan; sale concluded at contract (no fresh offer/acceptance); buyer-supplied material → Ujrah.","keyPoints":["Istihsan (juristic approbation) is the basis.","Differs from MPO: sale at inception, not a promise.","Legalised as a financing mode on the basis of Istihsan (juristic preference)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Istisna‘a","definition":"A sale in which the purchaser orders an item to be manufactured, assembled or constructed with agreed specifications for future delivery at an agreed price; the manufacturer supplies material and labour."},{"term":"Istihsan","definition":"Juristic approbation/preference for public interest — basis for legalising Istisna‘a as a financing mode."}],"examples":[{"title":"Istisna‘a or Ujrah?","kind":"textbook","text":"A client orders 1,000 pairs of shoes; the maker supplies the leather and labour — Istisna‘a. If the client supplies the leather and pays only for the work, it is Ujrah (hire of work), not Istisna‘a (pp. 263–264)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Istisna is merely a promise, so offer and acceptance must be repeated on delivery.","right":"Istisna is a sale concluded at the contract; unlike MPO, no fresh offer and acceptance is needed when the asset is ready (p. 263)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.11.2","t5.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A customer supplies cloth and pays a tailor only for stitching. This is:","options":["Istisna‘a","Ujrah (hire of work)","Salam","Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 264."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t12"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t12","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.11-10.11.2","sectionTitle":"Istisna'a (Order to Manufacture) / Definition and Concept / Subject Matter of Istisna'a","title":"Istisna'a: Definition, Concept, and Eligible Subject Matter","covers":["t10.11","t10.11.2"],"overview":"Introduces Istisna'a as a sale contract for manufacture before the item exists, its legal basis (Istihsan/public interest), and the rules on what can and cannot be its subject matter.","simpleExplanation":"Istisna'a is a 'commission to manufacture' contract - you order something to be built or made (a building, a ship, custom machinery) and the maker is obligated to deliver it to your specifications by an agreed date. Unlike Murabaha, the sale is considered finalized right when the contract is signed, not later. The item just needs to be clearly specified (type, quality, quantity) - it doesn't have to be a specific, already-existing thing, and the manufacturer doesn't even have to personally build it themselves, as long as it meets the agreed specs when delivered.","academicExplanation":"Istisna'a, like Salam, is a special Bai' transacted before the commodity exists, accepted by Shari'ah scholars because it involves no prohibition, has long been common practice, and eases human affairs. Zuhayli notes its historical evolution from manual trades (leather, shoes, carpentry) to modern major infrastructure/industrial projects (ships, airplanes, large machinery). As a financing mode, it is legalized on the basis of Istihsan (juristic preference for public interest). Istisna'a is an agreement culminating in a sale at an agreed price, whereby the purchaser orders manufacture/assembly/construction of something to be delivered at a future date, obligating the manufacturer/builder to deliver per agreed specifications and timeline. Crucially, because the sale is executed AT THE TIME of entering the Istisna'a contract (not merely promised), the parties need not renew offer and acceptance once the subject matter is prepared - unlike MPO, which requires formal offer/acceptance when the bank takes possession. Istisna'a can finance manufacture/construction of houses, plants, projects, bridges, roads, highways, etc.; price must be fixed by mutual consent. The manufacturer normally supplies BOTH raw material and labour; if the purchaser supplies material and the manufacturer contributes only labour/skill, this is instead a contract of Ujrah (wage-for-job), not Istisna'a. Istisna'a is binding on both parties: the manufacturer must supply per agreed specs, and the buyer must accept the stipulated asset and pay as agreed; the parties may also agree a post-delivery defect/maintenance responsibility period. On subject matter: Istisna'a applies to items identified BY SPECIFICATION, not by designation - valid only for items that must be manufactured/constructed (not natural products like animals, corn, fruit). The seller need not personally manufacture the item unless the contract states so. Both unique (Qimi) and homogeneous (fungible) goods qualify, provided specifications are agreed at contract time. It is NOT permissible for the subject matter to be an existing, already-identified asset (e.g. selling a particular designated car from a factory via Istisna'a is invalid) - though an already-produced asset CAN become Istisna'a's subject matter if it is not specifically identified in the contract (only specification-identified). Istisna'a may also apply to real estate development on designated land owned by either party (or where either holds usufruct), since the contract concerns specified-to-be-built buildings, not a particular identified location per se. The contract must clearly state type, dimensions, period, and place of delivery, and the asset can be manufactured by any specific manufacturer or from any/specific materials, as agreed. The 'manufacturer' (seller) may itself subcontract production to another manufacturer, enabling banks to serve as either manufacturer/seller OR purchaser in different Istisna'a legs.","examExplanation":"Istisna'a = manufacture-to-order sale, valid before the item exists; legal basis = Istihsan (public interest). Sale is EXECUTED at contract signing (unlike MPO's later offer/acceptance requirement) - no renewed offer/acceptance needed once goods are ready. Subject matter must be identified BY SPECIFICATION (not designation/existing identified asset); cannot be natural products (animals, corn, fruit) - only manufactured/constructed items. Manufacturer supplies BOTH material and labour (if buyer supplies material and manufacturer only contributes labour, it's Ujrah, not Istisna'a); manufacturer need not personally build it. Both Qimi (unique) and Mithli (fungible) goods qualify if specifications are agreed. An EXISTING identified asset cannot be Istisna'a's subject (e.g. 'this specific car'), but an already-produced UNIDENTIFIED (specification-matching) asset CAN be.","keyPoints":["Istisna'a's legal basis is Istihsan (juristic preference for public interest), distinguishing it from Salam's basis in direct Prophetic sanction.","The Istisna'a sale is executed AT THE TIME the contract is signed - unlike MPO (Murabaha to Purchase Orderer), no renewed offer and acceptance is required once the manufactured item is ready.","Istisna'a subject matter must be identified by SPECIFICATION, not designation - a specific, already-identified existing asset (e.g. 'this particular car') cannot be Istisna'a's subject, though an already-produced but unidentified asset matching the agreed specification CAN be.","The manufacturer normally supplies both raw material and labour; if the buyer supplies materials and the manufacturer contributes only labour/skill, the contract is instead Ujrah, not Istisna'a.","Both unique (Qimi) and homogeneous (fungible) goods can be the subject of Istisna'a, unlike Salam, which is restricted to fungible (Mithli) goods only.","The manufacturer need not personally build/manufacture the item unless the contract specifically requires this - fulfilling the agreed specifications is what matters."],"definitions":[{"term":"Istisna'a","definition":"A sale contract in which a purchaser orders the manufacture, assembly, or construction of an item to be delivered at a future date per agreed specifications, with the sale itself executed at the time the contract is signed."},{"term":"Ujrah","definition":"A wage/remuneration contract for a job where the buyer supplies the materials and the manufacturer contributes only labour and skill - distinct from Istisna'a, where the manufacturer supplies both materials and labour."},{"term":"Istihsan","definition":"Juristic preference based on public interest - the legal basis cited for Istisna'a's permissibility as a financing mode."}],"conditions":["The subject matter of Istisna'a must be identified by specification (type, quality, quantity), not as an already-existing, specifically-designated asset.","The subject matter must be something requiring manufacture or construction - natural, unmanufactured products (animals, corn, fruit) are not valid Istisna'a subject matter.","The Istisna'a contract must clearly state the type, dimensions, period, and place of delivery of the asset."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often confuse Istisna'a's execution timing with MPO's. In MPO, the actual sale requires a distinct, later offer-and-acceptance once the bank takes possession; in Istisna'a, the sale is considered executed at the time the manufacturing contract itself is signed."],"distinctions":["Istisna'a (both Qimi and Mithli goods eligible, manufacturer supplies material+labour) vs Ujrah (buyer supplies material, manufacturer supplies labour only) - two distinct contract types depending on who provides materials.","An existing, specifically identified asset (invalid Istisna'a subject) vs an asset identified only by specification, even if already produced (valid Istisna'a subject)."],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Bai' Mu'ajjal","Murabaha to Purchase Orderer"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11-10.11.2","pages":[263,264,265]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Order Placement and Specification","description":"The purchaser places an order to manufacture, assemble, or construct an item not yet in existence, clearly stating its type, dimensions, quality, quantity, and the period and place of delivery, so no ignorance or dispute remains about the subject matter."},{"step":"Price Agreement","description":"The parties fix the price by mutual consent - it can be cash, tangible goods, or usufruct of an identified asset (relevant to build-operate-transfer arrangements) - and, once settled, the price cannot be unilaterally changed."},{"step":"Contract Concluded (Sale Executed)","description":"Because Istisna'a is a sale contract and not a mere promise, the sale is treated as executed the moment the Istisna'a contract is signed - unlike Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, no fresh offer and acceptance is needed later when the goods are ready."},{"step":"Manufacture of the Asset","description":"The manufacturer supplies both the raw material and the labour to produce the asset per the agreed specification (he need not personally manufacture it unless the contract requires this, and may subcontract to another manufacturer)."},{"step":"Risk Remains with the Manufacturer","description":"Before delivery, the asset - including raw materials and work in progress - remains at the manufacturer's risk; the contract becomes binding once the manufacturer starts work (either party could rescind by notice beforehand)."},{"step":"Delivery","description":"The manufacturer delivers the completed asset, conforming to the agreed specification, at the agreed time and place; possession may be physical or constructive, with a clear demarcation between handing over and taking over."},{"step":"Acceptance or Rejection","description":"The purchaser accepts the asset if it conforms to specification (there is no Khiyar al Ro'yat/option of seeing, since the item was ordered by specification); if it does not conform, he may reject it or accept it as-is, which then constitutes satisfactory performance."},{"step":"Payment of Price","description":"The purchaser pays the agreed price, which, unlike Salam, need not be paid in advance - it can be paid in instalments linked to the manufacturing/completion stages."}]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Istisna‘a","b":"Ujrah (hire of work)","text":"In Istisna‘a the manufacturer uses his own material at a fixed sale price; in Ujrah the buyer provides material and pays wages."}]},{"id":"t10.11.2","section":"10.11.2","title":"Subject Matter of Istisna‘a","pages":[264,265],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna","mithli-qimi"],"intuition":"What can be ordered through Istisna‘a?","simpleExplanation":"Only things that need manufacturing or construction, identified by specification — not natural products (animals, corn, fruit) and not an existing identified asset (“this car”). Both unique and homogeneous items qualify. The seller need not manufacture personally unless stated. Real estate on designated land (buyer’s or contractor’s) is allowed. Type, dimensions, period and place must be stated. Banks can be sellers or buyers.","academicExplanation":["Istisna‘a applies to items to be manufactured, identified by specification not designation; valid only for objects to be manufactured or constructed; the seller need not manufacture personally unless stated. The subject must be known and specified as to kind, type, quality and quantity. It is invalid for natural products (animals, corn, fruit). Unique and homogeneous assets are covered if specifications are agreed — unique items with no regular market or substitute especially. The contract is binding according to specifications. An existing identified asset cannot be the subject (a particular designated car — invalid), but an already-produced item not identified in the contract may be supplied (AAOIFI). Real estate on designated land owned by either party (or where either owns the usufruct) is allowed, as buildings are sold by specification. The contract must state type, dimensions, period and place; manufacturer or materials may be specified or open. The seller may contract with another manufacturer; so banks can be both manufacturers (sellers) and purchasers."],"examExplanation":"Istisna‘a subject: manufactured/constructed items by specification; not natural products; not identified existing asset; unique or homogeneous; real estate allowed; state type, dimensions, period, place.","keyPoints":["Contract binding by specifications, not by who manufactures.","Istisna is invalid for natural products such as animals, corn and fruit.","Real-estate Istisna on designated land is allowed because the contract is for specified buildings."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"What cannot be ordered","kind":"textbook","text":"A contract to “manufacture” 50 goats or next season’s mangoes is not Istisna‘a, since these are natural products. An order for “this particular car in the showroom” fails too, because an existing identified asset cannot be the subject (pp. 264–265)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bank can sell a specific, identified car from a factory by Istisna.","right":"Istisna cannot be on an existing identified asset; an already-made asset may be delivered only if the contract names specifications, not a particular unit (p. 264)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.11.10","t5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which cannot be the subject of Istisna‘a?","options":["A ship of given specifications","An apartment building on designated land","A harvest of corn","Machinery to specification"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Natural products are excluded (p. 264)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t10.11.3","section":"10.11.3","title":"Price in Istisna‘a","pages":[265,266],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna","arbun"],"intuition":"Unlike Salam, does the Istisna‘a price have to be paid upfront?","simpleExplanation":"The price may be cash, goods or usufruct (e.g. BOT). It must be known; it may vary with the delivery date provided one offer is finally chosen. It cannot be changed unilaterally, but may be adjusted by consent for modifications or unforeseen changes. It need not be prepaid — instalments linked to completion stages are allowed (Istihsan; analogy with Ijarah). It cannot be priced on a Murabaha cost-plus basis. ‘Arbun may be taken; forfeiture preferably limited to actual damage.","academicExplanation":["Price may be cash, tangible goods or usufruct of identified assets — relevant to BOT, where government offers usufruct for a period. It must be known; it may vary with delivery date and multiple offers may be negotiated, if one offer is finally chosen. Once settled it cannot be unilaterally changed, but can be readjusted by mutual consent for material modifications, unforeseen contingencies or input price changes. Unlike Salam, advance payment is not necessary; instalments within the agreed period and linked to completion stages are allowed (AAOIFI 3/2/2–3/2/4) — legalised by analogy and Istihsan, since the seller’s labour and commitment resemble leasing where rental may be deferred without being debt for debt; large plants need long gestation and staged payments. (Most Hanafis allow this; Malik, Shafi‘i, Ahmad, Zufar require Salam conditions including prepayment.) Istisna‘a cannot be drawn on a Murabaha cost-plus basis, as Murabaha requires an existing owned commodity with known cost (AAOIFI). The bank as manufacturer or purchaser may give or take ‘Arbun — part of price if completed, forfeited if rescinded, preferably limited to actual damage."],"examExplanation":"Istisna‘a price: cash/goods/usufruct (BOT); known; one offer chosen; consensual adjustment only; no prepayment needed — instalments by stages (Istihsan, Ijarah analogy; most Hanafis); not cost-plus Murabaha pricing; ‘Arbun allowed.","keyPoints":["BOT: usufruct as consideration.","Minority (Malik, Shafi‘i, Ahmad): prepayment required."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"BOT through usufruct","kind":"textbook","text":"A government may pay the Istisna price by giving the builder the usufruct of the asset for an agreed period — the basis of build-operate-transfer arrangements (p. 265)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Istisna can be priced on a Murabaha cost-plus basis.","right":"Murabaha needs an existing, owned asset with known cost; Istisna does not, so it cannot be structured as cost-plus (p. 265)."}],"relatedTopics":["t10.11.4","t15.3.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why may Istisna‘a payments be deferred while Salam requires full prepayment?","options":["Istisna‘a is a loan","By analogy/Istihsan: the manufacturer’s labour and commitment make it akin to leasing where rent may be deferred","Because Istisna‘a has no price","It may not be deferred"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 265."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t13"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t13","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.11.3-10.11.5","sectionTitle":"Price in Istisna'a / Penalty Clause: Delay in Fulfilling the Obligations / The Binding Nature of an Istisna'a Contract","title":"Price, Penalty Clause (Shart-e-Jazai), and Binding Nature of Istisna'a","covers":["t10.11.3","t10.11.4","t10.11.5"],"overview":"Covers Istisna'a's flexible pricing and payment rules (unlike Salam), the special Shart-e-Jazai penalty clause that reduces price for late delivery (benefiting the buyer directly, unlike Salam's charity-bound penalty), and when the contract becomes binding.","simpleExplanation":"Unlike Salam, Istisna'a doesn't require full upfront payment - price can be paid in instalments tied to construction progress, which makes sense since building something takes time and effort. If the builder delivers late, the contract can specify that the price gets reduced - and unlike Salam's late penalty (which must go to charity), this Istisna'a discount goes straight to the buyer, because it rewards them for the builder's failure to stay focused on the job. The contract isn't fully locked in until the manufacturer actually starts working - before that, either side can walk away; but once work begins, it's binding.","academicExplanation":"Istisna'a's price can be cash, tangible goods, or usufruct of identified assets (relevant to 'build, operate and transfer' (BOT) arrangements, where usufruct of the built asset serves as consideration). Price must be known in advance to avoid ignorance/dispute; it's permissible for price to vary with delivery date, and multiple offers can be negotiated before settling on one final offer. Once settled, price cannot be unilaterally increased or decreased, though mutual-consent readjustment is possible for material modifications, unforeseen contingencies, or input price changes given the potentially long manufacturing timelines of large assets. Unlike Salam, advance payment is NOT required in Istisna'a (though most Hanafi jurists historically required it, other schools like Malik, Shafi'e, Ahmad and Zufar permitted Salam-style full prepayment as an alternative structure; the majority of Hanafi jurists relaxed this via Istihsan and analogy, given common usage without explicit prohibition) - price can be paid in instalments linked to completion stages, justified by analogy to leasing (where deferred rental payment is permissible without constituting debt-for-debt) and by the practical reality of long-gestation projects. Istisna'a cannot be priced on a Murabaha (cost-plus) basis, because Murabaha requires an already-existing, already-owned, known-cost subject matter - none of which Istisna'a satisfies. The bank (as manufacturer or purchaser) may take/give a security deposit ('Arbun) - part of the price if completed, forfeitable (capped at actual damage) if rescinded. On penalty for delay: an Istisna'a contract may stipulate compensation for late delivery, permissible only if the delay isn't caused by force majeure; conversely, a penalty clause AGAINST the purchaser for payment default is not permitted (this would be Riba). A voluntary prepayment rebate is permitted if not contractually agreed. The mechanism (by analogy to a classical Ijarah wage-reduction-for-delay precedent, e.g. a tailor's wage reduced for late completion) is termed Shart-e-Jazai (penalty condition) - reducing the price for delivery delay. Crucially, unlike Salam (where late-delivery penalties MUST go to charity because the advance price is a fixed debt claim on the seller), the Shart-e-Jazai reduction in Istisna'a directly enhances the PURCHASER's income (it does not go to charity) - justified because Istisna'a's timely completion depends on the manufacturer's own labour/commitment/focus, and this benefit compensates the purchaser who suffers from nondelivery; any such undertaking is binding on the manufacturer. On binding nature: Istisna'a is NONBINDING as long as the manufacturer has not started work - either party may cancel by notice before work begins. Once work starts, the contract cannot be unilaterally cancelled by the buyer. The majority of contemporary scholars, civil law in Jordan/Sudan, the Unified Arab Law, and the OIC Fiqh Council all treat Istisna'a as binding once conditions are fulfilled - a conforming asset must be accepted by the purchaser (no Khiyar al Ro'yat/inspection option available), though Khiyar al 'Aib (defect option) and quality-specification remedies remain available for defective/nonconforming delivery.","examExplanation":"Istisna'a price: can be cash, goods, or usufruct (BOT arrangements); price can vary with delivery date; once settled cannot unilaterally change (mutual-consent readjustment possible for modifications/contingencies). NO advance payment required (unlike Salam) - instalments tied to completion stages are permitted (justified by Ijarah-rental analogy). Cannot be Murabaha-priced (cost-plus requires existing, owned, known-cost goods - Istisna'a has none of these). Penalty: Shart-e-Jazai reduces price for late delivery (not caused by force majeure) - benefits the PURCHASER directly (not charity, unlike Salam), because timely Istisna'a completion depends on manufacturer commitment/focus; NO penalty allowed against the purchaser for payment default (would be Riba). Binding nature: NONBINDING before work starts (either party can cancel); BINDING once work begins (majority view, civil law in several countries, OIC Fiqh Council) - purchaser has no Khiyar al Ro'yat but retains Khiyar al 'Aib for defects.","keyPoints":["Unlike Salam, full advance payment is NOT required in Istisna'a - price can be paid in instalments linked to construction/completion stages, justified by analogy to permissible deferred Ijarah rental payments.","Istisna'a price, once settled, cannot be unilaterally changed, but CAN be mutually readjusted for material modifications, unforeseen contingencies, or input price changes over long manufacturing timelines.","Istisna'a cannot be priced on a Murabaha (cost-plus) basis, since Murabaha requires an already-existing, already-owned, known-cost item - none of which Istisna'a's subject matter satisfies at contract formation.","The Shart-e-Jazai penalty clause reduces the price for late delivery (unless caused by force majeure) - and crucially, this reduction directly benefits the PURCHASER's income (unlike Salam's late-delivery penalty, which must go to charity), because Istisna'a's timely completion depends on the manufacturer's own labour and commitment.","A penalty clause against the purchaser for payment default is NOT permitted in Istisna'a - this would constitute Riba.","Istisna'a is nonbinding until the manufacturer starts work (either party may cancel by notice beforehand); once work has started, the contract becomes binding and cannot be unilaterally cancelled by the buyer - though Khiyar al 'Aib remains available for genuine defects."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shart-e-Jazai","definition":"The 'penalty condition' in Istisna'a - a contractual clause reducing the price by an agreed amount for late delivery, whose proceeds enhance the purchaser's own income (unlike Salam's charity-bound late penalty), justified by analogy to a classical Ijarah wage-reduction-for-delay precedent."},{"term":"'Arbun (in Istisna'a)","definition":"A security deposit that becomes part of the price if the Istisna'a contract is completed, or is forfeited (ideally capped at actual damage suffered) if the contract is rescinded."}],"conditions":["Istisna'a price cannot be unilaterally increased or decreased once settled, though mutual-consent readjustment is possible for genuine modifications or contingencies.","A late-delivery penalty in Istisna'a is permissible only if the delay is not caused by force majeure.","A penalty clause against the purchaser for payment default is not permitted in Istisna'a, as this would constitute Riba.","Istisna'a is nonbinding until the manufacturer begins work; either party may cancel by notice before work starts."],"principles":["By analogy to classical Ijarah practice (e.g. a tailor's wage reduced for late completion), Fiqh permits a price-reduction penalty condition (Shart-e-Jazai) in Istisna'a for late delivery."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Tailor Wage-Reduction Analogy","kind":"textbook","text":"Classical jurists allowed a condition in Ijarah such as: a person hiring a tailor's services could stipulate that the wage will be 10 dirhams if the clothes are prepared within a week, but only 8 (or another reduced amount) if delayed. By analogy, jurists permit a Shart-e-Jazai penalty clause in Istisna'a reducing the price for delay in delivery, supply, or construction."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume the Istisna'a late-delivery penalty, like Salam's, must go to charity. It does not - the Shart-e-Jazai price reduction directly benefits the purchaser's own income, because it stems from the manufacturer's own controllable commitment/labour, unlike Salam's penalty (which reflects an already-fixed debt claim)."],"distinctions":["Istisna'a's Shart-e-Jazai penalty (reduces price, benefits purchaser directly) vs Salam's late-delivery penalty (goes to charity, since the advance price is a fixed debt claim on the seller)."],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Ijarah","Force Majeure"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3-10.11.5","pages":[265,266,267]},"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example - generated for learning: Istisna'a Price Paid in Progress Installments","formula":"Installment Amount = Contract Price x Installment Percentage","inputs":[{"label":"Total Istisna'a contract price (fixed at signing)","value":"$2,000,000"},{"label":"Installment 1 - on order confirmation","value":"20% of contract price"},{"label":"Installment 2 - on certified 50% completion of manufacture","value":"30% of contract price"},{"label":"Installment 3 - on delivery/acceptance of the finished asset","value":"50% of contract price"}],"steps":["Total contract price = $2,000,000, fixed by mutual consent at signing and not unilaterally changeable thereafter (Section 10.11.3).","Installment 1 (order confirmation, 20%) = $2,000,000 x 0.20 = $400,000.","Installment 2 (50% completion milestone, 30%) = $2,000,000 x 0.30 = $600,000.","Installment 3 (delivery/acceptance, 50%) = $2,000,000 x 0.50 = $1,000,000.","Check: $400,000 + $600,000 + $1,000,000 = $2,000,000, matching the agreed contract price."],"result":"The manufacturer receives $400,000 on order confirmation, $600,000 at the 50% completion milestone, and the remaining $1,000,000 on delivery - together equal to the full $2,000,000 contract price.","interpretation":"This staged structure is only possible because, unlike Salam, Istisna'a does not require the price to be paid in full in advance - Section 10.11.3 states the price 'can be paid in instalments within the agreed time period and can also be linked with the completion stages,' a flexibility justified by analogy to Ijarah's permissible deferred rental payments and by the practical reality that manufacturing large assets takes time. The exact split (20/30/50 here) is itself negotiable between the parties - the book's own case studies elsewhere in the chapter (e.g. the housing and project-finance Istisna'a examples) show total prices paid to contractors in multiple instalments, without any mandated formula, in direct contrast to Salam, where the entire price is mandatorily due in a single payment at contract signing.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265,266]}}]}]},{"id":"t10.11.4","section":"10.11.4","title":"Penalty Clause: Delay in Fulfilling Obligations (Shart-e-Jazai)","pages":[266,266],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna","late-payment-penalty"],"intuition":"Why can an Istisna‘a buyer keep the penalty for late delivery, while in Salam it goes to charity?","simpleExplanation":"An Istisna‘a contract may reduce the price by an agreed amount if the manufacturer delivers late (unless force majeure) — Shart-e-Jazai, by analogy with the tailor’s differential wage in Ijarah. The reduction is the purchaser’s income because timely completion depends on the manufacturer’s effort. No penalty may be imposed on the purchaser for late payment (Riba). In Salam, late-delivery penalties go to charity because the advance price is a debt.","academicExplanation":["An Istisna‘a contract may stipulate an agreed amount compensating the purchaser for late delivery, if not due to force majeure; a penalty against the purchaser for payment default is not permitted (Riba) (AAOIFI 6/7). A voluntary prepayment rebate is permissible if not agreed in the contract. Parties may agree that the price is reduced by a specified amount for delay — by analogy with the classical Ijarah rule (a tailor’s wage 10 dirhams within a week, 12 within two days). This Shart-e-Jazai (Zuhayli) enhances the orderer’s income and does not go to charity, because timely completion depends on the manufacturer’s labour and commitment — he may otherwise pursue other orders. In Salam any late-delivery penalty goes to charity because the advance price is a debt that cannot be increased; permissible only absent force majeure; no penalty against the purchaser for payment default."],"examExplanation":"Shart-e-Jazai: price reduction for late delivery in Istisna‘a (not force majeure), kept by purchaser (tailor analogy). No penalty on purchaser for late payment. Salam: late-delivery penalty to charity.","keyPoints":["Tailor analogy: 10 dirhams in a week vs 12 in two days."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Shart-e-Jazai in a road contract","kind":"textbook","text":"An Istisna‘a for a road priced at 50 million reduces the price by 100,000 for each week of late delivery (force majeure excepted). The reduction is the buyer’s income, by analogy with a tailor paid 10 dirhams in a week or 12 in two days. In Salam, by contrast, a late-delivery penalty would go to charity (p. 266)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.9","t10.4.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Istisna‘a, a price reduction for late delivery (Shart-e-Jazai):","options":["Goes to charity","Benefits the purchaser as income","Is prohibited","Is paid by the purchaser"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 266."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Istisna‘a late delivery","b":"Salam late delivery","text":"Istisna‘a: price reduction benefits purchaser (Shart-e-Jazai). Salam: penalty goes to charity because the advance is a debt."}]},{"id":"t10.11.5","section":"10.11.5","title":"Binding Nature of Istisna‘a and Guarantees","pages":[266,267],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna"],"intuition":"When does an Istisna‘a become binding?","simpleExplanation":"Before the manufacturer starts work, either party may cancel by notice; after work starts the buyer cannot cancel unilaterally. Most contemporary scholars, Jordanian and Sudanese civil law, the Unified Arab Law and the OIC treat it as binding under conditions. If the asset meets specifications, the buyer must accept — no Khiyar al-Ro’yat — but has defect and specification options (indemnity). Guarantees: security, collateral, performance bonds and ‘Arbun (forfeiture preferably limited to actual damage).","academicExplanation":["10.11.5: Istisna‘a is nonbinding until the manufacturer starts work; before that either party may cancel by notice; afterwards the buyer cannot cancel unilaterally. The majority of contemporary scholars, civil law in Jordan and Sudan, the League of Arab States’ Unified Arab Law and the OIC Fiqh Council treat it as binding subject to conditions. If the asset conforms, the purchaser must accept and cannot exercise Khiyar al-Ro’yat, but has Khiyar al-‘Aib and the option of specified quality — the right to be indemnified for proven defects or missing specifications. 10.11.6 Guarantees: the bank as manufacturer or buyer may give or demand security, collateral or a performance bond for timely, conforming work; ‘Arbun may be taken — part of price if fulfilled, forfeited if rescinded, preferably limited to actual damage (AAOIFI 3/3)."],"examExplanation":"Istisna‘a: revocable until work starts, binding thereafter (OIC, majority). No Ro’yat option if conforming; defect/specification options. Guarantees: performance bond, collateral, ‘Arbun.","keyPoints":["Binding once manufacture begins."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"After work has started","kind":"textbook","text":"A buyer who ordered a ship wants to cancel after the yard has laid the keel. He cannot cancel unilaterally once manufacture has begun. If the ship conforms he must accept it, though he may claim for defects or missing specifications (pp. 266–267)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.11.9","t5.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"When does an Istisna‘a contract become binding on the buyer?","options":["At signing always","Once the manufacturer starts work on the subject matter","Only on delivery","Never"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 266."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"subsections":[{"number":"10.11.5","title":"The Binding Nature of an Istisna‘a Contract","page":266,"points":["Nonbinding before work starts","Binding after (OIC)"]},{"number":"10.11.6","title":"Guarantees","page":267,"points":["Security, performance bond, ‘Arbun"]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Before work starts","b":"After work starts","text":"Before the manufacturer starts, either party may cancel by notice; once work begins, the OIC Fiqh Council and most scholars treat Istisna as binding (pp. 266–267)."}]},{"id":"t10.11.7","section":"10.11.7","title":"Parallel Istisna‘a (Subcontracting) and Agency","pages":[267,268],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna","parallel-istisna","wakalah"],"intuition":"Banks don’t build roads. How can a bank sell a road through Istisna‘a?","simpleExplanation":"Unless the contract requires personal manufacture, the seller may subcontract. A bank signs Istisna‘a with the customer (as seller) and a Parallel Istisna‘a with a contractor (as buyer). The parallel delivery date must not precede the original; ownership risks stay separate; the contracts are independent; parallel only with a third party. A bank may also prepay a manufacturer for liquidity and resell by Parallel Istisna‘a. Agents may supervise manufacture or sell the asset under a separate agency; consultants may be hired.","academicExplanation":["10.11.7: Istisna‘a is not confined to the manufacturer’s own work; if the contract is silent or allows, the seller may get it made by others — Parallel Istisna‘a. The bank contracts with the customer and separately with a contractor for the subject; the parallel delivery date must not precede the original; the bank is buyer in one and seller in the other; ownership risks of each contract stay with the respective parties until transfer; the two are independent, not tied; Parallel Istisna‘a only with a third party (AAOIFI 7/1, 7/3). The bank may buy items to specification paying cash to give the manufacturer liquidity, then sell items to another party by Parallel Istisna‘a. 10.11.8: the bank may appoint an agent, with the other party’s consent, to supervise manufacture or sell the asset — including the client/manufacturer — under a separate agreement; if the purchaser delays taking delivery, the seller may sell the asset and pay the excess over dues to the purchaser; consultants may supervise conformity, with expenses agreed."],"examExplanation":"Parallel Istisna‘a: bank = seller to customer, buyer from contractor; delivery date not earlier than original; independent; separate risks; third party only. Agency (separate) for supervision or sale; consultants.","keyPoints":["Parallel only with a third party.","Risks of each contract stay separate.","The parallel contract’s delivery date must not precede the original contract’s.","The bank may pay a manufacturer in cash upfront to give liquidity, then sell similar items to another party by Parallel Istisna."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Parallel dates","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank agrees to deliver a building to its client on 30 June and signs a Parallel Istisna‘a with a contractor for delivery on 31 May. The parallel date must not precede the original, and each contract stands independently: the bank must deliver even if the contractor fails (pp. 267–268)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.11.10","t4.2.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Parallel Istisna‘a, the delivery date in the parallel contract with the contractor must:","options":["Precede the original contract’s date","Not precede the original Istisna‘a date (per the chapter)","Be the same as signing","Be unspecified"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 267."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t14"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t14","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.11.6-10.11.9","sectionTitle":"Guarantees / Parallel Contract - Subcontracting / Istisna'a and Agency Contract / Post Execution Scenario","title":"Guarantees, Parallel Istisna'a, Agency, and Post-Execution Rules","covers":["t10.11.7","t10.11.9"],"overview":"Covers security/guarantee arrangements, how banks use Parallel Istisna'a to act as both buyer and seller, agency arrangements for supervision/sale, and the rules governing delivery, risk, and post-completion adjustments.","simpleExplanation":"Banks using Istisna'a can demand collateral or a security deposit to make sure the work is done properly and on time. A bank can also be the 'middleman' - taking an order from a customer (as seller) while separately subcontracting the actual manufacturing to someone else (as buyer in a second, independent contract) - this is called Parallel Istisna'a. Banks can also appoint agents to supervise construction or sell the finished product. Throughout, the risk of loss stays with whoever currently holds the goods (seller before delivery, buyer after), and if the bank ends up spending less than expected building something, it gets to keep the extra profit - it doesn't have to pass savings on to the customer.","academicExplanation":"Guarantees: the bank, as manufacturer or purchaser, can give/demand security, collateral, or a performance bond ensuring timely, per-spec performance, and can take 'Arbun (forfeitable if rescinded, ideally capped at actual damage). Parallel Contract/Subcontracting: since Istisna'a isn't confined to what the manufacturer personally makes, the seller/supplier can subcontract manufacture per agreed specifications (if the contract permits or is silent on this) - this creates Parallel Istisna'a: the bank enters an Istisna'a with a customer (bank as seller) AND a separate Parallel Istisna'a with a third-party contractor (bank as buyer) to produce the same subject matter; the parallel contract's delivery date must not precede the original contract's date; the two contracts (and their respective ownership risks) remain fully independent, never tied together contractually, and Parallel Istisna'a is permitted only with a genuine third party. A bank may also buy on clear specification and pay cash upfront (providing liquidity to the manufacturer), then separately contract to sell matching-spec items to another party via Parallel Istisna'a. Agency Contract: the bank (as seller or buyer) can appoint an agent (with the other party's consent) to supervise manufacturing or sell the received asset - commonly the client/manufacturer themselves acting as selling agent; the agency agreement must be separate and independent from the Istisna'a agreement. An agency arrangement can also handle a purchaser's delayed delivery-taking: the seller can sell the asset in the market and remit any surplus over dues to the purchaser. Banks can also engage consultants to supervise construction/verify specification conformity or provide other advisory services, with supervision expenses allocated by mutual agreement. Post Execution Scenario: before work starts, either party may rescind; once started, the contract is binding, alterable only by mutual consent. If the delivered subject matter doesn't conform to specifications, the customer may accept or refuse it; the purchaser is never regarded as owner of the manufacturer's in-process raw materials. If the bank's (as seller) ACTUAL cost is less than forecast, or it secures a subcontractor discount under Parallel Istisna'a, the bank is NOT obliged to pass on a discount to the purchaser - any additional profit (or loss, symmetrically, if actual costs exceed forecast) belongs to the bank. A customer may request the bank (as purchaser) to replace an existing contractor mid-project; the customer bears the cost/liability of assessing the project's existing status for this purpose. The bank, as manufacturer, must bear ownership risk, maintenance, and Takaful expenses prior to delivery, including theft/abnormal-damage risk, and CANNOT contractually disclaim liability for defects. The orderer has the right to obtain collateral from the manufacturer covering amounts paid and specification/timing performance. A voluntary prepayment rebate (not contractually pre-agreed) remains permissible. On delivery/risk: before delivery, the asset is at the SELLER's risk (including raw material/in-process losses); after delivery, risk transfers to the purchaser; possession (physical or constructive) requires a clear demarcation line between handing-over and taking-over. If delivered early, the purchaser should accept if specifications are met (can refuse only for nonconformity or other genuine justification); if the subject doesn't conform to specifications at delivery, the purchaser may reject OR accept as-is (acceptance-as-is constitutes satisfactory contract performance).","examExplanation":"Guarantees: security/collateral/performance bonds and 'Arbun permitted (forfeiture capped at actual damage). Parallel Istisna'a: bank is seller in one Istisna'a (with customer) and buyer in a separate, independent Parallel Istisna'a (with a genuine third-party subcontractor) - parallel delivery date must not precede the original; contracts and their risks stay fully separate. Agency: bank appoints an agent (often the client/manufacturer) to supervise construction or sell the asset - always a SEPARATE agreement from the Istisna'a contract itself. Post-execution: bank keeps any windfall profit if actual costs < forecast (no obligation to discount to the customer) and symmetrically absorbs losses if costs > forecast; risk stays with the seller pre-delivery, transfers to buyer post-delivery; manufacturer cannot contractually disclaim defect liability.","keyPoints":["A bank can act as both seller (Istisna'a with the customer) and buyer (Parallel Istisna'a with a genuine third-party subcontractor) in the same overall financing structure - the two contracts remain fully independent, never tied together, and the parallel delivery date cannot precede the original delivery date.","Parallel Istisna'a is permitted only with a genuine third party - not with the original customer or an affiliated entity of the seller.","The agency agreement (for supervision or resale) must always be kept separate and independent from the Istisna'a contract itself.","If a bank's actual production cost (as seller) is lower than forecast, or it secures a subcontractor discount via Parallel Istisna'a, it is NOT obliged to pass this saving to the purchaser - the extra profit (or, symmetrically, any cost overrun loss) belongs to the bank.","Before delivery, the asset (including raw materials and work-in-progress) remains at the seller's risk; after delivery, risk transfers to the purchaser - and the manufacturer cannot contractually disclaim liability for defects.","If the delivered subject matter fails to conform to agreed specifications, the purchaser may reject it or accept it as-is; acceptance as-is constitutes satisfactory performance of the contract."],"definitions":[{"term":"Parallel Istisna'a","definition":"A second, independent Istisna'a contract entered into by a bank (as buyer, with a third-party subcontractor) to fulfill its obligations under a first Istisna'a contract (where the bank is seller, to a customer) - the two contracts must remain legally separate, with the parallel delivery date not preceding the original."}],"conditions":["The delivery date of a Parallel Istisna'a contract must not precede the delivery date of the original Istisna'a contract.","Parallel Istisna'a is permitted only with a genuine third party, and the two contracts (with their respective ownership risks) must remain fully independent of each other.","The agency agreement for supervision or resale in Istisna'a must be separate and independent from the Istisna'a contract itself.","The manufacturer in Istisna'a cannot contractually disclaim liability for defects in the delivered asset."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume any cost saving realized by the bank in Istisna'a must be passed to the customer, similar to a Murabaha rebate rule. The book explicitly states the bank is NOT obligated to share such savings - Istisna'a pricing, once fixed, belongs entirely to the bank's own risk/reward, unlike Murabaha's strict cost-plus transparency."],"distinctions":["Parallel Istisna'a (bank as buyer in a second contract, subcontracting production - permitted with a genuine third party) vs simply asking the original customer to also act as the manufacturer's subcontractor (not a valid independent Parallel Istisna'a structure)."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Takaful","Bai' al 'Inah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.6-10.11.9","pages":[266,267,268,269]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Original Istisna'a (Bank as Seller)","description":"The bank enters an Istisna'a contract with a customer, undertaking as seller/manufacturer to deliver a specified manufactured or constructed asset to the customer's specifications by an agreed date and price."},{"step":"Parallel Istisna'a (Bank as Buyer)","description":"Separately, since Istisna'a is not confined to what the manufacturer himself makes, the bank enters a second, independent Parallel Istisna'a contract with a genuine third-party contractor, this time as buyer, ordering the same subject matter to be manufactured to the same specifications."},{"step":"Delivery Dates Sequenced","description":"The delivery date of the Parallel Istisna'a is fixed so as not to precede the delivery date of the original Istisna'a, ensuring the bank can receive the asset from its subcontractor in time to pass it on to its own customer."},{"step":"Contracts Kept Independent","description":"The two contracts, and their respective ownership-related risks, remain fully independent - the rights and obligations of one cannot be made contingent on the other, and Parallel Istisna'a is permitted only with a genuine third party."},{"step":"Subcontractor Manufactures and Delivers to the Bank","description":"The third-party contractor manufactures the asset and delivers it to the bank under the Parallel Istisna'a; before this delivery, the asset remains at the subcontractor's risk, transferring to the bank upon delivery."},{"step":"Bank Delivers to the Original Customer","description":"The bank, as seller under the original Istisna'a, delivers the same-specification asset to its customer, who accepts it if it conforms to specifications or may reject or accept nonconforming goods as-is."},{"step":"Profit or Loss Retained by the Bank","description":"If the bank's actual cost (including any discount secured from the subcontractor) is less than the forecast cost, the bank is not obliged to pass the saving to the customer and keeps the additional profit; the same rule applies symmetrically if actual costs exceed the forecast."}]}],"subsections":[{"number":"10.11.7","title":"Parallel Contract — Subcontracting","page":267,"points":["Independent contracts","Delivery date rule","Third party only"]},{"number":"10.11.8","title":"Istisna‘a and Agency Contract","page":268,"points":["Supervision or sale agency","Delay in taking delivery"]}],"processSteps":["Bank signs Istisna‘a with customer (bank = seller) at price P1, deferred","Bank signs separate Parallel Istisna‘a with contractor (bank = buyer) at price P2 < P1","Contractor builds; bank may appoint supervisor/consultant","Contractor delivers to bank (not earlier than needed)","Bank delivers to customer; customer pays P1 per schedule"]},{"id":"t10.11.9","section":"10.11.9","title":"Istisna‘a — Post-Execution Scenario","pages":[268,269],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["istisna"],"intuition":"During construction and at delivery, who owns what, and who bears which risk?","simpleExplanation":"Before work, either may rescind; after, only by consent. The buyer does not own materials held by the manufacturer. Cost savings or subcontractor discounts belong to the bank-seller (as do overruns). The bank may replace a contractor on a started project (assessment costs on customer). The bank-seller bears ownership, maintenance, Takaful and damage risk before delivery and cannot disclaim defect liability. Risk passes on delivery (physical or constructive). Early conforming delivery should be accepted; non-conforming may be rejected or accepted as satisfactory performance.","academicExplanation":["Work in progress: rescission before work starts; binding after, changes by consent. The purchaser pays per schedule; the manufacturer supplies per specifications; non-conforming subject may be accepted or refused. The purchaser does not own materials in the manufacturer’s possession. If actual cost is less than forecast or a subcontractor discount is obtained, the bank-seller need not pass it on — extra profit or loss is the bank’s, and vice versa (AAOIFI 3/2/6). The bank (as purchaser) may replace an existing contractor at the customer’s request, the assessment cost and liabilities to date remaining the customer’s. As manufacturer, the bank bears ownership risk, maintenance, Takaful, theft and abnormal damage before delivery and cannot stipulate non-liability for defects; the orderer may take collateral for amounts paid and delivery. A voluntary prepayment rebate is permissible if not agreed. Delivery: before delivery, loss to raw materials or work-in-progress is the seller’s; after, the purchaser’s; possession physical or constructive with a clear demarcation of handover; early conforming delivery should be accepted unless genuine justification; non-conforming may be rejected or accepted in its condition (acceptance = satisfactory performance) (AAOIFI 6/1–6/4)."],"examExplanation":"WIP: buyer doesn’t own materials; bank-seller keeps cost savings/bears overruns; bears ownership/maintenance/Takaful risks pre-delivery; cannot disclaim defects. Risk passes on delivery. Early conforming delivery accepted.","keyPoints":["Manufacturer cannot disclaim defect liability.","Cost savings not owed to purchaser.","A manufacturer cannot stipulate that it is not liable for defects."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Cost savings belong to the bank","kind":"textbook","text":"If the bank’s actual cost or subcontract price is lower than forecast, it need not pass a discount to the purchaser — gains and losses on cost are the bank’s (p. 268)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.11.11"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A bank (as Istisna‘a seller) gets a discount from its subcontractor. Must it pass the saving to the purchaser?","options":["Yes, always","No — additional profit (or loss) pertains to the bank","Only half","Only if the purchaser asks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 268."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t10.11.10","section":"10.11.10","title":"The Potential of Istisna‘a and Case Studies (Boxes 10.13–10.16)","pages":[269,278],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna","parallel-istisna","diminishing-musharakah"],"intuition":"Where can Istisna‘a be used — homes, exports, buildings, roads?","simpleExplanation":"Istisna‘a suits high-technology goods (aircraft, ships), buildings, dams, highways, housing, export finance and working capital where orders come in advance. Cases: housing via Istisna‘a + Diminishing Musharakah (Rs.7m apartment; client Rs.2m, bank Rs.5m over ten years); preshipment export of garments (Rs.100m Istisna‘a, Rs.110m L/C — not if L/C already opened); Parallel Istisna‘a for 100 flats (Rs.120m to builder, Rs.100m to contractor); government road (Rs.1200m over ten years vs Rs.1000m contractor; tolls assigned).","academicExplanation":["Potential: aircraft, ships, buildings, dams, highways; housing and export finance; working capital where orders are received in advance; construction (apartments, hospitals, schools, universities); residential/commercial development; aircraft, locomotive and shipbuilding industries.","Box 10.13 Housing: builder C sells apartments at Rs.7m cash; client A has Rs.2m and needs Rs.5m for ten years; A and bank B form a Musharakah (Shirkatul-milk) pool of Rs.7m and jointly contract Istisna‘a with C, paying in four instalments; B appoints A to supervise; on handover B leases its share to A; A buys one unit monthly, rent decreasing; after ten years ownership transfers (Diminishing Musharakah).","Box 10.14 Preshipment export: client A has a Rs.110m garment export order; B signs Istisna‘a for garments at Rs.100m within three months and appoints A its export agent; the importer’s L/C of Rs.110m is in B’s name (or A’s under agency); if an L/C has already been opened, Istisna‘a is not possible (avoid ‘Inah); on completion B takes delivery and bears risk; A exports as B’s agent; B receives Rs.110m.","Box 10.15 Building project: builder A contracts Istisna‘a with B for 100 flats in 12 months for Rs.120m; A books flats with downpayments; B signs Parallel Istisna‘a with contractor C for Rs.100m in four instalments; A supervises as agent; C delivers to B, which hands over to A; A sells flats in instalments and assigns receivables to B; allottees pay rent and buy units. Box 10.16 Government road: government G contracts Istisna‘a with B for a road by December 2007 for Rs.1200m over ten years; B signs Parallel Istisna‘a with C for Rs.1000m in four instalments; tolls are assigned to the bank, with G paying any shortfall as rental."],"examExplanation":"Potential: infrastructure, housing, high-tech manufacturing, exports, working capital. Cases: housing (Istisna‘a + DM), export (Istisna‘a + agency; not after L/C opened), Parallel Istisna‘a for flats (Rs.120m vs 100m), government road with tolls assigned.","keyPoints":["Export Istisna‘a impossible if L/C already opened (avoid ‘Inah).","Bank margin in Parallel Istisna‘a = difference between the two contract prices."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 10.13 — Housing via Istisna‘a and DM","kind":"textbook","text":"Rs.7m apartment: client contributes Rs.2m, bank Rs.5m; they jointly order the apartment by Istisna‘a; bank leases its share and the client buys units monthly over ten years."},{"title":"Box 10.16 — Government road","kind":"textbook","text":"Bank sells a road to the government by Istisna‘a for Rs.1200m payable over ten years and buys it from a contractor by Parallel Istisna‘a for Rs.1000m in four instalments; toll receipts are assigned to the bank."},{"title":"Box 10.13 — apartment by Istisna plus DM","kind":"textbook","text":"Client (Rs.2m) and bank (Rs.5m) form a Shirkatul-milk and jointly order a Rs.7m apartment by Istisna; after handover the bank leases its share and the client buys units monthly over ten years (pp. 274–275)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.9","t14.4.3","t10.11.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Box 10.14, why is Istisna‘a not possible if the importer has already opened an L/C in the exporter’s favour?","options":["L/Cs are prohibited","To avoid Bai‘ al ‘Inah","Because Istisna‘a needs prepayment","Because garments are natural products"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 276."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch10-t15"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch10-t15","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"10.11.10-10.11.11","sectionTitle":"The Potential of Istisna'a / Risk Management in Istisna'a","title":"The Potential of Istisna'a and its Risk Management, Compared with Salam and Ijarah","covers":["t10.11.10","t10.11.11"],"overview":"Surveys Istisna'a's growth potential across construction, housing, and high-technology industries, catalogs its key risks and mitigants, and contrasts it structurally with Salam and with Ijarah/Ujrah.","simpleExplanation":"Istisna'a is well-suited to financing big-ticket manufactured and constructed assets: aircraft, ships, apartment buildings, hospitals, schools, roads, and housing schemes - basically anything that needs to be built or manufactured to order over time. Like Salam, it carries real risks (price, delivery, quality), but banks manage these with collateral, performance bonds, and the special Shart-e-Jazai penalty clause. It's useful to keep clear in your head how Istisna'a differs from Salam (Istisna'a: only manufactured goods, no upfront payment required, penalty benefits the buyer, nonbinding until work starts) and from Ijarah/Ujrah (Istisna'a: manufacturer supplies materials and sets a fixed price; Ujrah: buyer supplies materials, manufacturer is just paid wages).","academicExplanation":"Istisna'a's potential spans high-technology manufacturing (aircraft, ships, locomotives), construction financing (apartment buildings, hospitals, schools, universities), residential/commercial development and housing finance schemes, and infrastructure. Risks banks face in Istisna'a financing: settlement risk, price risk, delivery risk, possession risk, and market risk - mitigated overall through proper collateral, performance bonds, technical expertise for effective marketing/cost control, suitable Takaful policies, careful client selection, and sound capital budgeting/liquidity management. Specific mitigants (Box 10.9): since the bank is not the owner of raw materials in the manufacturer's possession (so has no direct claim on them in nonperformance scenarios), security held by the bank substitutes for this; delivery risk from a Parallel Istisna'a subcontractor's late delivery is mitigated via the Shart-e-Jazai clause (reducing the Istisna'a price for delay); since sale of Istisna'a goods before physical possession is not permitted (creating asset/price/marketing risk), the bank can take a 'promise to purchase' from a third party and arrange agency-based sale; quality risk from inferior manufactured goods can be mitigated by obtaining a quality guarantee from the original supplier. Box 10.10 crystallizes the key Istisna'a-vs-Salam and Istisna'a-vs-Ijarah(Ujrah) distinctions: (1) Istisna'a's subject is always something needing manufacture, while Salam's subject can be natural products OR manufactured goods; (2) Istisna'a price need not be paid fully in advance, while Salam's must be; (3) Istisna'a mainly covers Qimi (unique) goods but can also cover fungible/trademarked goods, while Salam's subject must be Mithli (fungible), since the seller may need to source replacement from the market; (4) Istisna'a's late-delivery penalty (Shart-e-Jazai) directly enhances the purchaser's income, while Salam's penalty goes to charity, leaving the purchaser's P&L unaffected; (5) Istisna'a is nonbinding until work starts (either party can revoke), while Salam is binding once executed (rescindable only by mutual consent). Versus Ijarah/Ujrah: (1) in Istisna'a the manufacturer uses his OWN materials at a fixed sale price, while in Ujrah the manufacturer uses the BUYER's materials for agreed wages; (2) Istisna'a can cover anything needing manufacture, while Ijarah applies only to assets whose corpus is not consumed with use; (3) in Istisna'a, asset risk transfers to the purchaser upon delivery and the price must be paid regardless of what subsequently happens to the asset, while in Ijarah, asset risk remains with the owner/lessor throughout, and the lessee owes rental only if the asset remains usable per normal market practice.","examExplanation":"Istisna'a potential: aircraft/ships/high-tech manufacturing, construction (apartments, hospitals, schools), housing finance, infrastructure. Risks: settlement, price, delivery, possession, market risk - mitigated via collateral, performance bonds, Takaful, careful client selection, sound liquidity management. Istisna'a vs Salam (5 key differences): (1) subject always needs manufacture (vs Salam: natural or manufactured); (2) no full advance payment required (vs Salam: must be paid fully in advance); (3) covers Qimi + fungible goods (vs Salam: Mithli/fungible only); (4) Shart-e-Jazai penalty benefits purchaser directly (vs Salam: penalty goes to charity); (5) nonbinding until work starts (vs Salam: binding once executed). Istisna'a vs Ijarah/Ujrah: manufacturer's own materials + fixed price (vs Ujrah: buyer's materials + wages); covers anything needing manufacture (vs Ijarah: only non-consumed-with-use assets); risk transfers on delivery, price owed regardless of subsequent asset fate (vs Ijarah: risk stays with lessor, rental owed only if asset remains usable).","keyPoints":["Istisna'a's growth potential spans high-technology manufacturing (aircraft, ships, locomotives), major construction (apartments, hospitals, schools, universities), housing finance schemes, and infrastructure projects.","Key risks in Istisna'a financing: settlement risk, price risk, delivery risk, possession risk, and market risk - mitigated through collateral, performance bonds, technical expertise, Takaful, careful client selection, and sound capital budgeting/liquidity management.","Five core Istisna'a-vs-Salam distinctions: subject matter (manufactured only, vs natural-or-manufactured), advance payment (not required, vs mandatory), goods type (Qimi + fungible, vs Mithli/fungible only), late-delivery penalty destination (purchaser's income, vs charity), and binding nature (nonbinding until work starts, vs binding once executed).","Key Istisna'a-vs-Ijarah/Ujrah distinctions: who supplies materials (manufacturer in Istisna'a vs buyer in Ujrah), what can be financed (anything needing manufacture in Istisna'a vs only non-consumed-with-use assets in Ijarah), and risk timing (transfers to purchaser at delivery in Istisna'a, regardless of the asset's subsequent fate, vs staying with the lessor throughout Ijarah, with rental owed only if the asset remains usable)."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Housing Finance Combining Istisna'a and Diminishing Musharakah (Box 10.13, summarized)","kind":"textbook","text":"A builder constructs an apartment costing Rs. 7 million. A client with Rs. 2 million and a bank providing Rs. 5 million form a Shirkatulmilk Musharakah pool and jointly enter an Istisna'a agreement with the builder, paying in four instalments. After construction, the bank leases its ownership share to the client for rent, and the client buys out the bank's share month by month (Diminishing Musharakah) until, after ten years, full ownership transfers to the client."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often think Istisna'a is simply 'Salam for manufactured goods.' While related, the two differ fundamentally in payment timing (advance payment mandatory only in Salam), binding nature (Istisna'a nonbinding until work starts; Salam binding once executed), and penalty treatment (Istisna'a's benefits the purchaser; Salam's goes to charity)."],"distinctions":["Istisna'a (manufactured goods only, flexible payment timing, nonbinding until work starts, purchaser-benefiting penalty) vs Salam (natural or manufactured fungible goods, mandatory full advance payment, binding once executed, charity-bound penalty) vs Ijarah/Ujrah (usage-based rental of a non-consumed asset, or wage-for-labour-only, with risk staying on the lessor)."],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Ijarah","Diminishing Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10-10.11.11","pages":[269,270,271]}}],"calc":{"type":"parallel-istisna","note":"Reproduces Box 10.15/10.16 margins: customer price vs contractor price and payment schedules (practice variants labelled)."}},{"id":"t10.11.11","section":"10.11.11","title":"Risk Management in Istisna‘a and Comparisons (Boxes 10.9–10.12)","pages":[269,274],"tier":"core","concepts":["istisna","salam","ijarah","risk-management"],"intuition":"What risks does an Istisna‘a bank carry — and how exactly does Istisna‘a differ from Salam and from Ujrah/Ijarah?","simpleExplanation":"Risks: settlement, price, delivery, possession, market. Mitigation: collateral, performance bonds, technical expertise, Takaful, good clients, capital budgeting. Box 10.9: bank doesn’t own materials (security); subcontractor delay (Shart-e-Jazai); no sale before possession (promise, agency); quality (supplier guarantee). Box 10.10: Istisna‘a vs Salam (manufactured vs any; no full prepayment vs full; Qimi mainly vs Mithli; penalty to buyer vs charity; nonbinding until work starts vs binding) and vs Ujrah (own vs buyer’s material; manufactured items vs non-consumable assets; risk to buyer after delivery vs lessor keeps risk).","academicExplanation":["Risks: settlement, price, delivery, possession, market; mitigated by collateral, performance bonds, technical expertise for marketing and cost-effectiveness, Takaful, client selection and capital budgeting/liquidity policies. Box 10.9: ownership of material — the bank does not own materials held by the manufacturer, no claim on non-performance → security; delivery risk from subcontractor delay → Shart-e-Jazai price reduction clause; sale not permissible before physical possession → promise to purchase, agency; quality risk → quality guarantee from the supplier.","Box 10.10 Istisna‘a vs Salam: (1) always needs manufacturing vs natural or manufactured; (2) price need not be fully prepaid vs full advance; (3) mainly Qimi goods (also trademarked uniform items) vs Mithli goods obtainable from the market; (4) late-delivery price reduction is the purchaser’s income (Shart-e-Jazai) vs penalty to charity; (5) nonbinding until work starts vs binding once executed. Istisna‘a vs Ijarah (Ujrah): manufacturer’s own materials at fixed price vs buyer’s materials for wages; anything needing manufacture vs assets whose corpus is not consumed; asset risk to purchaser after delivery (pays regardless) vs risk with lessor and rent only if usable.","Box 10.11 accounting (bank as seller, AAOIFI): costs to Istisna‘a WIP (or cost account for parallel); billings to receivables and billing account offset against WIP; pre-contract costs deferred; revenue by percentage-of-completion or completed-contract method; deferred profit by proportionate allocation (preferred) or on receipt; early settlement discounts deducted; parallel profit by percentage of completion; change orders and claims; maintenance/warranty accruals; expected losses recognised. Box 10.12 (bank as buyer): progress billings as assets; conforming commodity at historical cost; late delivery compensated from performance bond; non-conforming — receivables or lower of cost and cash equivalent."],"examExplanation":"Istisna‘a risks + Box 10.9 mitigations. Box 10.10: vs Salam (manufactured; deferred payment OK; Qimi; Shart-e-Jazai to buyer; revocable until work starts) and vs Ujrah (own material; manufactured items; risk passes on delivery). Accounting: % completion or completed contract.","keyPoints":["Bank has no claim on manufacturer’s materials.","Percentage-of-completion revenue recognition."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Delay risk and its mitigant","kind":"textbook","text":"A subcontractor’s delay threatens the bank’s own delivery date. Box 10.9’s mitigant is a Shart-e-Jazai price-reduction clause in the Parallel Istisna‘a, plus security from the manufacturer, since the bank has no claim on materials it does not own (pp. 269–274)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.10.1","t11.2.1","t4.2.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to Box 10.10, which statement is TRUE?","options":["Salam is revocable until work starts","Istisna‘a is nonbinding until work starts; Salam is binding once executed","Both require full prepayment","Istisna‘a is only for Mithli goods"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 270."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"Risks in Istisna and mitigation (Box 10.9)","head":["Risk","Mitigation"],"rows":[["Bank does not own the maker’s materials","Security from the manufacturer"],["Subcontractor delay","Shart-e-Jazai price reduction clause"],["No sale before delivery","Promise to purchase from a third party; agency for sale"],["Inferior quality","Quality guarantee from the supplier"]]}}],"questions":[{"id":"q10.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What distinguishes Salam from Bai‘ Mu’ajjal?","options":["Salam involves interest","In Salam the price is paid in advance and delivery deferred; in Mu’ajjal goods are delivered now and price deferred","Both defer the price","Mu’ajjal requires prepayment"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 241.","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Define Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241,242]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.2","type":"multi","prompt":"Which can be the subject of Salam? (Select all.)","options":["500 tons of a defined wheat variety","Tinned consumer goods regularly available","‘This car’","A particular antique","Standardised copper"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,4],"explanation":"pp. 244–245.","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify valid Salam subjects","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.3","type":"tf","prompt":"A bank may use Salam to buy Pakistani Rupees for US Dollars for delivery in three months.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Salam in currencies is invalid (p. 246).","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply currency rule","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.4","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank advances 10 tons of improved wheat seed to a farmer against 25 tons of wheat at harvest. According to Section 10.4.2 this is:","options":["Valid Salam","Not permissible — same species advanced for deferred delivery of the same species","Istisna‘a","Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 247.","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply barter rules in Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.5","type":"match","prompt":"Match each disposal method for Salam goods to its feature.","pairs":[["Parallel Salam","New buyer prepays; contracts independent"],["Third-party promise","No prepayment by buyer; HJ may be taken"],["Agency","Separate agreement; agent sells for bank"],["Sale to original seller","Prohibited — buy-back"]],"explanation":"Section 10.6.","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"section":"10.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare disposal options","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.6","type":"application","prompt":"Box 10.4: the bank paid Rs.100m for wheat and C promised to buy at Rs.115m with Rs.15m Hamish Jiddiyah. If C backs out and the bank sells the wheat for Rs.108m, what does the bank retain from the HJ?","options":["Rs.15m","Rs.7m (actual loss vs promised price), refunding Rs.8m","Nothing","Rs.8m"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Actual loss relative to the promised sale = 115 − 108 = Rs.7m; excess HJ returned. (Practice variant — generated for learning; excludes opportunity cost.)","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.10","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply promise/HJ rules to Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.7","type":"identify","prompt":"A clause reducing the price for delay in delivery, where the reduction benefits the purchaser, is permitted in:","options":["Salam","Istisna‘a (Shart-e-Jazai)","Murabaha","Qard"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 266.","topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify Shart-e-Jazai","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.8","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which is a correct difference between Istisna‘a and Salam (Box 10.10)?","options":["Istisna‘a requires full prepayment","Istisna‘a is nonbinding until work starts, while Salam is binding once executed","Salam is only for manufactured goods","Both send late penalties to charity"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 270.","topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare Istisna‘a and Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[269,274]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.9","type":"scenario","prompt":"A customer supplies steel to a workshop and pays a fixed wage for fabricating gates. This contract is:","options":["Istisna‘a","Ujrah (hire of work)","Salam","Parallel Istisna‘a"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 264.","topicId":"t10.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish Istisna‘a and Ujrah","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","pages":[263,264]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.10","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps of Box 10.16 (government road by Parallel Istisna‘a).","items":["Government contracts Istisna‘a with bank for Rs.1200m over ten years","Bank signs Parallel Istisna‘a with contractor for Rs.1000m","Contractor hands over the road to the bank","Tolls are assigned to the bank; government pays any shortfall"],"explanation":"p. 277.","topicId":"t10.11.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence Parallel Istisna‘a","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10","pages":[269,278]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.11","type":"tf","prompt":"In Istisna‘a, a penalty may be imposed on the purchaser for late payment of instalments, with the amount kept by the bank.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"A penalty against the purchaser for payment default would be Riba (p. 266).","topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply penalty rules","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.12","type":"short","prompt":"Why does the author consider the permissive view on reselling Salam goods before delivery logical, and what caution does he add?","keywords":["weak","price risk","Parallel Salam","possession"],"correctAnswer":"The Hadith used to prohibit it is weak; there is no genuine text prohibiting it; price risk passes to the buyer once the contract is executed; and it supports Parallel Salam and Salam Sukuk. However, to be safe, actual or constructive delivery should not be made before possession, and negotiable Salam certificates are not yet accepted by most scholars.","explanation":"Section 10.6.","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"section":"10.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate views on Salam resale","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.13","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do Islamic banks generally under-use Salam according to Section 10.10?","options":["It is prohibited","It offers no practical advantage over Murabaha: full upfront payment, taking delivery and marketing goods, and default risks","It has no Hadith basis","It is only for governments"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 257.","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.10","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain banks’ use of Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.14","type":"identify","prompt":"Which jurists mainly used the term Salaf, and which used Salam?","options":["Iraqi used Salaf, Hijaz used Salam","Hijaz jurists used Salaf; Baghdad (Iraq) jurists used Salam","Both used only Salam","Neither"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 241.","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall terminology","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241,242]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.15","type":"mcq","prompt":"What did the Prophet (pbuh) require when he regulated the Madinan practice of advance payment for fruit?","options":["Ban it entirely","Known quality, specified measure and weight, price and time of delivery","Only a written contract","Delivery within one month"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 242.","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the Hadith on Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241,242]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.16","type":"multi","prompt":"Which benefits of Salam does the author identify? (Select all.)","options":["Seller gets advance funds","Buyer usually gets a lower price and a hedge","It can stabilise prices during seasonal gluts","It guarantees the buyer a fixed cash return","Lower transaction costs than a swap"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"pp. 242–243.","topicId":"t10.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the economic role of Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.3","pages":[242,243]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.17","type":"scenario","prompt":"A buyer wants a Salam contract for “the wheat of Farmer Ali’s north field”. Is this valid?","options":["Yes","No — Salam cannot be tied to a particular field whose produce may fail","Yes, if prepaid","Only in Syria"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 244.","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply subject-matter rules","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.18","type":"tf","prompt":"Precious gems and antiques are suitable subjects of Salam because they have high value.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Their value depends on subjective assessment (p. 244).","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify unsuitable Salam goods","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.19","type":"comparison","prompt":"Why did classical Fulus (copper coins) differ from modern paper currency for Salam purposes?","options":["Fulus had no value","Fulus had intrinsic metal value and were sub-money; paper money is pure Thaman and subject to gold/silver rules (SAB)","Paper money is a commodity","There is no difference"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 245–246.","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Contrast Fulus and paper money","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.20","type":"identify","prompt":"How long a delay in paying the Salam price did Imam Malik allow?","options":["None","Up to three days","One month","Until delivery"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 247.","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on price payment","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.21","type":"application","prompt":"A bank credits the Salam price to the seller’s current account instead of paying cash. Is this acceptable?","options":["No, it is debt for debt","Yes — the money is at the seller’s disposal; in spirit it is not prohibited debt for debt","Only for small amounts","Only with a guarantee"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 247.","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply payment-mode rules","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.22","type":"tf","prompt":"If no place of delivery is stipulated in a Salam, the place where the contract was executed is the place of delivery.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 248.","topicId":"t10.4.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall delivery rules","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.3","pages":[247,248]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.23","type":"multi","prompt":"Which options does the Salam buyer have? (Select all.)","options":["Option of defect after delivery","Option of specified quality","Option of stipulation (Khiyar al-Shart)","Option of seeing (Khiyar al-Ro’yat)"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"p. 248.","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall options in Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.24","type":"scenario","prompt":"At delivery the market price of the Salam goods has risen sharply. The seller wants to cancel. Can he?","options":["Yes, unilaterally","No — Salam is binding; it may be rescinded only with mutual consent, returning exactly the price paid","Yes, by paying the market difference","Only if the bank profits"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 248–249.","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the binding nature of Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.25","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to AAOIFI’s Salam Standard, a late-delivery penalty in Salam:","options":["Is the bank’s income","May not be stipulated as the bank’s income; a self-imposed charity undertaking is possible","Must equal the market loss","Is always forbidden in any form"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 249.","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall penalty rules in Salam","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.26","type":"tf","prompt":"A surety for a Salam seller may revoke the Salam contract on his own.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Only the seller may revoke (p. 250).","topicId":"t10.5","chapter":10,"section":"10.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall surety rules","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.5","pages":[249,250]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.27","type":"comparison","prompt":"What is the advantage of a promise to purchase over Parallel Salam for disposing of Salam goods?","options":["It is cheaper to document","The promisor need not prepay the price","It transfers risk earlier","There is no difference"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 252.","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"section":"10.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare disposal options","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.28","type":"identify","prompt":"Which scholars held that Salam goods may be resold before possession (to the seller at the same or lower price only)?","options":["Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn al-Qayyim","Ibn Abideen and Kasani","Shafi‘i and Hanafi majority","Ibn Hazm"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"Also Ibn Abbas, Imam Ahmad and Malikis, except foodstuff (p. 250).","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"section":"10.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on resale of Salam goods","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.29","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps in Box 10.4 (wheat Salam with a promise).","items":["Farmer contracts to sell 5,000 tons for Rs.100m","Bank pays Rs.100m on the spot","C promises to buy at Rs.115m with Rs.15m Hamish Jiddiyah","Farmer delivers on 1 May","C executes the purchase and takes delivery"],"explanation":"p. 260.","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence a Salam case","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.30","type":"multi","prompt":"What options does a Salam buyer have if the seller fails to deliver? (Select all.)","options":["Wait until the goods are available","Cancel and recover the price paid","Agree a replacement subject to the rules","Claim the price plus market gain in cash"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 253.","topicId":"t10.7","chapter":10,"section":"10.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall remedies on non-delivery","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7","pages":[252,254]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.31","type":"application","prompt":"The seller can deliver only 60% of the Salam wheat. What may the bank do for the remaining 40%?","options":["Charge interest on it","Accept the 60% and revise the order, recover the proportionate price, or use the other remedies","Reject the whole contract and claim damages above the price","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 253–254.","topicId":"t10.7","chapter":10,"section":"10.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply partial-delivery rules","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7","pages":[252,254]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.32","type":"identify","prompt":"Which country issued Salam Sukuk based on aluminium, with Bahrain Islamic Bank representing participants?","options":["Malaysia","Bahrain","Pakistan","Sudan"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 255.","topicId":"t10.8","chapter":10,"section":"10.8","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Salam Sukuk practice","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[254,255]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.33","type":"tf","prompt":"A Salam contract may be settled in cash instead of goods if both parties prefer, without rescission.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Salam involves no cash settlement; physical delivery is a must, and on rescission only the price is refunded (p. 255).","topicId":"t10.9","chapter":10,"section":"10.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Recall settlement rules","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.9","pages":[255,256]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.34","type":"comparison","prompt":"According to Box 10.2, which is a difference between Salam and Murabaha?","options":["Both require prepayment","Salam goods are specified by description and delivered later; Murabaha is executed on particular existing goods delivered on the spot","Murabaha cannot be deferred","Salam is used for currencies"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 257.","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Contrast Salam and Murabaha","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.35","type":"scenario","prompt":"In Box 10.7, sugar bought by Salam at Rs.19/kg is sold by the mill as agent at Rs.18/kg despite its efforts. Who bears the loss?","options":["The mill","The bank, as owner of the sugar","Shared equally","The buyer"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 261.","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply risk in Salam agency","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.36","type":"multi","prompt":"Which risks does the bank face in Salam, per Section 10.10.1? (Select all.)","options":["Counterparty risk","Commodity price risk","Asset-holding risk","Interest-rate risk on the price"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 258.","topicId":"t10.10.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.10.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Salam risks","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10.1","pages":[258,259]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.37","type":"identify","prompt":"On what juristic basis was Istisna legalised as a financing mode?","options":["Darurah","Istihsan","Qiyas on Murabaha","‘Urf only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 263.","topicId":"t10.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the basis of Istisna","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","pages":[263,264]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.38","type":"tf","prompt":"Istisna is valid for natural products such as corn or fruit.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Only for manufactured or constructed items (p. 264).","topicId":"t10.11.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Istisna subject matter","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.2","pages":[264,265]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.39","type":"application","prompt":"A government offers a builder the right to collect tolls on a new road for 15 years as the price of construction. Which contract feature does this illustrate?","options":["Salam capital in goods","Istisna price in the form of usufruct (BOT)","Hawalah","Murabaha rebate"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 265.","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply usufruct as Istisna price","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.40","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can Istisna not be priced on a cost-plus Murabaha basis?","options":["Cost-plus is Riba","Murabaha requires an existing, owned asset with known cost, which Istisna lacks","Istisna has no profit","AAOIFI bans all mark-ups"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 265.","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Istisna pricing","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.41","type":"identify","prompt":"By analogy with which classical rule did scholars allow Shart-e-Jazai in Istisna?","options":["A tailor’s wage of 12 dirhams in two days and 10 in a week","The six commodities Hadith","The boat parable","Umar’s market ruling"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 266.","topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the basis of Shart-e-Jazai","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.42","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Istisna contract is signed but the manufacturer has not yet begun work. The buyer wants to cancel. Can he?","options":["No, never","Yes — before work starts either party may cancel by notice","Only with a penalty","Only if the price falls"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 266.","topicId":"t10.11.5","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the binding point of Istisna","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","pages":[266,267]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.44","type":"application","prompt":"The bank’s subcontractor gives it a 5% discount in Parallel Istisna. Must the bank pass it on to the purchaser?","options":["Yes","No — cost savings and losses belong to the bank","Half of it","Only if requested"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 268.","topicId":"t10.11.9","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply cost rules in Istisna","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.9","pages":[268,269]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.45","type":"match","prompt":"Match each feature to Istisna or Ujrah (Box 10.10).","pairs":[["Manufacturer uses his own materials","Istisna"],["Buyer supplies the materials; maker paid a wage","Ujrah"],["Risk passes to the buyer on delivery","Istisna"],["Only non-consumable assets","Ijarah"]],"explanation":"p. 271.","topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Istisna and Ujrah","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[269,274]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.46","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps of Box 10.14 (Istisna pre-shipment export finance).","items":["Client receives an export order","Bank signs Istisna for the garments at Rs.100m","Bank appoints client as export agent","Bank takes delivery; garments at bank’s risk","Client exports as agent; bank receives Rs.110m under the L/C"],"explanation":"pp. 275–276.","topicId":"t10.11.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence an Istisna export case","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10","pages":[269,278]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q10.47","type":"tf","prompt":"In Parallel Istisna, the bank’s contract with the customer and its contract with the subcontractor may be made conditional on each other.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Each contract is independent; rights and obligations of one cannot depend on the other, and Parallel Istisna is allowed only with a third party (p. 267).","topicId":"t10.11.7","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the independence rule in Parallel Istisna","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.7","pages":[267,268]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch10-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which two contracts are recognized exceptions to the rule that a sold commodity must exist and be in the seller's possession?","explanation":"Salam and Istisna'a are the two exceptions, permitted because each carries specific conditions that remove Gharar despite the commodity not existing at contract time.","topicId":"t10.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.1","topicLabel":"Forward Sales as an Exception to the General Conditions of Sale","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why Salam and Istisna'a are treated as exceptions to the general Shari'ah conditions of a valid sale.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.1","pages":[241]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Musharakah and Mudarabah","Salam and Istisna'a","Murabaha and Musawamah","Ijarah and Wakalah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-2","type":"tf","prompt":"In Bai' Mu'ajjal, goods are delivered immediately and the price is paid at a future date - the opposite of Salam's structure.","explanation":"True - Salam requires the price to be paid in advance with goods delivered later; Bai' Mu'ajjal is the reverse (goods now, price later).","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.2","topicLabel":"Bai' Salam/Salaf: Origins and Prophetic Permission","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch10-3","type":"short","prompt":"How does the Shari'ah rule against reselling Salam goods before delivery help stabilize prices?","explanation":"This reflects the price-stabilization argument in section 10.3.","topicId":"t10.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.3","topicLabel":"Benefits and Economic Role of Salam","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why classical jurists saw Salam as a protective, restricted sale.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.3","pages":[243]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because Salam goods generally cannot be resold before actual transfer, speculative resale/trading of the not-yet-delivered goods is curbed, which protects prices from exposure to speculative rises and helps stabilize them at a moderate level during periods of seasonal demand fall - contrary to interest-financed production/inventory, where costs are further inflated by speculative transactions."},{"id":"q-ch10-4","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are valid conditions for Salam subject matter, per the chapter?","explanation":"Salam requires fungible, precisely determinable goods, generally available in the market by delivery time. A specifically identified item ('this car') and subjectively-valued items (antiques) are explicitly disallowed.","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","topicLabel":"Features of a Valid Salam Contract: Eligible Subject Matter","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,245]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The commodity must be fungible (Mithli) and precisely determinable in quality and quantity","The commodity must be generally available in the market at least at the agreed delivery time","The commodity can be a specifically identified item, such as 'this particular car'","The commodity's value can depend on subjective assessment, such as an antique"],"correctAnswer":[0,1]},{"id":"q-ch10-5","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank wants to structure a Salam contract where it pays in Saudi Riyals and the seller delivers US Dollars at a future date. Is this a valid Salam contract?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states that if the Salam principal is one currency and the 'commodity' sold is another currency, it is a currency exchange transaction requiring simultaneous payment under Bai' al Sarf rules - not valid as Salam.","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","topicLabel":"Features of a Valid Salam Contract: Eligible Subject Matter","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain why Salam in currencies is generally disallowed.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[245,246]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since both are legal tender","No - this is a currency exchange transaction subject to Bai' al Sarf, which requires simultaneous payment, conflicting with Salam's deferred delivery","Yes, provided the delivery date is fixed","Yes, if a third-party guarantee is obtained"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't a seller's outstanding debt to the buyer be used as Salam capital?","explanation":"Using an outstanding debt as Salam capital is an exchange of obligation for obligation (debt for debt), which is forbidden, and undermines the genuine-capital requirement underlying Salam's validity.","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","topicLabel":"Salam Capital","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Debts are never valuable enough","It would constitute a debt-for-debt exchange, which is prohibited, and Salam's validity depends on genuine advanced capital","Only gold and silver can serve as Salam capital","The seller must always pay in physical cash"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which Khiyar (option) right IS available to a Salam buyer, and only after what event?","explanation":"Khiyar al-Shart and Khiyar al Ro'yat are disallowed in Salam, but Khiyar al 'Aib (option of defect) remains available after delivery if the goods are defective or non-conforming.","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","topicLabel":"Delivery Terms, Option Restrictions, and Revocation Rules in Salam","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Khiyar al-Shart, available at contract signing","Khiyar al Ro'yat, available before delivery","Khiyar al 'Aib, available after delivery, for defective or nonconforming goods","No Khiyar right is ever available in Salam"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch10-8","type":"tf","prompt":"A Salam seller can unilaterally rescind the contract simply because the market price of the goods has risen above the Salam price.","explanation":"False - price movement alone never justifies unilateral rescission by either party; the sole recognized exception is genuine unavailability of the goods at delivery time (and only if the buyer refuses to extend to the next supply season).","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.5","topicLabel":"Delivery Terms, Option Restrictions, and Revocation Rules in Salam","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.5","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch10-9","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per AAOIFI's Salam Standard clause 5/7, what happens to a late-delivery penalty collected from a defaulting Salam seller?","explanation":"AAOIFI's clause 5/7 disallows the penalty becoming part of the bank's income; it exists purely as a deterrent, with proceeds directed to charity.","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.6","topicLabel":"Penalties, Security, Pledges and Sureties in Salam","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.6","pages":[249]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It becomes part of the bank's (seller's) income","It cannot become part of the bank's income - it is directed for charitable purposes","It is returned to the seller in full","It is used to pay bank employee bonuses"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-10","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank wants to sell Salam goods it purchased back to the same original seller before taking delivery, via a separate legal entity that the original seller happens to own. Is this valid Parallel Salam?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states that even if the purchaser in the second contract is a separate legal entity but owned by the seller in the first contract, this does not amount to a valid Parallel Salam agreement - it is prohibited buy-back.","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"section":"10.6.1","topicLabel":"Disposing of Salam Goods: Parallel Salam and Marketing Alternatives","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Walk through how a bank uses Salam as a financing technique, including Parallel Salam.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6.1","pages":[251]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since it is technically a separate legal entity","No - this is prohibited Bai' al 'Inah, since the purchaser in the second contract is effectively owned by the original seller","Yes, provided the price is higher than the original","Yes, if the delivery dates differ"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which three marketing alternatives does the chapter give for disposing of goods purchased under Salam?","explanation":"The three alternatives given are: entering a Parallel Salam contract, an agency contract with a third party or the customer (seller), or sale in the open market by the bank itself.","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"section":"10.6.1","topicLabel":"Disposing of Salam Goods: Parallel Salam and Marketing Alternatives","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through how a bank uses Salam as a financing technique.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6.1","pages":[251]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Parallel Salam, an agency contract, and direct sale in the open market","Only Murabaha resale","Only holding the goods indefinitely","Musharakah, Mudarabah, and Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch10-12","type":"short","prompt":"If a Salam seller fails to deliver, what three options does the buyer have, per AAOIFI clause 5/8?","explanation":"This matches the three options given in section 10.7.2.","topicId":"t10.7","chapter":10,"section":"10.7.2","topicLabel":"Salam Post-Execution Scenarios","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through how a bank uses Salam as a financing technique.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7.2","pages":[253]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The buyer may: (1) wait until the commodity becomes available, (2) cancel the contract and recover the price paid, or (3) agree to a mutually consented replacement subject to the relevant Shari'ah rules."},{"id":"q-ch10-13","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Salam seller cannot supply the agreed wheat because it is genuinely out of stock in the accessible market. What substitute rules apply if the bank agrees to accept a different commodity?","explanation":"The substitute commodity must be fungible, of a different genus from the original Salam commodity, and its value must not exceed the value of the original commodity - e.g. wheat could be substituted by cotton, not by corn (same general category) or animals.","topicId":"t10.7","chapter":10,"section":"10.7.3","topicLabel":"Salam Post-Execution Scenarios","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Walk through how a bank uses Salam as a financing technique.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7.3","pages":[253,254]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The substitute must be fungible, of the SAME genus as wheat, and can be of any value","The substitute must be fungible, of a DIFFERENT genus than wheat, and its value must not exceed the original commodity's value","Any substitute is acceptable regardless of genus or value","Substitution is never allowed under any circumstances"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-14","type":"tf","prompt":"Salam certificate (Sukuk) holders are guaranteed a fixed return regardless of the underlying commodity's actual market performance.","explanation":"False - Salam certificate income is tied to the specific underlying commodity/project's outcome and is not guaranteed, though it can be quasi-fixed; investors bear counterparty and market risk.","topicId":"t10.8","chapter":10,"section":"10.8","topicLabel":"Salam-Based Securitization: Salam Certificates/Sukuk","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how Salam-based securitization works.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[254,255]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch10-15","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why have Islamic banks historically underused Bai' Salam as a financing mode, per the chapter?","explanation":"The chapter states Salam has no practical advantage over Murabaha-Mu'ajjal, requires full cash payment upfront, and involves banks in taking delivery, storing, and marketing physical goods - a purely practical/operational reason, not a Shari'ah one.","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.10","topicLabel":"Salam as a Bank Financing Technique","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through how a bank uses Salam as a financing technique, including risks involved.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[257]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Salam is considered Shari'ah-noncompliant by most scholars","Because it offers no practical mark-up advantage over Murabaha-Mu'ajjal while requiring full upfront cash payment and actual physical handling of future goods","Because Salam can only be used for real estate","Because central banks prohibit its use entirely"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each Box 10.2 comparison to the correct mode: (1) Goods delivered on the spot, price spot or deferred; (2) Price paid fully in advance, goods delivered later.","explanation":"Murabaha: goods delivered on the spot, price spot or deferred. Salam: price paid fully in advance, goods delivered later - the structural opposite.","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.10","topicLabel":"Salam as a Bank Financing Technique","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Salam from Murabaha.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[257]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-Murabaha, 2-Salam","1-Salam, 2-Murabaha","1-Istisna'a, 2-Salam","1-Murabaha, 2-Istisna'a"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch10-17","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are risks specifically identified for banks using Salam financing?","explanation":"The chapter lists counterparty risk, commodity price risk, delivery/settlement risk, quality risk, asset-holding risk, asset-replacement risk, and Parallel Salam fiduciary risk. Interest rate risk on bonds is not applicable, since Islamic banks do not hold conventional interest-bearing bonds.","topicId":"t10.10.1","chapter":10,"section":"10.10.1","topicLabel":"Salam as a Bank Financing Technique","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the risks involved in Salam financing.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10.1","pages":[258]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Counterparty risk","Asset-holding risk (storage and Takaful costs)","Fiduciary risk in case of Parallel Salam","Interest rate risk on conventional bonds"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2]},{"id":"q-ch10-18","type":"mcq","prompt":"When is the sale in an Istisna'a contract considered executed?","explanation":"Unlike MPO (which requires a fresh offer and acceptance when the bank takes possession), the Istisna'a sale is executed at the time the contract is signed - no renewed exchange is needed once the item is prepared.","topicId":"t10.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","topicLabel":"Istisna'a: Definition, Concept, and Eligible Subject Matter","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Define Istisna'a and distinguish it from Salam and from Ijarah/Ujrah.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","pages":[263]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only when the manufactured item is delivered and a fresh offer and acceptance occur","At the time the Istisna'a contract itself is signed, with no need to renew offer and acceptance later","Only after a formal promise stage separate from the sale","Istisna'a never involves a sale, only a lease"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-19","type":"scenario","prompt":"A customer supplies fabric to a tailor and asks the tailor to sew it into clothing for an agreed wage. What contract type is this?","explanation":"Because the buyer (customer) supplies the material and the manufacturer (tailor) contributes only labour/skill for an agreed wage, this is a contract of Ujrah, not Istisna'a.","topicId":"t10.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","topicLabel":"Istisna'a: Definition, Concept, and Eligible Subject Matter","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Define Istisna'a and distinguish it from Ijarah/Ujrah.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","pages":[264]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Istisna'a","Ujrah","Salam","Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-20","type":"tf","prompt":"It is permissible for an Islamic bank to conclude an Istisna'a contract for a particular, already-designated car sitting in a specific factory.","explanation":"False - Istisna'a's subject matter must be identified by specification, not by designation; it is invalid for the subject to be an existing, specifically identified asset.","topicId":"t10.11.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.2","topicLabel":"Istisna'a: Definition, Concept, and Eligible Subject Matter","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Define Istisna'a and distinguish it from Salam.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.2","pages":[264]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch10-21","type":"tf","prompt":"Unlike Salam, full advance payment of the price is not required in Istisna'a.","explanation":"True - Istisna'a's price can be paid in instalments linked to completion stages, unlike Salam's requirement of full advance payment.","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","topicLabel":"Price, Penalty Clause, and Binding Nature of Istisna'a","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain Istisna'a's price rules.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch10-22","type":"scenario","prompt":"A contractor delivers a building three months late (not due to force majeure). The Istisna'a contract includes a Shart-e-Jazai clause reducing the price for delay. Where does this price reduction benefit go?","explanation":"Unlike Salam's charity-bound penalty, the Shart-e-Jazai price reduction in Istisna'a directly enhances the purchaser's own income, since the manufacturer's commitment/focus (not a fixed debt claim) determines timely completion.","topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","topicLabel":"Price, Penalty Clause, and Binding Nature of Istisna'a","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the Shart-e-Jazai penalty clause and its distinction from Salam's penalty.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["To charity, exactly like Salam's late-delivery penalty","Directly to the purchaser's own income, since Istisna'a's timely completion depends on the manufacturer's own labour and commitment","It is split 50:50 between the bank and charity","It becomes government revenue"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-23","type":"mcq","prompt":"Is a penalty clause against the PURCHASER for payment default permitted in Istisna'a?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states it is not permitted to stipulate a penalty clause against the purchaser for default in payment, because this would be Riba.","topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","topicLabel":"Price, Penalty Clause, and Binding Nature of Istisna'a","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Istisna'a's penalty clause rules.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, symmetrically with the seller's late-delivery penalty","No - this would constitute Riba","Yes, but only if approved by a court","Yes, if the amount is nominal"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-24","type":"mcq","prompt":"When does an Istisna'a contract become binding on both parties?","explanation":"Istisna'a is nonbinding as long as the manufacturer has not started work; either party may cancel before that point. Once work starts, the contract becomes binding.","topicId":"t10.11.5","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","topicLabel":"Price, Penalty Clause, and Binding Nature of Istisna'a","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the binding nature of Istisna'a.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","pages":[266]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Immediately upon signing, like Salam","Only once the manufacturer has started work on the subject matter","It never becomes binding","Only after full payment is received"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-25","type":"short","prompt":"Explain how a bank can use Parallel Istisna'a to act as both seller and buyer, and what condition governs the delivery dates of the two contracts.","explanation":"This reflects the Parallel Istisna'a structure described in section 10.11.7.","topicId":"t10.11.7","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.7","topicLabel":"Guarantees, Parallel Istisna'a, Agency, and Post-Execution Rules","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Describe how Parallel Istisna'a and agency arrangements let banks act as both manufacturer/seller and purchaser.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.7","pages":[267]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The bank enters an Istisna'a contract as SELLER with a customer, and a separate, independent Parallel Istisna'a contract as BUYER with a genuine third-party subcontractor to actually produce the item. The two contracts must remain fully independent (never tied together), and the delivery date of the Parallel Istisna'a contract must not precede the delivery date of the original Istisna'a contract."},{"id":"q-ch10-26","type":"tf","prompt":"If a bank's actual cost of production under Istisna'a is lower than the amount it forecast, it must pass the savings on to the purchaser as a discount.","explanation":"False - the bank is not obliged to give a discount; any additional profit (or, symmetrically, loss if actual costs exceed forecast) belongs to the bank.","topicId":"t10.11.9","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.9","topicLabel":"Guarantees, Parallel Istisna'a, Agency, and Post-Execution Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe post-execution rules in Istisna'a.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.9","pages":[268]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch10-27","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each Istisna'a-vs-Salam distinction correctly: (1) Advance payment requirement; (2) Goods type eligible; (3) Binding nature before work/execution.","explanation":"Istisna'a does not require advance payment (Salam does); Istisna'a covers both Qimi and fungible goods (Salam only Mithli); Istisna'a is nonbinding until work starts, while Salam is binding once executed.","topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","topicLabel":"The Potential of Istisna'a and its Risk Management, Compared with Salam and Ijarah","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish Istisna'a from Salam.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[270,271]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-Istisna'a: not required, Salam: mandatory; 2-Istisna'a: Qimi+fungible, Salam: Mithli only; 3-Istisna'a: nonbinding until work starts, Salam: binding once executed","1-Istisna'a: mandatory, Salam: not required; 2-Istisna'a: Mithli only, Salam: Qimi+fungible; 3-Istisna'a: binding immediately, Salam: nonbinding","Both require full advance payment and are identical in all respects","1-Istisna'a: not required, Salam: mandatory; 2-both identical; 3-both binding immediately"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch10-28","type":"mcq","prompt":"In Ijarah, what happens to rental payment obligations if the leased asset becomes unusable through no fault of the lessee?","explanation":"The chapter contrasts this with Istisna'a: in Ijarah, asset risk stays with the owner/lessor, and rental is owed only if the asset is capable of being used per normal market practice.","topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","topicLabel":"The Potential of Istisna'a and its Risk Management, Compared with Salam and Ijarah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Istisna'a from Ijarah/Ujrah.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[271]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The lessee must still pay full rental regardless","Asset risk remains with the lessor, and the lessee owes rental only if the asset remains usable per normal market practice","The lessee automatically becomes the owner","Rental doubles as compensation"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-29","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sectors does the chapter identify as having strong potential for Istisna'a financing?","explanation":"The chapter lists construction industry financing, residential/commercial development and housing finance, and high-technology industries (aircraft, locomotive, shipbuilding) as potential areas for Istisna'a.","topicId":"t10.11.10","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10","topicLabel":"The Potential of Istisna'a and its Risk Management, Compared with Salam and Ijarah","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe Istisna'a's financing potential.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10","pages":[269]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only retail consumer goods","Construction (apartments, hospitals, schools), housing finance, and high-technology industries (aircraft, ships, locomotives)","Only foreign exchange trading","Only agriculture"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-30","type":"short","prompt":"What can a bank use 'Arbun for in an Istisna'a contract, and what happens to it in each outcome?","explanation":"This reflects the 'Arbun rules described in section 10.11.6.","topicId":"t10.11.5","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.6","topicLabel":"Guarantees, Parallel Istisna'a, Agency, and Post-Execution Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain guarantee mechanisms in Istisna'a.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.6","pages":[267]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"'Arbun is a security deposit the bank can give or demand as manufacturer or purchaser. If the contract is completed, it becomes part of the price. If the contract is rescinded, it is forfeited - though it is preferable that the forfeited amount be limited to the actual damage suffered."},{"id":"q-ch10-31","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per S.M. Hasanuz Zaman's account, why might Bai' Salam have been preferable to an interest-based loan for farmers historically?","explanation":"From the farmers' viewpoint, Bai' Salam did not make an increase in cost as interest did, and it saved them from the hardships and risk involved in marketing their produce.","topicId":"t10.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.3","topicLabel":"Benefits and Economic Role of Salam","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the economic role and benefits of Salam.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.3","pages":[243]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Salam required no advance planning","Salam did not increase the farmer's cost the way interest did, and it saved farmers the hardships and risk of marketing their produce","Salam guaranteed the farmer a higher sale price than the market","Salam allowed the farmer to keep the crop"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-32","type":"scenario","prompt":"Instead of paying hard cash, an Islamic bank credits the agreed Salam price to the seller's account with the bank, where it remains at the seller's disposal. Does this satisfy Salam's advance-payment requirement?","explanation":"Cash payment is not necessary in Salam; the price can be credited to the seller's account or paid via a cashable pay order. In letter this resembles debt for debt, but not in spirit, since the money remains at the seller's disposal.","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","topicLabel":"Salam Capital","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"State the conditions of a valid Salam contract.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[247]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No - only physical cash satisfies Salam","Yes - crediting the account is sufficient; in spirit this is not a prohibited debt-for-debt exchange since the money is at the seller's disposal","No - this always constitutes debt for debt, which is prohibited","Yes, but only if the seller is a corporate entity"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-33","type":"tf","prompt":"Unlike Salam, the price in Istisna'a can be paid in cash, tangible goods, or usufruct, and may be linked to construction/completion stages rather than paid fully in advance.","explanation":"True - Istisna'a's price can take the form of cash, tangible goods, or usufruct of identified assets, and can be paid in instalments linked to completion stages, unlike Salam's requirement of full advance cash payment.","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","topicLabel":"Price, Penalty Clause, and Binding Nature of Istisna'a","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain Istisna'a's price rules.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch10-34","type":"mcq","prompt":"In an Istisna'a arrangement, a bank appoints the client/manufacturer as its agent to sell the finished asset. What condition must this agency arrangement satisfy?","explanation":"The agency agreement for supervising manufacturing or selling the asset should be separate and independent from the Istisna'a agreement.","topicId":"t10.11.7","chapter":10,"section":"10.11.8","topicLabel":"Guarantees, Parallel Istisna'a, Agency, and Post-Execution Rules","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain agency arrangements in Istisna'a.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.8","pages":[267]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It can be merged into the Istisna'a agreement for simplicity","It must be separate and independent from the Istisna'a agreement","It is not permitted under any circumstances","It can only be used by the bank as manufacturer, never as buyer"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch10-35","type":"short","prompt":"Describe how the Bahrain government's aluminium Salam Sukuk structure works, per the chapter.","explanation":"This reflects the Bahrain aluminium Salam Sukuk example given in section 10.8.","topicId":"t10.8","chapter":10,"section":"10.8","topicLabel":"Salam-Based Securitization: Salam Certificates/Sukuk","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how Salam-based securitization works.","source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[255]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Aluminium is designated as the underlying asset. The Bahrain government sells aluminium forward under a Salam arrangement. Bahrain Islamic Bank (BIB) purchases the aluminium, acting as representative for other banks wishing to participate in the transaction. In exchange for the advance payment, the Bahrain government issues Salam certificates and undertakes to supply a specified amount of aluminium on a specified future date."}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f10.1","front":"Bai‘ Salam","back":"Forward sale: full price paid at contract for specified fungible goods to be delivered at a fixed future time and place.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241,242]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.2","front":"Hadith conditions for Salam","back":"Known quality, specified measure and weight, along with the price and time of delivery.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241,242]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.3","front":"What cannot be the subject of Salam?","back":"Money/currencies, gold, silver; identical exchange (wheat for wheat); specific items (‘this car’, ‘this field’); land, buildings, trees; subjectively valued items (gems, antiques).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.4","front":"Why is Salam in currencies invalid?","back":"Currency exchange requires simultaneous payment of both sides (Sarf), while Salam defers delivery; allowing it would open a floodgate of Riba.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.5","front":"Salam capital (price) rules","back":"Money (or goods/usufruct); paid in full at contract (≤2–3 days stipulated delay allowed); cannot be an existing debt; account credit acceptable.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.6","front":"Options in Salam","back":"No Khiyar al-Shart or Khiyar al-Ro’yat; after delivery, options of defect and specified quality (refund of price only).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.7","front":"Substituting Salam goods after the due date","back":"By mutual consent; different genus; fungible; market value not above the original; not stipulated in the contract.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.8","front":"Parallel Salam rules","back":"Independent of the original; same specs allowed; delivery date not earlier than original; never with the original seller or entities he owns.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.9","front":"Promise vs Parallel Salam for disposing Salam goods","back":"A third-party promise (with HJ) does not require the buyer to prepay; Parallel Salam requires full prepayment by the new buyer.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.10","front":"Seven risks in bank Salam","back":"Counterparty, commodity price, delivery/settlement, quality, asset holding, asset replacement, fiduciary (Parallel Salam).","category":"Risk","topicId":"t10.10.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10.1","pages":[258,259]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.11","front":"Salam vs Murabaha (Box 10.2)","back":"Salam: deferred delivery, full prepayment, by specification. Murabaha: spot delivery, spot/deferred price, particular commodity.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.12","front":"Istisna‘a","back":"Order to manufacture/construct an item to specification for future delivery at an agreed price; manufacturer provides material and labour; legalised by Istihsan.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t10.11","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","pages":[263,264]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.13","front":"Istisna‘a price payment","back":"Need not be prepaid; instalments linked to stages allowed; can be cash, goods or usufruct (BOT); cannot be cost-plus Murabaha pricing.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.14","front":"Shart-e-Jazai in Istisna‘a","back":"Clause reducing the price for late delivery (not force majeure); the reduction benefits the purchaser — by analogy with the tailor’s wage in Ijarah.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.15","front":"When is Istisna‘a binding?","back":"Nonbinding until the manufacturer starts work; binding thereafter (OIC, majority).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.11.5","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","pages":[266,267]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.16","front":"Istisna‘a vs Salam (Box 10.10)","back":"Manufactured vs any goods; no full prepayment vs full; Qimi vs Mithli; price cut to buyer vs penalty to charity; revocable until work vs binding.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[269,274]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.17","front":"Istisna‘a vs Ujrah","back":"Istisna‘a: own materials, fixed price, risk passes on delivery. Ujrah: buyer’s materials, wages; (Ijarah) risk stays with owner.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[269,274]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.18","front":"Salam Sukuk (Bahrain example)","back":"Government sells aluminium forward; Bahrain Islamic Bank buys for participating banks; certificates issued; actual delivery binding.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t10.8","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[254,255]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.19","front":"Salam vs Bai‘ Mu’ajjal","back":"Salam: price now, goods later. Mu’ajjal: goods now, price later.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241,242]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.20","front":"Economic role of Salam (Hasanuz Zaman)","back":"Large advances to farmers for consumption and fixed investment; buyers were traders; direct grower–trader link; stabilises prices.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t10.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.3","pages":[242,243]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.21","front":"Can the Salam price be an existing debt?","back":"No — using the seller’s or a third party’s debt as the price is debt for debt.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.22","front":"Salam barter restriction","back":"The same species cannot be advanced for deferred delivery (e.g. 10 tons of wheat seed for 25 tons of wheat); a tractor for cotton is fine.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.23","front":"Salam delivery rules","back":"Known date and place (place of contract if silent); instalments allowed; goods at seller’s risk until delivery.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.3","pages":[247,248]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.24","front":"Rescinding Salam","back":"Only by mutual consent; buyer gets back exactly the price paid.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.25","front":"Who may revoke a Salam backed by a surety?","back":"Only the seller; revocation releases the surety and the pledge.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.5","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.5","pages":[249,250]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.26","front":"Salam goods sold back to the seller","back":"Not allowed (buy-back) — even to a company owned by the seller; use agency or a third-party promise/Parallel Salam.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.27","front":"Negotiability of Salam certificates","back":"Not yet accepted by the majority of contemporary scholars — they represent a debt of goods.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t10.8","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[254,255]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.28","front":"Box 10.7 — agent sells Salam sugar","back":"Target price Rs.20; above that the excess can be the agent’s fee; below cost, the bank bears the loss as owner.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[256,263]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.29","front":"Istisna: when binding?","back":"Non-binding until the manufacturer starts work; binding thereafter (OIC Fiqh Council, majority).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.11.5","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","pages":[266,267]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.30","front":"Istisna on an identified asset","back":"Not allowed; an already-made asset can be supplied only if the contract specifies features, not a particular unit.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.11.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.2","pages":[264,265]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f10.31","front":"Istisna price forms","back":"Cash, goods or usufruct (BOT); may be in instalments linked to stages; not cost-plus.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-1","front":"What are the two exceptions to the rule that a sold commodity must exist and be in the seller's possession?","back":"Salam and Istisna'a - both 'forward sales' where delivery is deferred to the future, permitted because each carries specific conditions that neutralize Gharar.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.1","pages":[241]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-2","front":"What is Bai' Salam (Salaf)?","back":"An ancient forward-sale contract in which the price is paid fully in advance at contract formation, for prescribed goods (specified quality, quantity, date) to be delivered later.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-3","front":"How does Salam differ structurally from Bai' Mu'ajjal?","back":"Salam: price paid now, goods delivered later. Bai' Mu'ajjal: goods delivered now, price paid later. They are structural opposites.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[241]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-4","front":"What did the Prophet (pbuh) require of advance-paid fruit sales in Madinah, and why is this significant for Salam?","back":"He ordained that whoever pays in advance must specify known quality, specified measure and weight, along with the price and time of delivery - this Hadith is the foundational basis for Salam's unanimous permissibility.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.2","pages":[242]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-5","front":"What does 'Mithli' mean, and why does it matter for Salam?","back":"Fungible - goods whose units don't significantly differ from each other. Only Mithli goods can be the subject of Salam (unlike Istisna'a, which can also cover unique/Qimi goods).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-6","front":"Why is Salam in currencies generally disallowed?","back":"A currency-for-currency Salam is in substance a currency exchange (Bai' al Sarf), which requires simultaneous payment of both values - directly conflicting with Salam's deferred-delivery structure.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[245,246]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-7","front":"Does the Salam seller need to personally own or produce the goods being sold?","back":"No - per Hadith (Companions confirming they never asked Syrian peasant sellers if they owned standing crops), the seller only needs to be able to supply the specified goods from the market by the delivery date.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,245]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-8","front":"Can an existing debt owed to the seller be used as Salam capital?","back":"No - using an outstanding debt/loan as Salam capital would be a prohibited debt-for-debt exchange; genuine fresh capital must be advanced at contract formation.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[246,247]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-9","front":"How much delay in Salam price payment do Imam Malik and contemporary jurists allow?","back":"Up to about two to three days, provided it is stipulated between the parties and occurs before the delivery period begins.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[247]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-10","front":"Which two Khiyar (option) rights are disallowed in Salam, and which one remains available after delivery?","back":"Disallowed: Khiyar al-Shart (rescission option) and Khiyar al Ro'yat (option of seeing). Available after delivery: Khiyar al 'Aib (option of defect), for genuinely defective/nonconforming goods.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.4","pages":[248]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-11","front":"What is the one recognized unilateral exception allowing a Salam contract to be rescinded?","back":"Genuine unavailability/inaccessibility of the goods in the market at delivery time - and only if the bank/buyer refuses to extend the delivery period to the next supply season.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.5","pages":[248,249]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-12","front":"Where must late-delivery penalty proceeds go in Salam, per AAOIFI clause 5/7?","back":"To charity - such penalties cannot become part of the bank's (seller's) income; they exist purely as a deterrent against wilful default.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.6","pages":[249]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-13","front":"What Hadith establishes the permissibility of security/pledge (and, per Ibn Hajar, surety) in Salam?","back":"The Hadith of the Prophet (pbuh) borrowing grain from a Jew against the pledge of his iron breastplate.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.5","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.5","pages":[249]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-14","front":"Can Parallel Salam ever be entered into with the original Salam seller?","back":"No - this constitutes prohibited Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back), even if done through a separate legal entity owned by that same seller.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6.1","pages":[251]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-15","front":"What is Parallel Salam?","back":"A second, independent Salam contract in which the bank (having bought goods under an original Salam contract) acts as seller, selling similar goods on similar specifications - never tied to the first contract and never with the original seller.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6.1","pages":[252]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-16","front":"If a Salam seller defaults and the bank opts for a cash refund instead of replacement goods, how much can it recover?","back":"Only the exact original advance amount - no increase or decrease - since the advance is treated as a fixed debt claim on the seller.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.7","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7.2","pages":[253]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-17","front":"What risks do Salam certificate (Sukuk) investors bear, and why is their return not guaranteed?","back":"Counterparty risk (seller's possible inability to deliver) and market risk (unfavorable resale conditions); returns are tied to the specific underlying commodity/project's actual outcome, so they are not guaranteed, though they can be quasi-fixed.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t10.8","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[254,255]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-18","front":"Why have Islamic banks historically underused Salam relative to Murabaha?","back":"Salam offers no mark-up advantage over Murabaha-Mu'ajjal while requiring full upfront cash payment and genuine physical handling of future goods (receipt, quality check, storage, marketing) - operational, not Shari'ah, reasons.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[257]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-19","front":"Give the core structural difference between Salam and Murabaha (Box 10.2).","back":"Salam: goods delivered later, price paid fully in advance, commodity defined by specification. Murabaha: goods delivered on the spot, price may be spot or deferred, executed on a particular identified commodity.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.10","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.10","pages":[257]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-20","front":"What is Istisna'a, and on what legal basis is it permitted?","back":"A sale contract ordering manufacture/construction of an item for future delivery per agreed specifications, with the sale executed at contract signing; permitted on the basis of Istihsan (juristic preference for public interest).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t10.11","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","pages":[263]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-21","front":"What distinguishes Istisna'a from Ujrah?","back":"In Istisna'a, the manufacturer supplies both material and labour for a fixed sale price. In Ujrah, the buyer supplies the material and the manufacturer is paid a wage for labour/skill only.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.11","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.1","pages":[264]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-22","front":"Is advance payment required in Istisna'a, unlike Salam?","back":"No - unlike Salam, full advance payment is not required; price can be paid in instalments linked to construction/completion stages.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-23","front":"How does the Shart-e-Jazai penalty in Istisna'a differ from Salam's late-delivery penalty?","back":"Shart-e-Jazai (Istisna'a) reduces the price for late delivery and directly benefits the purchaser's income. Salam's late-delivery penalty must go to charity, leaving the purchaser's own P&L unaffected.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-24","front":"When does an Istisna'a contract become binding?","back":"It is nonbinding as long as the manufacturer has not started work (either party may cancel by notice); once work has started, the contract becomes binding and cannot be unilaterally cancelled by the buyer.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.11.5","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","pages":[266]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-25","front":"What is Parallel Istisna'a?","back":"A second, independent Istisna'a contract where a bank (buyer in this second contract) subcontracts manufacture to a genuine third party, to fulfill its obligations as seller under a first Istisna'a contract with a customer.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t10.11.7","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.7","pages":[267]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-26","front":"If a bank's actual Istisna'a production cost is less than forecast, must it pass the savings to the customer?","back":"No - the bank is not obliged to give a discount to the purchaser; any additional profit (or, symmetrically, loss) belongs to the bank.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.11.9","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.9","pages":[268]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-27","front":"How does risk transfer differ between Istisna'a and Ijarah?","back":"In Istisna'a, asset risk transfers to the purchaser upon delivery, and the price must be paid regardless of what later happens to the asset. In Ijarah, asset risk remains with the lessor throughout, and rental is owed only if the asset remains usable per normal market practice.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[271]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-28","front":"How does Salam's prohibition on reselling goods before delivery help stabilize commodity prices, per the chapter?","back":"Since Salam goods generally cannot be resold before actual transfer, speculative resale of not-yet-delivered goods is curbed, protecting prices from speculative rises and helping stabilize them at a moderate level during periods of seasonal demand fall - unlike interest-financed inventory building, whose costs are further inflated by speculative transactions.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t10.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.3","pages":[243]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-29","front":"Must a bank pay hard cash to satisfy Salam's advance-payment requirement?","back":"No - crediting the agreed amount to the seller's account (or issuing a cashable pay order) is sufficient. In letter this resembles a debt for a debt, but in spirit it is not, because the money is placed at the seller's disposal.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.4.2","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.2","pages":[247]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-30","front":"If a Salam bank sells a defaulting seller's pledge and recovers cash instead of goods, how much can it keep?","back":"No more than the price it paid in advance - the advance price is treated as a debt outstanding on the seller, so recovery is capped at that amount, with any balance returned to the seller.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.5","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.5","pages":[249]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-31","front":"In what forms can the price be paid in an Istisna'a contract, and what does 'usufruct as price' commonly enable?","back":"Cash, tangible goods, or usufruct of identified assets. Usufruct-as-price is relevant to 'build, operate and transfer' (BOT) arrangements, where a government offers usufruct of the asset being built for an agreed period as consideration.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-32","front":"Can the price in an Istisna'a contract ever be changed after it is settled?","back":"It cannot be unilaterally increased or decreased, but it CAN be readjusted by mutual consent - e.g. due to material modifications to the item being manufactured, or due to unforeseen contingencies or changes in input prices.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t10.11.3","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.3","pages":[265]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch10-33","front":"Give a real-world example of Salam-based securitization (Salam Sukuk) described in the chapter.","back":"Bahrain designated aluminium as the underlying asset: the Bahrain government sells aluminium forward, Bahrain Islamic Bank purchases it (representing other participating banks), and the government issues Salam certificates undertaking to supply a specified amount of aluminium on a specified future date.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t10.8","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.8","pages":[255]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e10.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain the conditions of a valid Salam contract and discuss how Islamic banks can use Salam for agricultural and export finance while managing its risks.","expectedStructure":["Definition and Hadith basis","Subject matter conditions (incl. currencies)","Price (Salam capital)","Delivery, options, revocation, penalty","Disposal: Parallel Salam, promise, agency","Applications: Box 10.4, 10.5, 10.7","Risks and Box 10.3 mitigations"],"keyConcepts":["Mithli","prepayment","Parallel Salam","Hamish Jiddiyah"],"essentialPoints":["No sale back to seller","Penalty to charity"],"commonMistakes":["Allowing currency Salam","Allowing debt set-off as price"],"topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4.1","pages":[244,246]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e10.2","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between Salam, Istisna‘a and Ujrah.","expectedStructure":["Subject","Payment","Nature of goods","Penalty treatment","Binding nature","Material and risk"],"keyConcepts":["Shart-e-Jazai","Istihsan"],"essentialPoints":["Box 10.10"],"commonMistakes":["Requiring Istisna‘a prepayment"],"topicId":"t10.11.11","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.11","pages":[269,274]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e10.3","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A government wants a highway built and paid over ten years. Design an Istisna‘a-based structure for an Islamic bank and identify the Shari’ah conditions.","expectedStructure":["Istisna‘a with government","Parallel Istisna‘a with contractor","Independence and delivery dates","Supervision agency","Payments/tolls assignment","Risks and mitigations"],"keyConcepts":["Parallel Istisna‘a","BOT"],"essentialPoints":["Bank bears risk until delivery","Shart-e-Jazai with contractor"],"commonMistakes":["Tying the two contracts together"],"topicId":"t10.11.10","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.10","pages":[269,278]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e10.4","kind":"short","prompt":"What options does a Salam buyer have if the seller fails to deliver or supplies inferior goods?","expectedStructure":["Failure: wait, cancel/refund, replacement","Using pledge to buy goods","Inferior goods: refuse, discount, substitute, partial","Substitution conditions"],"keyConcepts":["refund of exact price"],"essentialPoints":["Different genus, value ≤ original"],"commonMistakes":["Claiming more than the advance in cash"],"topicId":"t10.7","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.7","pages":[252,254]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e10.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"Why can’t a late-delivery penalty in Salam be the bank’s income, while in Istisna‘a a price reduction can benefit the buyer?","expectedStructure":["Salam advance = debt","Istisna‘a depends on manufacturer’s effort"],"keyConcepts":["Shart-e-Jazai"],"essentialPoints":["Tailor analogy"],"commonMistakes":["Treating both the same"],"topicId":"t10.11.4","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.4","pages":[266,266]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e10.6","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain how an Islamic bank can use Salam for agricultural and export finance and how it disposes of the goods. Illustrate with a case.","expectedStructure":["Salam conditions recap","Disposal options: Parallel Salam, promise, agency, own sale","Prohibition of selling back to the seller","Box 10.4 or 10.5 walk-through","Risks and Box 10.3 mitigations","Post-execution scenarios"],"keyConcepts":["Parallel Salam","Hamish Jiddiyah","agency","counterparty risk","buy-back"],"essentialPoints":["Price paid in full upfront","Contracts independent","Bank bears risk after delivery"],"commonMistakes":["Selling to the Salam seller","Linking Parallel Salam to the first contract","Cash settlement instead of delivery"],"topicId":"t10.6","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.6","pages":[250,252]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-7","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the features that make a Salam contract valid.","expectedStructure":["State the full-advance-payment requirement","Explain fungibility/specification requirements for the subject matter","State delivery date/place certainty","Note the prohibitions (no currency, no specific non-fungible item, no partial deferral of price)"],"keyConcepts":["Salam","Salam capital","Fungible (Mithli) goods"],"essentialPoints":["The price (Salam capital) must be paid in full, on the spot, at the time of contract; any deferral turns it into a prohibited sale of debt for debt.","The subject matter must be fungible, and its quality, quantity, and grade must be specified clearly enough to avoid future disputes.","The delivery date and place must be fixed in the contract.","Salam cannot be used for money/currencies or for a specific, non-fungible item such as a particular car or a named plot of land."],"commonMistakes":["Allowing partial payment 'now, balance later' — any deferral of the Salam price invalidates the contract.","Forgetting that instalment delivery of the goods (as opposed to the price) is actually permissible."],"topicId":"t10.4.1","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.4","pages":[243]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-8","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the binding nature of an Istisna'a contract and how this differs from Salam.","expectedStructure":["State the Istisna'a rule (nonbinding until work starts)","Contrast with Salam (binding from execution)","Explain the practical implication for Parallel Istisna'a"],"keyConcepts":["Istisna'a","Binding contract","Parallel Istisna'a"],"essentialPoints":["Istisna'a is nonbinding as long as the manufacturer has not started work; either party may cancel it by notice before that.","Once work has started, the contract becomes binding on both parties and cannot be cancelled unilaterally by the buyer.","By contrast, Salam is conclusive and binding immediately upon execution and can only be altered or revoked by mutual consent.","In a Parallel Istisna'a, the two legs (bank as buyer in one, seller in the other) must remain independent contracts, each governed by its own binding-nature timeline."],"commonMistakes":["Assuming Istisna'a is never binding — contemporary Shari'ah scholars and several legal frameworks treat it as binding once conditions (like work starting) are met.","Conflating the two legs of a Parallel Istisna'a as a single contract."],"topicId":"t10.11.5","chapter":10,"source":{"chapter":10,"section":"10.11.5","pages":[266]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
